      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>FESTIVAL OF THE HOME.</head>
        <p>THE FESTIVAL OF THE HOME.</p>
        <p>Second Christmas Of The War Approaches.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>OLD ASSOCIATIONS.</head>
        <p>OLD ASSOCIATIONS.</p>
        <p>To speak of Christmas and " Peace on eorth, good will toward men" may seem out of place this year, but if we cannot have peace on earth. at least we can have "Peace toward men of good will." This must be the spirit of the Christmas season in 1915. Forgetting the war for, a day or two we will, say with a writer of old,</p>
        <p>"But is old, old, good old Christmas gone? Nothing but the hair of his good, grey, old head and beard left? Well, I will have that, seeing that I cannot have more of him."</p>
        <p>There is something about Christmas, more than any other festival that appeals to the imagination of Englishmen. We , like to gather round the ,fireside, crack jokes and join in hearty choruses. This festival, with its message of religion and love has been made the festival of the home. It is the season for the gathering of families—and where, alas, this cannot be, it is the season when the thoughts of those who cannet be with us ,are specially in our minds. So at Christmas we have two joys— the joy of conviviality and the more sacred joy of relationship.</p>
        <p>from the, bonfires rose to the skies. The trees on which the mistletoe had lived were some- what exhausted and short of sap. Therefore being the driest, they burned best and created the biggest flames. It was the belief of the ancient worshippers that Thor had caused the mistletoe to grow on the tree for his own pleasure, and in time, mistletoe became the sign of friendship. The Scandinavians took it home and hung it over the door, and it was the custom, that even if an enemy passed beneath that mistletoe he became a friend. From that it became she. usage to greet people who came under the mistletoe with a kiss in token of friendship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CHARM OF CHRISTMAS.</head>
        <p>THE CHARM OF CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>In the words of an American writer,</p>
        <p>"There is a tone of soleTn and sacred feel- ing that blends with our conviviality, and lifts the spirit to -a state of hallowed and elevated enjoyment. The services of the Church at Christmas are extremely tender and inspiring. They dwell on the beautiful story of the origin of our faith, and the pastoral scenes that accompanied its announcerrkent. They gradually increase in fervour and pathos during the season of Advent, until they break forth in full jubilee on the morning that brought peace and good will to men. I do not know of a grander effect of music on the moral feelings, than to hear the full choir and the pealing organ performing a Christmas anthem ,in a cathedral, and filling every part of the vast pile with triumphant harmony." The very season of the year tends to make Christmas more homely and more charming. At other times, at Easter, Whitsuntide and at Midsummer, we go to the woods, the fields and the brooks for our enjoyment. There, "the I song of the lOrd, the murmur of the stream, the I breathing fragrance of the spring, the soft ! voluptuousness of summer, the golden pomp of autumn; earth with its mantle of refreshing green, and heaven with its deep, delicious blue ' and its cloudy magnificence all fill us with mute ! but exquisite delight, and we revel in the luxury of mere sensation. But in the depth of winter, I when Nature lies despoiled of every charm, and wrapped in her shroud of sheeted snow, we turn for our gratifications to moral sources. ,The dreariness and desolation of the landscape, the short gloomy days and darksome nights, while they circumscribe our wanderings, shut in our 'feelings also from rambling ?broad and make I us more keenly disposed for the pleasure of the social circle. Our thoughts are more concen- trated; our friendly sympathies more aroused. I We feel more sensibly the charm of each other's society, and are brought more closely together !by dependence on each other for enjoyment. Heart calleth unto' heart, and we draw our pleasures from the deep wells of loving kindness, which lie in the quiet recesses of our bosoms; land which, when resorted to, furnish forth the pure element of domestic felicity."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>MISTLETOE THE SYMBOL CF FRIENDSHIP.</head>
        <p>MISTLETOE THE SYMBOL CF FRIENDSHIP.</p>
        <p>In this time of war we, the people of England, can more fully realise something of the joys of the fireside. With those who reflect what is happening close to our own shores, the heart fairly jumps for joy, when weary as we may be by the extra demands of this period of "war work," one enters a room filled with .the glow and warmth of the evening fire. , Here at the Christmas season one is sure of meeting with a kindly welcome. "Where," to quote another writer, "does the honest face of hospitality, ex- pand into a broader and more cordial smile— where is the shy glance of love more sweetly eloquent—than by the winter fireside ?" With Christmas are associated many things. One has but to hear the word carols, or mistletoe, or Santa Claus, and immediately the thoughts of that happy festive season flies into the mind, but it is little known how these things came to be associated together. So per- haps a few words of explanation would not be out of place.</p>
        <p>It is safe to say that most people know that kissing under the mistletoe is permissible. It is an'uld custom, and for its origin we _go back to the time of our Scandinavian forefathers. They built big " Jule " or " Yule " fires as a method of honouring the god Thor, who was supposed to be well pleased when the flames</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CHILDREN'S SAINT.</head>
        <p>THE CHILDREN'S SAINT.</p>
        <p>"Santa Claus" is the contracted form of "St. Nicholas." Last Monday was St. Nicholas's Day, the real fete-day of Santa Claus. Nicholas is one of the most benevolent and popular saints in the calendar, and the patron af children, prospective brides, scholars, sailors and parish clerks, as well as patron saint of Russia. His traditional liberality led to the Justom of hanging up the children's stockings .ea his feast day that he might place a gift ;herein. We have transferred this practice to jhristmas Eve, but the boys and girls of Holland are faithful to the older custom, and hang up their stockings on St. Nicholas's Day. St. Nicholas is indirectly associated, too, with the poor man's bank—the pawnshop. The saint was famous for his generosity, and in art he is often represented as 'bearing three purses, or three gold balls. It is highly probable, then, that the pawnbroker owes the distinctive emblem of his trade to this fact. True, the emblem was directly derived from the Lombard merchants who settled in England at an early period, and were the first to open money-lending establishments. The three golden balls were also the signs of the Medici family of Florence, who amassed a vast fortune in business. But the same origin is traceable in both cases— "the emblematic device of the charitable St. Nicholas."</p>
        <p>The,song of the Angels at the birth of Christ was the first Christmas carol, and ever since that time sacred songs to the Saviour have been in constant use. The custom of singing at mid- night on Christmas Eve dates from that time, and is practised in many parts with unabated fervour all over the world. The Reformed Church introduced a new sort of carol to the old Latin hymns which had formerly been in vogue, and many modern carols of a high order succeeded. To-day there is a happy blending of old and new, and there is no more profitable way of spending some portion f our Christmas time than in listening in our beauti- ful churches to these tributes of praisa to the Saviour's birth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>CHRISTMAS TREE.</head>
        <p>THE CHRISTMAS TREE.</p>
        <p>The Christmas tree is, so far as this country is concerned, a comparatively modern innova- tion in our Christmas festivities. It was brought into greater practice after the mar- riage of the late Queen Victoria to the Prince of Saxe-Coburg-Gotha, and the country will never regret that the late Queen helped to make it popular among her people. Coleridge spent a Christmas in Germany eipout a hundred years ago, and he sent home to a friend a description of a scene that he witnessed at a German family Christmas tree. Parents and children saved up for months before the time for the purpose of buying Christmas presents. The gifts for the parents were laid out under a huge branch of yew at the end of the, room, lit up with tiny ,efuy4es and vrernted withQut inwi. pretence of secrecy. But they contrived all sorts of means of keeping from their brothers and sisters all knowledge of the gifts they were to receive until the' delightful moment of pre- sentation arrived. Nowadays the Christmas tree with its load of good and pretty things, is the most attractive feature in the children's celebration of the anniversary at every juvenile gathering, and its despoilment a work of real joy-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Speeds, British and American.</head>
        <p>Speeds, British and American.</p>
        <p>An Englishman, boasting of the superiority of the horses in his country, mentioned that the celebrated Eclipse had run a mile in a minute. "My good fellow," exclaimed an American pre- sent, "that is rather less than the average rate of our common roadsters. I live at my country seat, near Philadelphia, and when I ride in a hurry to town, of a morning, my own shadow can't keep up with me, but generally comes into the store to find me, from a minute to a. minute and a half after my arrival. One morn- ing the beast was restless, and I rode him as fast as I possibly could several times around a large factory—just to take the old Harry out of him. Well, sir, he went so fast that the whole time I saw my back directly before me, and was twice in danger of riding over myself!" •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>FLANNELETTE I ADERCLOTHINOI SPECIAL VALUE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
