        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>rattrs to tilt Oita</head>
        <p>rattrs to tilt Oita</p>
        <p>DRINK RESTRICTIONS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Since " Citizen's " last letter there has been a spontaneous outburst of working-class opposition to the policy of restriction so strong that even your correspondent cannot fail to see it. The Control Board has had to bend to the storm and make very important modifications in the London regulations. " Citizen " thinks he has scored a very important point about, the "five per cent. minority, but if he had fetid my letter carefully he would have noticed that I never expressed my acceptance, or otherwise, of this figure, which I merely quoted from Mr. Ben Tillett's speech. The point I wished to make was Mr. Tillett's protest against the inajerity being needlessly penalised for the fault of a few,' and it was impossible to quote this from his speech without giving his "five per cent." estimate. "Citizen ", could quite easily see that this figure was not important to my argument; and, in any case, it,probably refers to Mr. Tillett's own Union, and not to munition workers or labour generally. It is not often that I find any point of agree- ment with "Citizen," but I cordially support his contention that the small minority of drink- slackers are traitors and deserving of the severest punishment. I have never said one word to condone the conduct of any men who can be proved to be regularly neglecting their vitally important wprk through excessive drink- ing during this time of national crisis. My whole case is that such men are so few that they can be dealt with individually, and by the way they are being dealt with to-day through the Munition Courts. To• restrict and badger the whole population as the Control Board is doing is needless, foolish and dangerous. At the pre- sent time it is treason to divide, on a petty issue like this, a nation united to fight the com- mon foe.</p>
        <p>J. E. SIMPSON.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your correspondent, "Mr. Simpson, of Liverpool," has very rapidly reached that stage in the controversy when, finding he has no case, he has no recourse but to "abuse the other side." It is no concern of mine that there are Temperance Societies such as he refers to, con- nected with which are officials who receive salaries, and this, fact in no degree affects my statement that the present agitation against the Control Board's action is spurious and fosterei entirely in the interests of the liquor trade, and financed by them, and that it in no way reflects the opinion either of the working men or any other class in our country, the vast majority oi whom willingly accept the drinking restrictions with the utmost loyalty. It may, however, be pointed out to Mr. Simpson that the only reward which the men and women who are striving to make our country the home of a sober nation, is the fact that their consciences are clear of all responsibility, and their only interests are the well-being in their fellows. The vital difference in this respect between temperance workers and the apologist for the liquor trade is so obvious that it needs no emphasis, for the fact remains that the trade.:is out for ',money all the time and for nothing else, and to judge by ,results it appears as if it. cared neither for politics nor patriotism so long as its " coffers " are well lined. The liquor trade has received a nasty blow in its efforts to drag Trade Unionism into the pre- sent quarrel. Mr. Robert Williams, of the National Transport Workers. Union, has this week denounced as "the most infamous libel upon Trade Unionism" the attempt to in- fluence a "strike against the new drink re- striction order," and further and equally or even more important is .his statement that no -com- plaint has reached us at the Federation Head- quarters from any of the large seaport areas where Orders have been introduced similar to that proposed in London." But to come back to the local question. Can any impartial man who reflects upon the figures furnished by your correspondent, Mr. Griffiths, in your last issue as to Saturday and Sunday night drinking—especially by women—feel comfortable in his mind as 'to the alarming facts disclosed, and the need for further restriction? It is hardly necessary to repeat, but it is an In- assailable fact that there is great hindrance z nd Jose Of time in rf,g3nl to 1111111itin, production in Warrington, and the overwhelming eatw, ia drink. What is good enough for "Cleveland" is good -enough for Warrington, and it is high time that action was taken by all to whom both the reputation of the town and the safety of the country are of pressing importance. The con- test in Cleveland Division would be highly farcical if it occurred at any other time but now, when the Government, representing as it does all parties in the country, requires the undivided and loyal support of all who desire to see victory for our cause. When every penny needlessly spent is a wicked offence against the interests of the ' State, when the evidence of the good results which have followed the operations of the Control Board are so At-lolls and overwhelming; then it does appear to me that whoever is responsible for the present un- timely contest deserves condign and sr ift punishment.</p>
        <p>One word more, and it is this—the Control Board is beyond the reach of the clamours of poisoned tongues, is unassailable from the point of view of vested interests, and its decisions ar*.• prompted solely by a, def_rmination to do that which is best calculatffto ensure the greatest and best output of munitions. I feel very hope- ful of seeing V(Tarrinoiton included in the Order which may shortly be issued, and if it is the results will be gratifying to every lover of his town and country. CITIZEN.</p>
      </div>
