      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>XF_</head>
        <p>XF_</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 11, 1915.</p>
        <p>Y A</p>
        <p>etuu TMIAS SI4OE PING</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>carEs LTD</p>
        <p>' The Brave Show They Will Make With Seasonable Goods.</p>
        <p>POWER TO PURCHASE.</p>
        <p>Our advertisement columns to-day, no less than the shop windows in town, demOnstrate in marked degree the fact that our tradesmen are intent upon making Chrikmas as happy as it is in their power to make it. Fortunately, the state of trade makes it possible for the working classes to welcome the show of the Shop rather than to envy others the power of participating in pleasures not open to themselves. There will, of course, be due regard to economy, but there are ample means of enjoying the coming .festival, rationally and fully, and without that excess or indulgence that means waste and extravagance. If our readers desire to spend their money to the best advantage—which is the real kind of thrift—they have a great range of reputable tradesmen placing themselves at their disposal through announcements in to-day's "Examiner." Next week we shall go more fully into the question of shops and shopping in view of .the festival which will then be just a week ahead.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS CARDS.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Raphael Tuck have again come for-</p>
        <p>ward in great force with their Christmas cards and seasonable publications of a kindred• type. Last week we _reproduced the picture which adorns the King's Christmas card this year, and it reflects fairly well the manner in which the firm has endeavoured to serve His Majesty's sub- jects. Nothing more beautiful could be imagined than these goods, individually or col- lectively, nothing could more completely show the world how serenely we expect to rise superior to the conditions to which Germany s, for the moment, drawn us. We shall still go on wishing each other "A Itappy Christmas" in Tuck's best style—and hope to be enjoying the happiness of peace before another Christ- mas comes round.</p>
        <p>WHEN CHRISTMAS WAS UNDER BAN.</p>
        <p>The General Court of Massachusetts Bay Colony, following the example of the English Parliament; in 1659 enacted a law that " any- body who is found observing, by abstinence from labour, feasting, or any other way any such day as Christmas, shall pay for every such offence five shillings." This law was repealed in 1681.</p>
      </div>
