      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, DECEMBER 11, 1915,</p>
        <p>THE SINGLE MAN'S CONSCIENCE.</p>
        <p>In a few hours the time in which the single Man who is of military age and otherwise free to serve his country will be able to satisfy his conscience will have passed. In these few hours he must make his choice for all time—whether he. will be up and doing and ranging himself along with other soldiers of the King and so earning his self- respect, or whether he will commit himself to the perpetual self-reproach that he shirked his duty when the supreme need of his native land called him to its defence. There; has been a great rush to the recruiting stations this week, arid, in Warrington alone the equivalent of several battalions has been enrolled. But there is ,a suspi- cion that the single men have not come forward in the propor- tion in which they ought to have done. Whether this is so or not, we have no means of definitely knowing"; but assuming that it is so, we would say to those of them who , are hanging back to shelter themselves behipd:, their brothers, single. and married: "Remember that conscience which will last as long as life." What shall it benefit a man if he gets through this crisis easily and safely. if,. for all time, he lose the immortal part of him- self ? It was that dread of " something" after death that prevented Hamlet from slaying himself ; that " hereafter'" in the life of an Englishman who has slunk from the path of'duty may well make the veriest slacker to re-consider his ways before it is too late.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>SINGLE MAN'S DANGER.</head>
        <p>THE SINGLE MAN'S DANGER.</p>
        <p>/Nor is the matter of conscience the only thing that will trouble the future of the present-day shirker. If the hints that have issued from high places mean anything, they mean that if the single men do not enlist volun- tarily they will be 'forced to enlist before the married men</p>
        <p>who have offered themselves are called upon to make good their patriotic.p*romise. It May sound strange to use the words volun- tarily and forced enlistment in such close connection, but, in reality, there is nothing strange about it. The voluntary system, of which we are all so proud, the system that has been, and is, the glory of this free and unfettered land, guarantees us against perpetual military ser- vitude. It does not mean that in time of peril one section of the community shall be free to leave an,other- section to fight it§ battles 'while they stand aloof because they do not see fit to volunteer. It is therefore pro- bable, to use no stronger a term, that, if need be, a partial system of Conscription will be set up by which the single men who have lagged will be gathered up and forced to serve.. If we do reach such a pass, there will be no one to raise a voice against this class of partial Conscription, But maybe we -are anticipating `6on- ditions that will never arise. We hope it will prove to be so, and that the young unmarried men who are available will have attested before the fatal hour has passed to-night. As a matter of fact, the general response has been splendid—a response which will asSuredly hearten our Allies and serve as a death- knell to all the hopes of our enemies.</p>
      </div>
