      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>BELGIANS IN BELGIUM.</head>
        <p>BELGIANS IN BELGIUM.</p>
        <p>Organised Effort _ For Residents Of</p>
        <p>Desolated Country.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—His Worship the Mayor, at the request of the National Committee for the Relief of the Belgians in Belgium, has consented to a Christmas collection being made. 'Warrington has done its part in providing means of support for a number of Belgians who were able to reach our hospitable shores, but this will be the first organised effort made in the town to help those who were obliged to remain in their desolated country. The Germans, after bring- ing ruin to the industries by which these sup- ported themselves, have ignored the obligation to sustain the life of those who lived and toiled in the land so despoiled. If neutral nations,. more .particularly America, had not provided relief „many must have starved. -There are, now about three million of the inhabitants who are wholly, or par- tially, dependent on outside support.. While the Germans have refused to help by supplies,. we have the 'assurance of those engaged in the relief work that they have not impeded, but have given assistance to, the work of distribu- tion. No supplies have been appropriated, and all that has been sent has found its way to those in need. We have also reliable evidence that the system of relief is well organised, worked on economical lines, and the army of willing workers are almost entirely voluntary. • Christmas Day Collection.</p>
        <p>With the approval of the Mayor, the Christian Gkurches, of all denominations, have been accorded the honour Of • service, and have undertaken the Work of collection. To each 'Church has been allocated a portion of the town. It is intended ttEdistribute envelopes to all houses a day or two before Christmas, and every family is invited to put their gifts in the envelope on Christmas Day; say, at the dinner hour. It, is hoped that .all will participate in this offering upon the natal day of Him who is •the Prince of Peace, and the Prince of Love. The widow's mite will be as acceptable as .the gift of those who are able to give more. The pennies and the half-pennies may help to keep in life little -children who may come to birth on this coining Christmas ,Day. • Responsible authorised ,collectors will call ,for the envelopes after 'Christmas Day, and. these will be brought by some appointed •Church officer to the Town Hall, to be counted, ' and .the; total amount, and, this only, will be; announced. It; is hoped that. the districts out- side the borough, but adjoining, will partici- pate in this effort. His Worship the Mayor has -already; expressed his willingness to 'accept 'larger sums from anyone Who will send direct.' to him. These will ;be duly acknowledged, and we trust that this - method will be largely used by sym-</p>
        <p>pathisers who prefer tq pajb cheque direci the Mayor.</p>
        <p>For the Committee, FREDERIC W. WILLIS, Chairman. CHARLES E. PARKER, ALFRED TILLING,</p>
        <p>eC:retaries.</p>
        <p>, . . . l'ound D. .. . . .. . . The 'usual monthly Poun Day ii-il s. held at Raddon Court ted Cross Hospital ..bn Wednes- day. • .. Many 'useful •artieles were receiVed,-for which the committee are very grateful, and the latter wiA to thank the many kind donors. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 11, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 11, 1915.</p>
        <p>RECRUITING BOOM!</p>
        <p>Great Rush Of Men To Enlist During The Week.</p>
        <p>THE LAST DAY.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Recruiting Authorities have been. overwhelmed with recruits during the past week—the last for . recruiting under Lord Derby's scheme. This fact, and the knowledge of the fairness and justice of the scheme, has given the men of Warrington a great stimulus to 'attest. A rush was expected by the authori- ties on Monday morning, and., accordingly the Baths were opened as a 'recruiting. office, in addition to the Police Station and the Drill Hall. But even this -extra aocommodation was insufficient; and for 'hours men were waiting their turn to be attested. About 600 men were attested on Monday, and each day 'since the average has been over 500.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SCHOOLS AS RECRUITING OFFICES.</head>
        <p>SCHOOLS AS RECRUITING OFFICES.</p>
        <p>On Thursday and Friday the rush was so great that the authorities decided to open eight ele- mentary "schools as extra recruiting" offices. They are :.Bolton Boys', Beamont Boys', Silver 7 street, Hamilton-street, Evelyn-street, St. Bar- nabas' School, National School and St. ,James' School. Magistrates were in Attendance at each place from 11 a.m. until 10 p.m., and at each school men were attested, and everything done with the exception of the medical examination. These arrangements will stand good for to-day, the intention being to give every man a chance of being attested, and thereby to come under the scheme. . . _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>. . Warrington has becn specialty thanked by the authorities in London for the way that they have made their returns and kePt. everything up to date. At the time of writing (Friday night) there are still a few More cards to be taken out by canvassers, but these will have .been dealt with before to-night (Saturday). It is especially de- sirable that the canvassers should return their ,cards by to-night, so that the report Can be sent to London_ not later than Sunday.- • .The canvassers and a host of other ladies and :gentlemen have worked diligently to bring ,about the success of the scheme, and amongst ,these must be included • the name of Councillor •John WilkinsOn, -who for several days .has been 'busily engaged recruiting at the works of Messrs. Pearson and Knowles,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ARRANGEMENTS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>ARRANGEMENTS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>In addition to the schools and offices named above, the offices of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict .Council, " in BewseY-Street, Will be also open to-day both for men from the rural dis- trict area and also from the borough. Mr. W. Furness will be present for the purposes of attestation. . -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>POLICEMEN JOIN.</head>
        <p>POLICEMEN JOIN.</p>
        <p>At a; special meeting of the Warrington Watch Committee on Friday it was decided to give permission to all policemen of military age to attest under Lord Derby's scheme. A .meeting of the Chief Constables of Lancashire was held on Thursday, at which Lord Derby was present. An undertaking .was given that no police officer will be 'taken from a district with- out the consent of the Watch Committee. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WESLEYAN CIRCUIT.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN CIRCUIT.</p>
        <p>Quarterly Meeting Favours Restriction Of Drinking Facilities.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting, of the Warrington Wesleyan Circuit was held at Bold-street on Thursday. The Rev. W. T. Derry presided, and announced that the membership stood at 1,103, and that there were 36 on trial and '142 junior 'members: A resolution was unanimously passed urging the Liquor Traffic Central Control Board to make Warrington a scheduled area not only in the interests of thrift and the homes of the people, the heads of which were, in so many cases, away on active .service, and that War- rington is an important military centre, but also .because of. the loss, of time and deficiency in the munition works. , ,</p>
        <p>The circuit stewards, Mr. J. G. Withinshaw and Mr. F. Hickman, presented a favourable financial statement, showing that the income during the quarter was more than the expendi- ture.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. T. Derry reported that the War Expenses collection amounted to ,R 32 10s. Bd. The application of the Lymm Church for per- mission .to employ a supernumerary minister was referred to a smaller committee. The circuit stewards were thanked for their services, and unanimously re-appointed. Mr. F. A. Harris was elected secretary to the meeting in place of Mr. E. Murphy,. Mr Isaac Barlow was re-appointed circuit chapel secretary; the Rev. H. T. Dixon, circuit edu- cation secretary, and Mr. Kenneth Bishop, cir- cuit guild. secretary. The local preachers were heartily thanked for their services during the year, and Mr. S. Moss replied on their behalf. It was resolved to devote a Sunday collection throughout the cir- cuit, as usual, to the Local Preachers' Mutual Aid Association, th&amp;general committee of which meets this year early in June at Liverpool. The 'circuit trustees' meeting was fixed for March 9th at four o'dock at Bewsey-road. The report of the Circuit Sunday School Coun- cil waq-received; and an interim report of the Foreign Missionary Campaign. The work of the Soldiers' Home in Bold-street was brought for- ward by the treasurer, Mr. William Isaac, and the secretary, Mr. A. Daub.</p>
        <p>It was re" Solved to hold the next •quarterly meeting on March 9th at Bewsey-road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>THRIFT CAMPAIGN.</head>
        <p>THRIFT CAMPAIGN.</p>
        <p>Warrington Commitieti</p>
        <p>Await A Government Plan For Saving.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. P. Rylands presided over h meeting of the 'Thrift Campaign Committee this week at the Town Hall to receiN4 reports of work done and to consider future possibilities. Up to the present .the campaign has included meetings indoors and out, two public confereneee at the Town Hall, and. two .crowded gatherings at the Co-operative Hall, addressed by Miss Petty; the distribution and sale of literature; the display. of posters at banks, in works, and on the hoard- ings, with a most useful backing from the local Press. It was stated that over 900 recipe bcoks had been sold, and many new departures had been made in economical housekeeping as a :direct result of the demonstration by the "Pud- ding Lady." •</p>
        <p>If was,. however, decided to suspend further operations until the Government had brought out some well-considered and practicable plan for war saving, and the London Committee was to be informed of this decision. The out-of- pocket expenses of the campaign to date was, only .213 or ~,tl4, and Mr. Felix .Maginn, the hon. treasurer, hoped that some of Warrington's public-spirited men would send in donations. Mr. Wilfrid Rowland, the hon. secretary, an- nounced that he 'had copies of. a twopenny pain; phlet for sale. entitled "War and, SelfAenial," by Mr. Hartley Withers, late financiateditor of the "Morning Post," arid now' a special adviser to the Government on War Finance. It isb•Aa lucid presentation of the case for National Economy.</p>
        <p>Government Request to Tanners. The *revival in trade a4ready neted was con- tinued last week, ,and business was much brisker than it has been for some considerable time. Many of the leading, factors were. early on the ground and bought freely, sales being spread over the full area of production. Towards the end of the week, consequent upon the brisker :buying, prices, became firmer, and, in some cases, have begun to creep upward again. This tallies with the position in the hide market, where a 'sharp recovery has been effected, the -advance in some cases being id. on the previous week. Some comment. is raised by the expressed desire of the Government "that tanners shall continue to work steadily," a pertinent query being,. Is the Government prepared to do any- thing to ensure a better supply of tanning ex- tract, So that tanners may work steadily? A further question is, 'What is to become of stocks, of heavy leather, supposing it :should not be nee. essary to issue large orders?—The Warring- ton* correspondent 'of the "Leather Trades</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>" TOMMIES' " TEA.</head>
        <p>" TOMMIES' " TEA.</p>
        <p>Successful Effort For War</p>
        <p>Hospital Cinema.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MEETING OF TWO V.C.'s.</head>
        <p>MEETING OF TWO V.C.'s.</p>
        <p>The " Tommies' " tea, in which all Warring- ton ha 4 taken the keenest interest, wai held with unbounded success at the Parr Hall, War- rington, on Thursday evening. Throughout the whole six hours—from .3 p.m. to 9 p.m.—during which the "tea" was held, the hall was crowded.</p>
        <p>Though named a " tea," it was really a sale of work on, novel lines, with refreshments pro-. .vided. There were a number of stalls in the hall .and a tea room. One of the most popular . . , stalls was that 'which contained gifts suitable for Christmas presents for soldiers. The stalls were heavily laden with goods of the very best quality, and they had been decorated with patriotic designs; which displayed the artistic instincts of the decorators The number of visi- tors must have run into four figures, and as each visitor either sent or brought a gift, it can 'be easily imagined what a variety of goods there was to puzzle the buyer. The object of the sale--or tea—was to provide funds for the installation of a permanent cine- matograph at. the Lord Derby War Hospital at Winwick, and also to provide a Christmas tree, tobacco; cigarettes and other "comforts" for -the patients. As the Winwick Hospital is one of the largest military hospitals in the country, about' £2 a day is required. to supply each patient with a small packet of cigarettes. , The sale was opened by Lady J. Harmood Banner, in the presence of a large gathering. In the chair was ..Councillor Henry Roberts (chairman of the General Committee), and also on the platform were Mrs. Carruthers Johnstone isister of Sir J. Harmood Banner), Mrs. Henry Greenall and the Mayor of Warrington (Alder- man Peter Peacock). Theattendance also in- cluded Mr. W. Peter Rylands; Mr. John Coxon, the Rev. Canon. Willis '(rectcir o Warrington); the 'Rev. H. V. Pigot, the Rev. James Collins and Alderman Smethui.-:-.t.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>EVERYBODY'S DUTY.</head>
        <p>EVERYBODY'S DUTY.</p>
        <p>Opening the proceedings, Councillor Roberts said he was afraid that the weather was not quite as gooa as they would have liked it to have been; it was something like the King of Greece —rather uncertain and likely to be treacherous. Laugliter.) They could not overcome the weather, but they could do something for their `Own countrymen in their midst, who had done their duty and • suffered ..in performing It. either by wounds or sickness. They. Could not all serve at the Front; but' they could all do !komething to help. It .was not simply a duty or pleasure.. but it was the privilege of every- body to do his best to make the hit of the men who had risked their lives. as comfortable as possible.. He expressed his egret at the absence nf .the Ma,,oress, who was detained at home through sickness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>LADY HARMOOD BANNER'S GOOD WISHES.</head>
        <p>LADY HARMOOD BANNER'S GOOD WISHES.</p>
        <p>Introducing Lady Harmood Banner to the audience, the Mayor said that he felt sure that the very fact that she had come amongst them had been a 'great attraction to the people of Warrington. Her husband played an im- portant part in the borough as the Chairman of the Board of Directors of. Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. He (the Mayor) welcomed her, and hoped that she would often visit them. He felt sure that when such objects as the present were to the fore they would not appeal to her in vain. (Applause.) Declaring the sale opeil, Lady Harmood Banner said that it really was a great pleasure to her to be present. They. ,all knew the' object of the sale—it had been ably put.bY the Chair- man—and as it was a veri, good one, she hoped they would buy well and quickly. At the suggestion of Councillor Roberts, hearty vote of thanks was 'aCoorded the opener, and also to his Worship the,Mavor. As the sale was proceeding a high-class enter- tainment—arranged by, !Mrs, R. Johnson— was gone , through, a .special feature of which was the selections on bagpipes by Piper Laidlaw, the V.C. who is at Winwiek Hosnital The piper played the same tunes that he played when he won the V.C.—" Blue Bonnets over the Border" and the "Standard on 'the Braes o' Mar." and he was loudly cheered.</p>
        <p>- - --- Miss Pamela; Holden sang the songs "The Reason "and "Till the Boys Come Home," and also rendered two whistling, solos. Two songs— " Take a Pair of Sparkling Eves" and "Glorious Death" were sung by 'Lieutenant Dugdale, of the 4th King's Own Loyal Lancaster Regiment.- An interesting musical item NrBS that of the bin. composed of Mr. R. H. Pear4on W. E. Balmford ('cello). and Miss Gertrude Jcnes (piano). who played the two movements of Mendelssohn's "D Minr." Miss Ersnla Hughes gave a. graceful eNhibition in her " Romance ", dance and the " Patriotic " dance of the pumas of Miss Ratcliffe was also very pleasing. Mr. Ernie Hart, of St. Helens, rendered a violin solo, for which he was encored. At intervals selections were played by the band of the 2nd South Lancashire Regt.. under the able conductorship of Mr. Robert Cahill (bandmaster).</p>
        <p>TWO V.C.'s MEET.</p>
        <p>During the evening, on interesting little scene took place at the side of • the stagei Piper T,sidlaw, V.C., was standing alone for a few minutes and another soldier, with his right arm under his coat, apnroached him and offered him I,is left hand. Not knowing who it was, the Piper accepted the offer of a handsbake arrl was e'reativ nleased when 'the second soldier intv•ochiced himself as Corporal Tombs, V.C. Towards the close of the 'sale the remaining goods were sold by auction, and practically everything was cleared out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>PROCEEDS.</head>
        <p>THE PROCEEDS.</p>
        <p>The exact proceds are not .yet to hand, but it is expected that at least £350 will be available for the objects for which the,sale was held.</p>
        <p>That such a result. came about is entirely due to the enthusiasm of a large band of workers The various committees worked-very hard in- deed, and the credit for the success is due to them and to the executive, -Which comprised the Mayor of Warrington (Aldermnn Peter Pea- cock), the president, Councillor Henry Roberts (chairman). Mrs. Vere Davies, Mrs. Rylands, Mrs. Peacock. Mrs. Seeker, Mrs. Densham, Mrs. Henry Greenall, Mr. H. 13. Houghton (hon. secretary), and Mr. -H. J. Westbrook (hon. treasurer). The ladies mentioned above were re- sponsible for the stalls and the tea room, and it was chiefly, through their efforts that such a large quantity of goods was collected.</p>
        <p>The success of the sale was also contributed to by Messrs. Peter Walker -and Son, who fixed the stalls. and Mr. R. Johnson. who was re- sponsible for the decorations, and, a number of other ladies,and gentlemen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>LOCAL SCOTS' CONTRIBUTION.</head>
        <p>LOCAL SCOTS' CONTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>As a result of the speeial appeal at the St. Andrew's Dav celebrations last 4veelc, the sum of 110 10s. will be forwarded to the Lord Derby Hospital Cinema Fund. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>DISCHARGED SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>DISCHARGED SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>Insurance Committee Make Recom- mendations.</p>
        <p>At a, meeting of the Warrington Insurance Committee on Thursday• evening, the Sana- torium Benefit Sub-committee reported that at a recent meeting the Clerk stated that the majority of the tuberculous soldiers who had been granted institutional treatment at Sankey had left the sanatorium without consent, and against the advice of the tuberculosis officer, within a few days after receiving their Army discharge papers and deferred Army pay. He had accordingly written the Commissioners drawing their attention to the matter, and sug- gesting that the Army *uthorities should: (a) Withhold payment of the moneys.due until the period of institutional treatment had expired; or (b), Pay the amount to the Insurance Com- mittee, the money .to be handed, over,to the men at the conclusion of the treatment; or (c) Retain the discharge certificates until the termination of institutional treatment. The 'Clerk's action was approved.;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Surplus' Building Sites.</head>
        <p>Surplus' Building Sites.</p>
        <p>The Town Olerk has been :authorised by the Finance and. Estate Committee to advertise for sale surplus building sites and the letting of vacant premises.</p>
        <p>Paving of Liverpool-road and Mersey.-</p>
        <p>street.</p>
        <p>The Borough Surveyor (Mr. Andrew M. Ker) has received instructions to proceed with the paving of certain portions of Liverpool-road and Mersey-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Increases for Police Officials.</head>
        <p>Increases for Police Officials.</p>
        <p>, The Town Council have Sanctioned, on the recommendation of the Watch Committee, in- creases in the salaries of a number of police officials. The amounts of the increases are either Is. or 2s. each.</p>
        <p>Vacant Land Near Warrington Bridge. The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee having received a request from the Finance and Estate Committee with regard to the laying out of the vacant land abutting on the South-east side of Warrington Bridge, have requested the Borough Surveyor to prepare a plan and submit it to their next meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>Council Appointments..</head>
        <p>Council Appointments..</p>
        <p>Mr. W. E. Clegg hi'At7, been appointed a co- optative member of the Warrington Education Committee, on the recommendation of the War- rington Friendly Societies.</p>
        <p>• Aldermen Bennett and Smethurst have been appointed representative governors of tha governing body of the Grammar School.</p>
        <p>The Rev._ H. B. Firth Recovering.</p>
        <p>Having made a rapid recovery since he—was operated upon for appendicitis three weeks ago at Dr. Fox's Nursing Home, the Rev. H. B. Firth, vicar of St. James' Church, Latchford. was, on Tuesday, removed to his home. He continues to make good progress, and hopes shortly to resume his parochial work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Children's New Year Party.</head>
        <p>Children's New Year Party.</p>
        <p>Arrangements are being made to give a grand New Year party to the children of the town in the John Crosfleld Memorial Hall on January 6th. There will be dancing, games and supper, and a New Year gift will be handed to each child in attendance. Tickets will be a shilling each, and the proceeds will be ddvoted to the soldiers' comforts fund. The Women's Liberal Association are making the arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Health of the Town.</head>
        <p>The Health of the Town.</p>
        <p>During the week ended December 4th the Medical Officer of Health (Dr. G. W. N. Joseph) was notified of seven cases of scarlet fever,, four of tuberculous phthisis, two of enteric fever, two of erysipelas, one of diphtheria and , one of puerperal fever. The number Of patients in the hospital was 135. Ninety-five were suffering from scarlet fever, thirteen from enteric fever. eight from diphtheria and nineteen from other diseases.</p>
        <p>Pneumonia Busy. The vital statistics for Warrington for the week ended December 4th show that the number of births recorded was 26, a: decrease of six for the corresponding week of last year, whilst the deaths numbered 28, an increase of 100 per cent. on the figures for the same week last year. The excess of deaths over births last week was two. Of the 28 deaths, eight occurred to children under five years of age. Twelve of the deaths were caused by bronchitis (5) and pneumonia (7).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>"What's In A Name?"</head>
        <p>"What's In A Name?"</p>
        <p>Brigadier Read, of Liverpool, was the leader of the special ,Sunday evening service at the Pieturedrome on Sunday, and gave the address. The Male Voice Party sang a piece entitled "Eternity," and the band contributed a selec- tion of music. Band-Sergeant Ashton, of Eccles, also gave a short address. There was an excellent congregation, which was the best of the series. The Brigadier also conducted the usual meetings at the Academy-street Hall, and at the afternoon meeting, when Band-Sergeant Ashton, of Eccles, presided, gave a striking and in- teresting address on "What's in a name?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Gymnasium for Troops</head>
        <p>Gymnasium for Troops</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting of the Warrington Gym- nasium Committee a letter was read from the Central Quartering Committee stating that the Gymnasium had, subject to certain minor altera- tions, been found suitable for the accommoda- tion .of troops, and asking what rent poration would be prepared to accept in the event of the premises being required for the purpose named. It was resolved, "That the rate of £250 per annum be accepted, the heating and lighting to be defrayed by .the Govern- ment." This was confirmed at the Council meeting on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>Paesent at the Council Meeting. At the monthly meeting of the local Town Council, held at the Town Hall on Tuesday, there Were present the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Councillor G. W. Joseph (deputy- mayor), Aldermen John Evans; D. Tinnion, J. Smethurst and A. Bennett, and C,ouncillors H. Roberts, W. H. J. Jenkins, J. Dickenson, S. Gough, J. G. Craik, J. Skelland, J. T. Locker, R Henshiill, G. Dale. J. Wilkinson, W. J. Wilson, F. Lindley, J. Pemberton, F. Lash- ford, E. Cunnington, T. Chantler, G. Archer and J. Barton, and Mr. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>To Enlist and Assist.</head>
        <p>To Enlist and Assist.</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting of the Finance Sub-com- mittee of the Warrington Insurance Committee, the Clerk read a letter from Mr., D. H. Ward, the assistant clerk, thanking the committee for granting him permission to enlist, and intimat- ing that he had obtained admission to the Man- chester University Contingent of the Officers' Training Corps, with a view to obtaining a com- mission. During his Period of training at Manchester he could, if desired, attend at the office on, certain evenings of each week, and was willing to render all assistance possible. It was resolved That in. consideration of Mr. Ward rendering such assistance in the evenings as may be considered necessary by the clerk, Mr. Ward's offer should be accepted." It was decided not to engttge any temporary. clerical help at present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Forty _ Years of Faithful Sert ice.</head>
        <p>Forty _ Years of Faithful Sert ice.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Parsons, who is managing clerk to Messrs. 11. B. White and Sons, solicitors, 3, Egypt-street, and also assistant clerk to tlii! Borough Justices, has just completed 40 years' service (28 years with the .present firm, and 12 years, with Messrs. Nicholson, White and Nicholson. its predecessors). Mr. Parsons was on Thursday presented by Messrs. H. B. White and Sons with a gold watch bearing the follow- ing inscription :—" Presented to Mr. William Parsons, by Messrs. H. 13: White and Sons, solicitors, Warrington; in appreciation ;of 40, years' faithful service, December, 1915." Mr. Parsons has been resident at Moore for the past 16 years, and .is the present chairman of the. Moore Parish Council.</p>
        <p>In his position as assistant clerk to the magis- trates, Mr. Parsons performs an important pub- lic duty with skill,tact and courtesy, appreciated all the more by those who are brought into touch with him, because the service is performed with unostentatious thoroughness.</p>
        <p>. .Newsagents Fraternjse. . • A somewhat unique sight was witnessed at Atkinson's Cafe on Thursday • night, when a large number of the newsagents of Warrington and their ,wives and friends partook of tea, capi- tally served, and. subsequently enjoyed a- first- class musical .programme..- The occasion was the first annual theeting of the local branch of the Northern Counties Newsagents' Union, which, though only recently formed, has a large mem- bership. Mr. W.,Critchley, •John-street, the Warrington president, occupied the chair. Mr. T. -Carter, secretary of the -Union, Who !resides at-- Accrington, attended and, in the course of a practical address, congratulated the Warrington newsagents upon having combined to promote good fellowship among themselves and to defend their common interests against any encroachments from without. He drew attention to the advantages derived from com- bination in the matter of insurance, legal de- fence and the maintenance of rights against un- fair publishers, and even unfair newsagents. Mr. Burgess gave a recitation, and Songs were sung by the Chairman and istessrS. Auckland, J. Angus, W. Sutton, J. Hannan and. Lewis, and the Orpheus Quartet. '• Mr.. Adams, Lovely-lane, secretary of the branch, made. 'arrangements for the gathering, which ran with the utmost-smoothness. , I</p>
        <p>• No Smallpox Hospital Loan. The Local Government Board have informed the Warrington Health Committee that they are not at present prepared to approve a loan for the erection of a smallpox hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>M.A. Degree for Local Curate.</head>
        <p>M.A. Degree for Local Curate.</p>
        <p>The Rev. C. F. Waller, of Worcester College, and assistant curate at St. James' Church,, Latchford, received his degree of Master of Arts at a congregation held at Oxford on Thursday of last week. The reverend gentleman was ordained deacon on December 20th of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Printing Contract. _</head>
        <p>Printing Contract. _</p>
        <p>The Town Council re-solved on Tuesday, On the recommendation of the Finance Committee : "That the tender of Messrs. John Walker and Co., Ltd., for the printing required for the Town Clerk's Department for the ensuing 12 months be accepted."</p>
        <p>Recreation Grounds for Warrington?</p>
        <p>A strong sub-committee has been created in order to investigate and report to the Warring- ton General Purposes Committee as to the most appropriate places to be secured by the Cor- poration for the purpose of forming and laying out open spaces and recreation_ grounds in the borough.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Proposed Catholic Presentation.</head>
        <p>Proposed Catholic Presentation.</p>
        <p>The treasurer of the fund which has been opened with a view to making' suitable pre- sentations to the Rev. Fathers Neville Wilson and Basil Primavesi, who recently left War- rington, is Mr. William Dilworth, 96, Man- chester-road. We erroneously gave the name as Duckworth last week. The Chairman is Mr. J. Eckersley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Female Labour at Public Works.</head>
        <p>Female Labour at Public Works.</p>
        <p>, As indicative of the times in which we live, it is interesting to note that the Borough Electrical Engineer (Mr. F. V. L. Mathias) has been authorised to engage female labour. The Manager of the Tramways has also been authorised to employ female conductors through the agency of the Labour Exchange. The new conductors will be paid the fixed rate of Pay. Indispensable. The Medical Officer of Health (Dr. G. W. N. Joseph), the Water Engineer (Mr. James Gray), and the Assistant Gas Engineer (Mr. A. F. Yates) have expressed their willingness to join H.M. Forces, but the responsible committee in each case have resolved "That permission be not granted, the committee being of opinion that his services are indispensable to the town.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>Lecture On "India."</head>
        <p>Lecture On "India."</p>
        <p>At St. George's Mission School on Monday evening, the Rev. J. Collins, vicar of St Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, gave a very interesting lecture on India. The Rev. T. J. M. Vickers occupied the chair. There was a good number of people present, and the lecture wa.-, much enjoyed. At -the close of the lecture the Rev. J. Collins was heartily thanked for his services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>War Service for Women.</head>
        <p>War Service for Women.</p>
        <p>A special service was held at the Parish Church on Monday afternoon for the women of the town who have relations or friends serving at the Front. Special hymns were sung, and special intercessions offered. An address was given by the Rev. H. C. Moor, vicar of Earles- town, who said that no doubt the bitterness which all now felt in their hearts on account of the war, would be sweetened by God's love and discipline.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Death of a Vanier 'Warrington Freemason.</head>
        <p>Death of a Vanier 'Warrington Freemason.</p>
        <p>It is with regret that we have to announce the death of Mr. Marls Baxter, who died at _Fleet- wood last Monday morning at the age of 40 years.. Up to about, nearly four years ago Mr. Baxter resided at Latchford Without, and previous to that at Stockton Heath and Lymm. He was a prominent Freemason, and during the stay in this neighbourhood he was highly respected.. He died of enteric fever after an ill- ness of about three weeks. A widow and three children are left to mourn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Liberal Women's Social Evening.</head>
        <p>Liberal Women's Social Evening.</p>
        <p>Another of the series of whist drives and dances which have been arranged by the Liberal Women's War Sewing Committee, took place at the Liberal Club on Thursday evening. There was an attendance of about 100, who spent a thoroughly enjoyable time until about mid- night. Mrs. Pemberton presented the prizes which had been won at the whist tables. Mr. O'Brien acted as M.C. A draw for a writing case took pince, and was 'won by Mr. Johnson, who kindly gave back the prize to be drawn for at the next whist drive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Insidious Form of Extravagance.</head>
        <p>Insidious Form of Extravagance.</p>
        <p>"There is one insidious form of extravagaiace," says the "Holy Trinity Parish Magazine," "that is not so easy to deal with, and that is: the random, reckless way in which many give to charities and charitable institutions, often not taking the slightest trouble to inquire what they are giving to and not taking trouble to inquire how the money is disbursed. New charities start up daily, and no trouble is taken to learn anything about them or about their mode of working. Where there is any doubt about any Oren nicney it is far better for the charitably inclined to keep their money in hand till they have satisfied themselves as to the bona- fides of the work. In any case, a new ageneY at the present time incurs a grave responsi- bility, and should not be allowed to launch itself, especially under private authority."</p>
        <p>Wood's College Successes. At a recent examination conducted by two outside superintendents, the undermentioned Ancients I:era btaining Vitwan's Shorthand Certificates. Elementary Theory: Florence Cain, Dorothy Derbyshire, Janie Dodd, Nora Gorton, Ethel Hardcastle, Elsie Harrison, Dora Hulse, Ruth Kilshaw,- Annie Mylott, Ivy Owen, Florence Owen, Nellie Povey, Edith Saunders, Irene Hamilton-Smith, Margaret Thompson, Ethel Wells. Advanced Theory: Harold Shirt,cliffe, Marjorie Leigh, Doris Hol- brook, Ivy Owen, Nora Gorton. 50 words per minute: Ethel Johnson, Maud Mortimer, Eva Pendlebuty, May Rabbitt, Bessie Wright, Vera Williamson. 70 words per minutes: Gladys Challoner, William Hughes. 80 words per minute: Rose Byrne, Dorothy Dendy, Helen Littler. 90 words per minute: Sissie Chernock, Marion Lowe. 100 words per minute: Gertrude Bradley, Annie H. Chantler.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Rev. Herbert Dunnieo and the Peace Society.</head>
        <p>Rev. Herbert Dunnieo and the Peace Society.</p>
        <p>The Executive of the Peace Society have unanimously elected the Rev. Herbert Dunnico, minister of the Baptist Church, Kensington, Liverpool, and formerly of Golborne-street Baptist Church, Warrington, to be secretary of the Society in succession to Dr. W. Evans Darby, who is retiring. He will enter upon his duties on New Year's .Day. Mr. Dunnico has been chosen from a large number of applicants. of whom he was facile princeps. He was educa- ted at Nottingham University and .the 4idland •Raptist College, and has been in .the ministry. for fourteen years, having settled at Warrington on leaving college, when after four years': suc- cessful labour he removed to his. present charge, Which is over one of the most vigorous and influential Baptist churches in the North of England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ASsessinent of a Licensed House.</head>
        <p>ASsessinent of a Licensed House.</p>
        <p>• The' monthly . meeting of . the Warrington Assessment Committee, was .held in. the Board' Room of the. Workhouse on Friday. Mr. W. Furness presided; and there were also present Messrs. 'J. Biltinge, W. C. Brooks, A. J. Jolley, R. Lee.; a Mather, J. Robinson., J. A. Hughes, 'A. Bottomley (clerk), Gunson (valuer), W. 11. Woodcock (Warrington) and J. Dickenson (Hay. dock) assistant overseers: Only a few appeals were heard, including one of Mr. W. J. Shaw; of the Greyhound Inn, Dallam-lane, 'lt , was shown that for some considerable time- the takings had greatly reduced. The committee decided to reduce the assessment. With regard to the .new valuation list. now being prepared for Warrington, the question of the rating • :of • sewers was con- sidered, and in view of the decision of the House of Lords that sewers were rateable, the Valuer was instructed' tO aka valuation Of the sewers the, arit 'of the Union.- • The valuer was also instrtieto' to Make a 'valuation Of-the extensions at' the United' Alkali. Works at Cuordley, Messrs.'; T. and I‘. Fidars,'Earles, ;town; and the Tukaik 'Polindry, garlestOwn</p>
        <p>fr`Viiirk V 11111164 111111 k %OW 111101111 Wask Wil" NsOW', i =4 i. . "Tft E - EXAMINER" % ALMANAC .:Icir - 1916 $ ) -will •be - liven with's our issue of I I . SATURDAY NEXT, % i- DECEMBER - 18th. % i . . % lIUp-to-date Calendar, Useful Local 11 it Information and a Picture of P DISAPPEARING WARRINGTON: % li THE OLD FOX INN, i li BUTTERMARKET STREET. % .0 Of All Newsagents. % i</p>
        <p>"THE- EXAMINER"</p>
        <p>and ALMANAC.</p>
        <p>ONE PENNY.</p>
        <p>4.0 Volk Niiim Nam NENlkilk</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>, The annual sale of work organised on behalf of the Wycliffe Congregational Church, Bewsey- street, will be held on Thursday, December 16th, the time of opening being 3 p.m. "In Peace and War."</p>
        <p>The special services of the Salvation Army will be continued to-morrow in the Picture- drome. In the afternoon at 3 o'clock the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) will preside over a gathering, at which the topic will be "The Salvation Army in Peace and War."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>"The Messiah."</head>
        <p>"The Messiah."</p>
        <p>The choir of, the Primitive Methodist Church, Legh-street, assisted by a number of principals; will give a rendition of Handel's " Messiah " at their Church to-morrow. Part • 1 will be given at the service which -commences at 2.30, and Parts 2 and 3' will be rendered at the evening service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Organist and Choirmaster.</head>
        <p>The Organist and Choirmaster.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Smith. Woods, music lecturer at the Warrington Training College, gave a lecture entitled "The Organist and Choirmaster as a Dual Notationist," before the Liverpool Organists' and Choirmaster? Association on Monday. The illustrations were given by a class of boys.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Workhouse Concert.</head>
        <p>Workhouse Concert.</p>
        <p>On Monday Mr. W. Taylor, at Miss Broad- bent's request, kindly brought his excellent con- cert party to the Workhouse, where the inmates and soldiers from the Whitecross Hospital en- joyed a most delightful entertainment. Each item rendered was so excellent and spirited ;that it is impossible to select any artiste for special praise. Amongst those who gave their services were Misses 11. Taylor, W. Williams, C. Rigby and Savery, and Messrs. G. Taylor, R. Forster, and R. Syers, all of whom rendered capital items. The humorists were Messrs. R. Scarisbrick and John Bibby. The accompanist was Miss Eva Shaw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Mayor's Whist Drive and Dance.</head>
        <p>Mayor's Whist Drive and Dance.</p>
        <p>• As will be noticed f rem our advertisement columns, the Mayor and Mayoress have arranged a whist drive and dance in aid of the tobacco fund for soldiers at the Front, and the. Working Men's Infirmary Committee. Due to take place on Thursday, readers are urged to purchase their tickets without delay, so as to assist the organisers in making provision for the comforts of the patrons. The M.C.'s will be:—Mr. George Walker, whist drive; His Wor- ship the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) and .Mr. Thomas Locker, dances, assisted by several capable stewards. Several silver prizes are to be awarded. Intending players are requested to occupy the tables not later than 7.45 p.m., so that there may be a prompt start. Church Army Huts, Itt. a well-attended meeting of the Church of England Men's Society, Warrington and Dis- trict Federation, held at Stockton Heath on Saturday, .it was decided to recommend to branches that each member should give or col- lect 2s. 6d. towards the cost of providing 40 nevr Church Army huts, which are shortly to be erected near the scenes of the campaign in Flanders France, Gallipoli, or Serbia. . Mr. Nowhold's report upon the question of providing social entertainment for soldiers in Warrington was submitted and referred to a special committee. An invitation was accepted to visit the San- key' branch in April, 1916 i the next Federation meeting having been fixed for -February at the Latchford Without branch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Soldiers' Comforts Organisation.</head>
        <p>Soldiers' Comforts Organisation.</p>
        <p>A voluntary organisation of workers for Rom- forts for soldiers has been formed under official recognition. The committee is making an appeal for materials for workers, workers who are willing to assist and articles that may be utilised. The officials of the committee are as follows: The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Mr. J. H. Smethurst (chairman), Mr. J. Fairhurst (hon. treasurer) and Mr. Henry Collin (hon. secretary).- The depot for goods is at Misr ' White's, Holly House, Sankey-street. The Executive Committee gratefully acknowledge the receipt of a donation of £lO 10s. from th., students of the Warrington Commercial Insti- tute of the County Borough of Warrington Education Department, per Mr. Frank IL Longshaw, principal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Hot-Pots for Soldiers.</head>
        <p>Hot-Pots for Soldiers.</p>
        <p>Some weeks ago arrangements were made to provide a hot-pot supper once a month for the soldiers attending the Soldiers' nome, Boa- st., and for the second time the khaki men were entertained last Saturday evening. Supper was provided from 7 o'clock and during the evening, ninety-four soldiers sat down and thoroughly appreciated the meal. Thus has been added one more attraetion to make the home of real value to the men to whom we all owe so much. Hot-pots have been given by Mns. Hickman. Mrs. Wm. Isaac, Mrs. Geo. Guest, Mrs. G. Wilde, Mrs. Naden, Mrs. Lindley, Mrs. Locker, Mrs. Fairhurst, Mrs. Clegg, Mrs. Duck- worth, Mrs. Withinshaw. Mrs. T. Brough, Mrs. Lunt, Mr. C. Toft, Mrs. Bradshaw, Mrs. Greenhalgh, Mrs. Plinston, Mrs. Lumb and Mrs. R. Prince. '</p>
        <p>• Railway Temperance Workers. On Thursday evening an excellent concert was provided by the L. and N.-W. Railway Temper- ance Union (Warrington District), in the Friars' Green School, Cairo-street. Unfor- tunately it did not receive the support it de- served, owing to the very inclement weather. Notwithstanding the moderate attendance, how- ever, the audience was not slow to mark its appreciation of the efforts of the artistes, as evidenced by the repeated demands for encores. The Rev. W. Reid gave a most inspiring and encouraging address, recalling some of his early days spent in the service of a railway company. • He emphasised the value of personal influence in connection with any good work. Whilst the recent legislation for restricting the sale of liquor was admirable and essential, personal contact, the hearty handshake, and an ap- propriate word were more effective in real tem- perance work. The following were responsible for the vocal Items: Miss Elsie Hewitt (soprano), Mrs. Smallshaw (contralto), Mr. J. Sephton (tenor). Mr. A. W. Pearce (bass). Each singer was in fine .voice. Mr. Percy Smith fulfilled the duties of accompanist with marked ability. The nmorous element was provided by Mr. J. Anderton, of Wigan, who kept the audience in a merry mood by his clever imitation of a brass band. Mr. H—Butterfield's recitals were a feature, and as an 'encore he gave an excel- lent portrayal of Dickens's " Uriah Heap." Mr. Frank Jubb also contributed flute and piccolo solos, which were much enjoyed. During the evening Mr. C. Eccles. briefly out- lined the work of the Union in the Warrington District, and in proposing a vOte of thanks to the speaker and artistes, Mr. J. V.'Parisington, the assistant to the.distriet goods menager,id a tribute to the high quality of the evening** proceedings.</p>
        <p>PPP'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>TERRITORIALS Sergt. Widocks &amp; W Conductors For W SUCCESS TO T SCHEM Councillor Henshall has ing letter from Sergeant E. machine-gun section of the torials, from which it ap given the Battalion has co "Saturday,—somewhere i "Dear Mr. Ilenshall,— ", Having had my bath my • clean clothing for thought I would write although, 'if I remember e but you know the old pr• no that's not it; "If Well, I don't think I you this time, as we ha adventures since: my In am glad to say I am w ginning. I told you t (whether deserved or say). Well, that rest i day ending it, and on ti faces once more toirarc up in good time and already packed our and at 9 a.m. said 'Ai marching about 15 e several halts on the • and -took up our abe •• very big school. Cinemas "It housed all the it was pretts big. ' room # and we- were already there, So so', a bed, consequently up the next mornin day we overhauled and after: dinner IV We found ourselv• part of which is • Huns evidently kn path, as no shel stay, which lasted (lay night most of Sir, I -am not dr, of a picture she and very good spent a most enj we went ' home ' noon we bade 'g a short march, arrived at also unearthed in strong . force by the Di enlivened by t Band. We h. our old pal, C who, taking th amusement. war, does it? know. I am friends good but all good "1 may te same kind o to resting. I. they gave s this I dare. their acqu be living t I expect it QA they do trifl: " I hear well in ti it, say I out here .ciay I m• Ti.tighy e 111) the r -Warrin who, I lit up -us good " Th• nis is.• -never `burie you c, them inow and . 111011, the has Ver t • -sho 71. to 'S ‘ve 1</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS Sergt. Widocks &amp; W Conductors For W SUCCESS TO T SCHEM Councillor Henshall has ing letter from Sergeant E. machine-gun section of the torials, from which it ap given the Battalion has co "Saturday,—somewhere i "Dear Mr. Ilenshall,— ", Having had my bath my • clean clothing for thought I would write although, 'if I remember e but you know the old pr• no that's not it; "If Well, I don't think I you this time, as we ha adventures since: my In am glad to say I am w ginning. I told you t (whether deserved or say). Well, that rest i day ending it, and on ti faces once more toirarc up in good time and already packed our and at 9 a.m. said 'Ai marching about 15 e several halts on the • and -took up our abe •• very big school. Cinemas "It housed all the it was pretts big. ' room # and we- were already there, So so', a bed, consequently up the next mornin day we overhauled and after: dinner IV We found ourselv• part of which is • Huns evidently kn path, as no shel stay, which lasted (lay night most of Sir, I -am not dr, of a picture she and very good spent a most enj we went ' home ' noon we bade 'g a short march, arrived at also unearthed in strong . force by the Di enlivened by t Band. We h. our old pal, C who, taking th amusement. war, does it? know. I am friends good but all good "1 may te same kind o to resting. I. they gave s this I dare. their acqu be living t I expect it QA they do trifl: " I hear well in ti it, say I out here .ciay I m• Ti.tighy e 111) the r -Warrin who, I lit up -us good " Th• nis is.• -never `burie you c, them inow and . 111011, the has Ver t • -sho 71. to 'S ‘ve 1</p>
        <p>TERRITORIALS RESUME.</p>
        <p>Sergt. Widocks &amp; Women Tram Conductors For Warrington.</p>
        <p>SUCCESS TO THE DERBY</p>
        <p>SCHEME.</p>
        <p>Councillor Henshall has received .the follow- ing letter from Sergeant E. J, Widocks, of the machine-gun section of the Warrington T3rri- torials, from which it appears that the rtSt given the Battalion has come to an end "Saturday,—semewhere in Belgium. . "Dear Mr. Henshall,—</p>
        <p>"Having had my bath and Shaving put On my clean clothing for the ensuing week thought I would write a few lines to you, although, 'if I remember rightly, it is your-turn, but you know the old proverb "If Mahomet," no that's not it; "If the mountain, &amp;c:" Well, I. don't think I have very much to tell you this time, as we have not had very many adventures since My last' epistle. Anyway, I ani glad to say I am well—that is a good be- ginning. I told you that we were on a rest (whether deserved or otherwise I must not sag). Well, that rest is now over—last Satur- day ending it, and on the Sunday we turned our faces once more towards the enemy. We were up in good time and after breakfast, having already packed our &lt;worldly goods, we fell in, and at 9 a.m. said 'Au revoir' to . After marching about 15 miles (of course, we had several halts on the way), we arrived at and -took up our abode in what had been a very big school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Cinemas At The Front</head>
        <p>Cinemas At The Front</p>
        <p>"It housed all the Battalion, So You can see it was pretts big. The gunner S had a separate room, and we- were lncky to: find some beds already there, So some of us had the luxury of a bed, consequently it was rather hard to get up the next morning. After breakfast on Mon.: day we overhauled our guns, ammunition, and after: dinner we were at liberty to 'go out. We found ourselves in 'a very nice town, one part of which is periodically shelled, but the Huns evidently knew the 4th w.-n-e on the war- path, as no shelling took place durin our stay, which lasted until Wednesday., On Tues- day night most of us visited the Cinema. No. Sir, I -am not dreaming, for this town boasted of a 'picture show' run by the - Division. and very good pictures they were, too! We spent a most enjoyable two hours; after which we went ' home ' to bed. On Wednesday after- noon we bade ' good-bye ' to this place, and after a short march, in comparison to the other, we arrived at , also a very nice place. We also unearthed a Cinema here, and turned up in strong . force on Friday night. This is run by the - Division, and the proceedings were enlivened by the sweet strains of the Divisional Band. We had the pleasure of Meeting with our old pal, Charley Chaplin, at these pictures, who, taking the part of a tramp, caused us great amusement. This does not sound much like war, does it? But 'all work and no play,' you know. I am sure it would have done all our friends good to have heard the happy laughter, but all gpod things conic to ,an end.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Familiar Faces.</head>
        <p>Familiar Faces.</p>
        <p>"I may tell, you the old Battalion are on the same land of work as they were doing previous to resting. Isn't that a sufficient testimonial that they gave satisfaction'? , 'By the time you get this, I daresay the gunners will have resumed their acquaintance .with the trenches, and will be living the life of the pre-historic man again. 1 expect it will seem funny at first, but as long QA they don't drop their whizz-bangs and other -small trifles in our vicinity we shall not mind.</p>
        <p>" I hear Lord. Derby's scheme is working very well in the good old town, and every success to it, say I! I hope to see a few familiar faces out here before long, and, by the way, last Sun- 4tay 1 met Knox Gibbs, one of the Warrington 'Rugby team, in , and a bit farther up the road I also met one of the postmen from Warrington P.O.—a young man named Wilson -who, I think, hails from Orford. Their faces lit up with pleasure to see the 4th, and it did ..us good as well to see them. Cold And Mud, But Thriving.</p>
        <p>The 'weather is very cold, and all around rug is mud! laut- pidoWii'yofir feet, but ' you- -never know if you are going to 'leave your'shoes -buried about one foot under the surface until von can see them on your feet when you lift them up ready for the next step into the ml- -known! We are living in comfortable huts, and are doing Al. I might add that during our month's rest 1 have had to put my buttons on -the 'edge of my kersey, so that proves the rest -has been beneficial. But, all the same, it is not very nice to be addressed as Tubby!' "I see the Tramwlays Committee have decided -to employ lady conductors on the cars Fancy if 1 could have foreseen that! DO you think I -should have vacated Iny car and come To Flanders? Well, Sir, I think this must be all for this time. I trust that all are well at •Sunnyside, to the inmates-of Which please con- ‘vey my kindest regards, and remembrances to an friends.</p>
        <p>" Sincerely yotirs, E. .1. IVIDOCKS."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>BATTALION SPORTS.</head>
        <p>BATTALION SPORTS.</p>
        <p>Keeping "Fit" In Rest Time For</p>
        <p>Strenuous Days Ahead.</p>
        <p>O:Me of the numerous correspondents of the Rev. G. Vale Owen, vicar of Orford, writes a 'vivid description for the "Orford Parish Maga- Aine " for December, of Battalion sports held 6during a. recent Friday. An enjoyable afternoon was Spent, we are told, ',despite the weather, which was inclined to be _trather damp.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Officers Compete With Me.</head>
        <p>Officers Compete With Me.</p>
        <p>•!` The one-mile flat race was won by Lieut. Dickson, and a very good race it was, too. The officer • won it in grand style, with Corporal Stephens, of the liinxim-gun Section, second. Then there. was the. half-mile fiat race, which was won by another officer, Lieut. Bachelor. 'The, 100 yards flat race was won by Private :Sherratt, A Company, after a hard race. '‘,"liuch fun was obtained' from the four- ilegged race, run by. three Men tied together, the man in the centre, to run backwards with a leg 'fastened to either of the others. After much 'falling and bumping three men of B Company :sUcceeded in winning the event. Another event 'from which much fun. was derived was the wrestling on mules' backs, confined to the Trans wit Section alone, so, many representing each .company. 'The result of this particular event was that the Quartermaster's stock of shirts was somewhat lowered.</p>
        <p>Major Crosfield Compliments The Gunners. "A most interesting .event of the sports was the one confined to the Machine-gunnerS. Four .teams took .part in it, and it consisted of taking the gun out of .the limber and getting into action agkiin, and; after firing one blank cart- ridge, get out of action again and store the gun hack again in the limber. It was very smartly 4one, and the winning team was complimented personally ,by Major 'Crosfield; ho, along with all the officers, .was, very Pleaed with the dis- 'Play,- The winning team was captained by Private Warburton, but much credit Was due to Sergeant E. J. Widocks,..the Machine-gun sergeant. • • • "There was also a special event 'allotted to the Signallers. It included two melt in one. team. One had to run a reel of wire out and fix a telephone on his end, while his mate also fixed one on the opposite end. Then they had to send one message through on the buzzer by the More code and take it. to the judge,. Private J. Woek, B Company; and Private F. Jackson', D Company, were easy winners "The Bombers'. competition, which consisted in throwing dummy hand-bombs, was won by Private Whittaker, of the Bombing Section, he having thrown his bomb the farthest. I must</p>
        <p>your</p>
        <p>Cigare, &amp;see you say</p>
        <p>-</p>
        <p>also mention the tug-of-war on mules' backs, without .saddles. whibh was won easily by the team representing A Company. Lieutenant And Private Win.</p>
        <p>"The last, and one of the most interesting 'events of the clty; was the company relay race, in which officers and N.C.O.'s and men took part. A Company was successful in bringing off 'this event. Much credit for this was due to Lieutenant Dickson and Private Sherratt, who easily left their men behind. D Company obtained second place, Lieutenant Clark and Lieutenant Bachelor both running well.</p>
        <p>"The prizes consiited of cigarettes, kindly given by Major Crosfield. The winning officers did not participate in the prizes, so they were handed over to the man who got second place. Everyone enjoyed it fine, and we are anxiously looking forward to the next lot.</p>
        <p>Make RLWA ..G own USE ,• tte ha 't, • - " .e. 'CICaRETTE ER, BEST E: ST 'Nom</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
