      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>Comfortable Rest Billet.</head>
        <p>A Comfortable Rest Billet.</p>
        <p>4.‘ We have made the name of the Unbreak- able Fourth,' and we must not lose it now.; and that is one of the reasons for trying to get our- selves fit again, as I can assure you trench work takes all fitness out of a man. We are billeted in barns while we are resting, and we have a nice clean barn to sleep in. At the farm there are two old ladies and one old man. An old lady gets hot water for us to wash in every morning, and will not listen to us washing in •celd. And each night she allows us to go into the house and write our letters, as she says it is too cold in the barn. We also indulge in a little concert now and again among ourselves, I and the old people appear to enjoy it. They -don't understand much English, but luckily we have in our section a fellow who can speak and understand the French language, so we get along very well, using him as an/interpreter. Well Prepared For The Winter.</p>
        <p>It is getting very cold out here, but we are being well supplied with plenty of warm cloth- ing. We have just had issued to us a long waterproof cape and a pair of woollen gloves and plenty of warm underclothing, and those in the trenches here have been supplied with fur coats and legginged boots. These boots are much bet- ter than our others for trench work, as with these we do not wear putties, which were the cause of much frost-bite last winter, because when you went into the trenches the putties got wet through and you had to let them dry on you. with the result that one's legs and feet were always cold."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>SAW THE KING.</head>
        <p>SAW THE KING.</p>
        <p>A Treat To See Undamaged Houses And Visit Pictures.</p>
        <p>"Our most gracious King," says another Territorial correspondent in the same 4naga- zine, "has just passed . through here, and I felt very proud and also pleased to see him once more. Our company has helped to rig ,a picture- palace up for the division. We have been given the privilege of free admission during the week, and I can assure you it is quite a change. I enjoyed myself fine on the opening night. It is quite a treat to see houses that have not been damaged by shell fire, and to see the people going about just as they might be now in dear old England. It makes me feel that I have been out of civilisation since I landed last February."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SERGEANT-MAJOR DAVIES.</head>
        <p>SERGEANT-MAJOR DAVIES.</p>
        <p>Alemorial Services At Golborne-street To-morrow.</p>
        <p>Memorial services will be held to-morrow in the Golborne-street Baptist Church for the late Sergeant-Major Davies. The preacher for the day will be the Rev. H. Ogden (pastor). At 10.45 there will be a service for the family and per- sonal friends,'and•in the evening there will be a public service, which file 'Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) and a detachment of the local Territorials, under Captain Mathams, will at- tend. Madame Sara Silcock will be the soloist. The Free Church Boys' Brigade Battalion, ia which the deceased was Captain, will be pre- sent.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Davies has received letters of inquiry and messages of condolence from all parts.' Colonel Fairclough, Captain Gordon Roberts, Quartermaster-Sergeant Coakley and others have written froni the Front;'' ád the Town Council have passed a vote of condolence. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>LIEUTENANT' DICKSON PROMOTED</head>
        <p>LIEUTENANT' DICKSON PROMOTED</p>
        <p>CAPTAIN.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Brian Thorburn Dickson is gazetted a temporary captain of the 4th South Lancashire Regiment. Captain Dickson is a son of Dr. Dickson, of Marlow, Bucks, brother of Mrs. John Cros- field.</p>
        <p>Captain Dickson was on of several local officers wounded in the great clan° in Flanders on June 16th.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>LOCAL LAW STUDENT PROMOTED</head>
        <p>LOCAL LAW STUDENT PROMOTED</p>
        <p>Bairslow Gets His Company.</p>
        <p>Ins many friends in Warringtou will be pleased to hear that Lieut. Eric J. Bairstow leas recently been promoted to the rank of captain. Mr. Bairstow—who was articled to Mr. T. S. Steel, solicitor, Warrington—on the outbreak of war joined the 3rd Cheshire Regiment as second- lieutenant, and was quickly promoted to lieuten- ant. He has been in France since January, and has seen much fighting, bu.V fortunately has so far escaped injury. Whilst in Warrington Mr. Bairstow acted as hon. secretary of the War- rington La* Students' Society. His home is at Queen's Park, Chester. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Appeal To The Warrington Town Council For Support.</head>
        <p>Appeal To The Warrington Town Council For Support.</p>
        <p>At the Town Council meeting on Tuesday evening, S_ _</p>
        <p>The Mayor (Alderman Peacock) 'read a letter om the Warrington and District Volunteer Train ing Corps, which mate an appeal for support. The letter stated that.. a meeting of the Exe- cutive Committee of held on the 12th November, it Was resofv ed that a letter be sent to the Mayor asking for the support of the Council in connection with the Volunteer move- ment in the town.</p>
        <p>Official Recognitices. The letter added:—</p>
        <p>"The Volunteer Training Corps throughout the country are now recognised by the War Office, and in Various parts of the country they are actively employed on home defence work. In addition; the training of the Army (Class B) reserves with Volunteer Corps is at present under negotiation between the Central Associa- tion and the War Office. The Warrington Company is at present only about 120 strong, and it should be possible to very considerably increase this number if the members of the Town Council and others would take a practical interest in the movement, and bring it directly to the nOtice of men over military age in their employ, and others with whom they are acquainted." Mr. Swallow, on November 29th, wrote to the Mayor, enclosing a copy of a communication re- ceived by the Area Commandant from Sir James de Hoghton, the County Commandant, which Was, he said, read at the committee meeting held on the previous Friday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>" Group " Men And The Corps.</head>
        <p>" Group " Men And The Corps.</p>
        <p>The letter. from Sir James alluded to the men who enlist under Lord Derby's scheme for one day with the Colours, and are then passed to the Reserve. It added The War Office welcome the proposal that these recruits should be oncouraged to take advantage of the means of obtaining the military training furnished by the Voluntary Training Corps. Officers com- manding units of the Lancashire Volunteer Brigade should offer every facility to these recruits to obtain training. Such men will join the units as ordinary members, but subscrip- tions or entrance fees should, wherever possible, be waived. They should not be asked to pur- chase uniforms, but should be .provided with G.R..brassard. Special sections should be formed drawn from men pf a particular section number under Lord Derby's scheme, so that when men I are called up to join the Colours they can all join the same regiment and so retain the esprit de corps b£ their Volunteer Corps." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>.</p>
        <p>Mr. and 'Mrs. Walter Shaw, of 287, Liverpool- road, have receiVed the news that -their .adopted son and nephew, Private Harold Shaw, of the 1/4th South Lancashire Regiment, has been wounded in action. At present he is a. patient of the Cornelia Hospital, Poole, -Dorset, and according to the latest news is progressing satisfactorily. The .first intimation of Private H. Shaw being •wounded was received from A comrade in the same regiment (Jack Corbett), who stated:— " I have to inform You that Harold was, wounded on the 23rd inst., but you need not worry, as his injuries are not serious; they will keep him away from here for a little, while. I think he is one of the pluckiest chaps who ever came out, for he never flinched when hit. At the time I was a distance up the :firing-line, _but- he sent for the and told pie to 'let you know what had happened." • • ' Private Harold : Shaw was wounded by a "whiz-bang" .bursting near a dug-out, his right hand, head and back catching- it the most. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Prior to the war ho was employed by Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., Ltd., in their tube de- partment. As a youth he attended the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Church and was educated under Mr. Clark, Sankey Schools. On visiting Mrs. Shaw we were informed that she had a son, Private Ernest Shaw, of the same regi- ment, an inmate of the Napsborough Hospital, near St. Albans, suffering from a severe ner- vous breakdown. Her husband is at present stationed at Halton Park Camp, near Tring, and is expecting any moment to be drafted abroad. _ _</p>
        <p>Private Harold Shaw lost both father and mother some eight years ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</p>
        <p>WALKING STICKS.</p>
        <p>A Need That Is Being Unostentatiousl</p>
        <p>Met.</p>
        <p>A very large number of walking sticks have been supplied to wounded soldiers through the donations given to the fund now being raised by Mr. W. Stewart Royston, " Sunnyside," Pao- dington, " near , Warrington. Lieut.-Colonel Simpson, R.A.M.C., administrator of the Lord Derby War Hospital, has gratefully acknow- ledged a supply of 750, and riper D. Laidlaw, NT.C., has written to Mr. Royston warmly appreciating the work, and saying:— ' am sure the walking sticks are very acceptable; I know, as I have had to use one for over two months." Appeals for more and more walking sticks continue to reach,Mr. RoYston, but cannot be granted, as his fund has run dry. Sticks have been sent as far north as North Shields, and as far south as Woolwich, but Mr. ' Royston has decided to confine the grants to Lancashire Military Hospitals alone. This is a work of personal service where there is no "munition pay." for doing 'duty for King and country, no deductions for heavy postage expenses, whether the donation be pence or pounds—only a Briton's desire to give of his best services for the brave soldier and sailor who have been lamed for you. Will you help him?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>AICHMOND'S EMPLOYES' SEASONABLE GIFTS.</head>
        <p>AICHMOND'S EMPLOYES' SEASON- ABLE GIFTS.</p>
        <p>The war relief iund so successfully run by the employes of the Richmond Gas Stove Co. has this week added appreciably th the many good deeds that have been done in its name. In addition to sending Christmas presents to their fellow workmen, the committee have made the following grants towards providing Ohristmas presents for wounded soldiers :—To' the Lord Derby War Hospital, five guineas; Whitecross Military Hospital, four guineas; Raddon Court, three guineas; Heys Convalescent Home, two guineas; Infirmary Military Ward, two guineas; and Orford Barracks Hospital, two guineas.- They have • also sent three guineas to the Prisoners in Germany Fund, and three guineas to the Serbian Relief Fund. The Richmond •employee war relief fund has still a-reserve of over MOO.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>WHITECROSS MILITARY HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THE WHITECROSS MILITARY HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Miss Marie Houghton gave the fifth of her series of .concerts ,to the, wounded soldiers at the above hospital on Siturday. The following artistes took Part: Miss Doris Read (solo piano- forte). Miss Cook (contralto), Mr. A. W. Pearce, Mr. Chas. Lambert, and Mr. Walter Jones (of Manchester). •.A capital programme was ar- ranged. and the audience were very enthusiastic and called for encores to each item, and joining in the chorus songs in such a manner that left no doubt that a most enjoyable evening had been. spent by all.</p>
        <p>If you could have just whatsou wanted, would you want it?. . . Many a man who boasts that 'he knows him: self might. wsll be ashamed of the friendship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>11111 •</head>
        <p>11111 •</p>
        <p>THE REAL STORY OF CORPORAL TOMBS, Y,C,, O.S,G.</p>
        <p>Nephew Of An Indian Mutiny V.C. Who Rose To</p>
        <p>High Rank.</p>
        <p>HERO AND WARRINGTON'S "PRIOR CLAIM."</p>
        <p>SPECIAL INTERVIEW FOR "THE</p>
        <p>A gaunt and gli)omy building loomed ahead of me, a few lights twinkled in the distance through the damp and haze of approaching night, and in the streetwhich I traversed a child cried pitifully over a broken vessel which lay at her feet. The rain had ceased, and inside the lodge through which I had to pass a fire glowed cheerfully and bright. I passed on into the open courtyard; where a few men, hard of visage, growled and grumbled at the weather, under cover of a • wooden strrture, and as I wended my way they looked at me tts if my presence was obtruSive, yet I heeded them not. Ahead of me an open door, through which I. could see nurses clad in their picturesque and neat costumes, making their journeys to the bedsides of wounded sol- diers to comfort or to tend those heroes who lay in the Whitecross Military Hospital, an insti- tution famous in this our town. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Thinking of Christmas.</head>
        <p>Thinking of Christmas.</p>
        <p>• At the entrance I stood a moment in doubt, then walked in. No one stopped me, so I espied a part open door on the left. It was Ward No. 3.</p>
        <p>" Come in," shouted a young man, who lay reading an evening paper in bed. I took his word. In front of me, seated at a table, was a soldier busy making festoons out of red tissue paper, and, as I caught sight of what he was doing, the thoughts of the coming festive season flashed through my mind. Here, in, spite of the toll of battle, was one working to add a little cheer to the surroundings. The soldier who at there was small of stature, regular of feature, with hair and moustache of sandy hue, and on the crown of his head I noticed that with the advance of years a small patch, which be- spoke of approaching baldness. To me he seemed much the same as the other soldiers</p>
        <p>there,. yet how easy can one be mistaken. My mission had taken me to visit Corporal Tombs, V.C., 0.5. G., who arrived in this hospital from Nottingham on Tuesday last.</p>
        <p>_ To the soldier with the busy fingers engaging. his time for festive days I asked of the where- abouts of the valiant hero of May 16th last. '".He is close by," replieemy informer, with smile;- and I waited. He did not move, and then I understood, for, in front of me, dangling from the khaki tunic, I. espied, with sorie surprise, England's most coveted honour and prize. I had found my man. After the usual customary salutations I ex- plained my visit, and he was all smiles.</p>
        <p>Then, over cigarettes (and_ Corporal Tombs</p>
        <p>, likes a good cigarette), he told me his story—of his life, of his career, of the receptions by his countrymen—and as I listened I knew that not only had I found a British soldier proud of his country, but a well-educated man, one who could mingle in society and be .not afraid, at ease under any circiimstances, yet withal without a trace of snobbishness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Warrington's Claim to Corporal Tombs.</head>
        <p>Warrington's Claim to Corporal Tombs.</p>
        <p>To many of our readers the question has often been raised as to whether or not the gallant warrior is a native of Warrington. This was one of the first questions I put to Corporal Tombs, and his answer I give_ in this interesting con- versation I had with this estimable gentleman. This is the story : " Aly friend," said he, "you ask me if my birthplace is Warrington, and to ,that question I am unfortunate enough to inform you that I cannot tell.you. You see there are many things in one's life of a purely personal nature that one does not want the outside world to know. I have been a wanderer over God's earth, and my early childhood is clouded as with the mist of night. Strange sights have I witnessed and strange places have I visited, all of which have gone, and with their passing more anon. This much I can say, that the town in which I ain at present staying I look upon as my birthplace, for</p>
        <p>- • u me here have I found those who are dear and</p>
        <p>near to me. To me it is my native place, and as such I will admit to everyone who comes in Contact with me.</p>
        <p>"Strangely enough, there have .been Many</p>
        <p>inquiries respecting- the place of my birth, and to them I have always given the answer, I do not know, but Warrington, that busy manufac- turing centre, has prior claims. It is four years</p>
        <p>last March since first I came to the town,' and as I look over the past I do not regret it, for in it I found happiness in more ways than one. When I first arrived I little thought that within a few years I should have gone through what I have done, yet what of it, for is it not so, my friend, that success in many shapes and forms comes to us with the passing of the years?" An Ancestor's Mutiny V.C.</p>
        <p>I nodded assent, and he continued : " I am not the first member of my family to win the soldier's most coveted honour," he said with a smile, "for my late uncle. was Major- General Sir Henry Tombs, V.C., K.C.8., an Indian Mutiny veteran. Would you like to read the. story of how he earned it?" he continued. • I answered in the affirmative, and for a few brief moments he left me. Across the room two soldiers were participating in a game of</p>
        <p>draughts, each studying the other's next move. At ray. right hand a sister tenderly nursed a</p>
        <p>bruised soldier, another lay only a few feet away swathed in bandages, and in the next room a Belgian soldier sang a lullaby maybe learned at his mother's knee in far-away Flanders. Through the open door came the sound of' a playing gramophone, and the singing of an Irish melody. " Killarney " it was, and with the strains I thought of those round about me, in pain—yet how patient in the midst of suffer- ing! With the 'appearance of Corporal Tombs the soldiers finished their game, for, although they knew whom they had amanist them, they did not know of the information he Was about to impart to the company. • Before reading the .account of how his uncle, Major-General Sir Henry Tombs, won the'V.C., our hero explained that his late relative had seen service throughout the Indian' Mutiny, also in Abyssinia and in the Afghan wars of 1879-80. He was gazetted "fort. most obstinate and cour- ageous defence of two guns in the Indian Mutiny, where by his heroic conduct he saved the life of the now General Sir James Hill- Johnes, son :of the late James Hills, of Neech- indipore, Bengal. s . The • following particulars are related from ." The History of the Victoria Cress," by Philip A. Wilkins, and give a graphic account of Cor- poral Tombs's late uncle • "On July .9, 1857, Lieut. Hills was placed in command of two gnns of his battery in a specially selected and dangerous position, to be ready at a- moment's notice to move to a given . point in case of a sortie by the garrison, or to :repel outside attack or an attempt to raise, the -siege. Here this young officer, then hardly 24, was frequently attacked' -by cavalry at close quarters, on each occasion. defending the Post .most gallantly, being aided by his comnianding officer, Major (late Major-General) Sir Henry Tombs, V.C., K.G.B. The official despatch of Lieut.-Colonel Mackenzie to Brigadier Wilson, reporting the bravery of Lieut. Hills and Major Tombs, reads as follows: `lt is with great ,pleasure I submit the following account of the very gallant- conduct of Second-Lieutenant 'James Hills, 2nd Troop, Ist Bengal Home Artil- lery, and the noble behaviour of his command- ing officer, Major H. Tombs. Lieut. Hills was on picket duty, with two .guns, at the mould to the right of the camp. About 11 a.m, there was a rumour' that €4e*enerity's cavalry were 'coming AOWn on this post Lient gills proceeded to 'take .up the position, assigned in case of alarm, but before he reached the .spot he saw the enemy close upon his guns before he Led time to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>EXAMINER" BY HAROLD MAYBURY.</head>
        <p>EXAMINER" BY HAROLD MAYBURY.</p>
        <p>form up. To enable him to do this_Lient. Hills</p>
        <p>boldly Charged, single-handed, the heart of the enemy's column, cut down the first man, struck</p>
        <p>the second, and was then ridden down, horse and</p>
        <p>all. On getting up and searching for his sword,</p>
        <p>three more men (two mounted) came at him. The first he wounded 'with his pistol, lie caught</p>
        <p>the lance of the second with his left hand and</p>
        <p>wounded him with his sword.</p>
        <p>The first man</p>
        <p>then came on again and was cut down; the third man on foot then came up and wrenched the sword from the hand of Lieut. Hills, who</p>
        <p>fell in the struggle, and the enemy was \ about to cut him down, when Major Tombs, who had</p>
        <p>gone to visit his two guns, saw what was going on. rushed in, shot the man and saved Lieut.</p>
        <p>Hills. By this time. the enemy's cavalry had passed by, and Major Tombs and Lieut. Hills went to look after fife wounded men, when Lieut. Hills observed one of the enemy passing with his (Lieut. Hills) pistol. They walked to- wards him. The man began flourishing his sword and dancing about. He first cut at Hills, who parried the blow, and he then turned on Major Tombs, who received • the blow in the same manner. His second attempt on Lieut. Hills was, I regret to say, more successful, -as he was cut down with a bad sword cut on the head, and would have no doubt been killed had not Major Tombs rushed in and put his sword through the man. I feel convinced that such gallant conduct on the part of these two officers has only to be brought properly forward to meet with an appropriate reward. Major Tombs was saved from a severe sword cut on the head by the wadded head dress he wore."</p>
        <p>And so Corporal Tombs ended his narrative, which is authentic, of his ancestor's valour in the memorable days IA the Indian Mutiny.</p>
        <p>Urged on by Glorious Example.</p>
        <p>"SO you see," he continued, "I had to place another honour in the annals of our family. I suppose in the crisis through which I passed there was stirred within me the blood and courage if this beloved uncle of mine, now un- fortunately at rest. Who knows, after all, can one help it, if at moments like these, we lose ourselves for the glory which has only been dormant? I need not remind you of what I have done. It would, perhaps, have been better that the notoriety attached to the honour had not been so persistent, for at the least I can say that out there, in. France and Belgium, -each hour, each day, there, are heroes even better than I, yet who are unknown, who have not the medal such as I have.</p>
        <p>"The fortunes of war, you say? Yes, but many have not been as fortunate as I. Mine has been a varied career. For a long time I dwelt in Australia, visiting the -large cities there, and from that isle I went to Central America, work- ing for about two years on the Panama Canal in the engineering construction department.</p>
        <p>"It was dull and montonous, the climate did not agree with me, so I migrated to Cuba. From there I journeyed to the United t:tates• eventually finding myself in Philadelphia. "That was a little over four years ,ago, and my next place was, well, cannot you guess? It was here, Warrington. As you already know, I first enlisted at Orford Barracks, and when I left England to. fight the accursed foe I made up my mind in the silence of my own room on board the vessel bound for France to do my best for King and country. On the battlefield I thought of my uncle, of the honour, and it was this that helped me perform that task which I to-day' recall with pleasant memories. To me publicity counts for little; I did my duty, for foremost I was a soldier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>Cherished Thoughts.</head>
        <p>Cherished Thoughts.</p>
        <p>"The times through which I have passed are cherished in my thoughts, as they should be in every soldier's. Even after gaining the honour, which you see pinned* upon my breast, I was not satisfied, and the call, even in spite of my previous experiences, was strong within me. To France I again went, and had only been with the battalion ten days when I was in the thick of it again. One Friday night we went into the trenches, a little way from the La Bassee Canal, slow of movement, winding along the countryside peaceful and sleepy. The moon was at its height, and I thought of home, yet I was reminded that here were the horrors of war. The shells burst overhead, and away in the distance one could hear the booming of the big guns. Saturday morning dawned, bright and clear, yet I knew not of what the day held in store. At 6 a.m. I was wounded' by shrapnel in the arm and face, as well as having my fill of poisonous fumes. It was only slight, yet it turned the buttons on my tunic a vivid green. For days I could not eat properly, and even how have to partake of special diet. I landed back in England on October 2nd, and was sta- tioned at the West Bridgeford Military Hospi- tal, Nottingham, where, under care and kindly consideration I was nursed into something of my old self. Many of my teeth had ,been knocked out in the ' scrap ' with the exit/mans, as you no doubt will have noticed. I Nanted to be back in Warrington, for, after all, this is where I want to settle. Every kindness was shown to me in Nottingham, for which I am very grateful, but, after all, one' can become bored with festivities and receptions, and now I want peace and rest. "Whilst out billeting near La Bassee I met many comrades, one of whom was Sergt. liar-</p>
        <p>ris, who unfortunately, I now learn, has been killed in action. He and I were great pals, and his loss has been a severe on to me.</p>
        <p>From Generation to Generation.</p>
        <p>"Such is war, my friend," he continued, "but maybe there's a little rift in the clouds and the sun is nigh peeping through; at least. I hope so."</p>
        <p>So this gallant soldier concluded his story. At times his eyes were moist, for to him has been given a clear understanding of the chahg- ing fortunes of a soldier's life. The little ward was strangely. silent, the hour was getting late, and rest is essential to the afflicted warrior. The sisters had refined after their labours, the gramophone had been covered, and the sound of song and laughter was stilled. Here in our midst was life in reality, and amongst us a man who seeks not publicity for gain—just a soldier honoured and respected, without pre- tensions of looming too much in the light of publicity, whose every word and action showed that first he was a soldier. The rain had now • begun to fall again, and night had fallen. So I left him—him who had been amongst strangel people in strange climes, him with a chequered career, and as I passed out into the street, now empty and deserted, I felt a loneliness unex- plainable. I heard no longer the cry of the child who had broken the vessel.when first I made my way down the street, neither were there any men under shelter of a rough constructed shed, and I was glad to be alone with my thoughts— thoughts of gladness of how proud I was of this British hero. A few hours later, in the vague mist of troubled sleep, I saw in the centre of a scene of bloody carnage, the figure of a khaki- clad soldier making its way over the uneden •ground to comrades wounded and torn. Shot and shell and horror unspeakable! But ever through the maze a figure bringing his fellow comrades to safety! I saw also a beam of light in the shape of a cross whereon were emblazoned the words "For valour.? I saw, too, the form of an Indian Mutiny veteran, contented and happy, .smiling all the time. `f 1857-1915," he murmured—" and for the honour and, glory of</p>
        <p>our name." Then the ,sight was blotted out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>ww(</head>
