      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Iv.</head>
        <p>Iv.</p>
        <p>EX-INSPgCTOR'S PATRIOTISM.</p>
        <p>7,ongratulalions And Good Wishes</p>
        <p>The Witteh Committee.</p>
        <p>At the Town Council meeting an •Tuesday, the Chief Constable' reported that ex-Chivf In- spector Phillips, who was gra:l,...ed a police pen- sion- in 1907, after :18 years' serviee with the Borough Police Force, was now serving •as Sergeant in the King's (Liverpool) Regiment; that Sergeant Phillips was over 53 years of age. and had served for nearly seven years in the First Royal Dragoons before joining the Borough Force. • • • It was resolved "That the Town Clerk con- vey to Sergeant Phillips the hearty congratula- tions of the, committee on his patriotism and long service, and their best wishes-for his sUp- cess and safe return."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>CORPORAL'S WEDDING.</head>
        <p>CORPORAL'S WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Pretty Ceremony -•A t The Leh-stree Primitive Methodist. Church.</p>
        <p>A quiet and pretty wedding took place, by special licence, at . the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Chapel on Saturday, the officiating minister being the Rev. .A. J. Chessum. The contracting parties were Miss Lily Mabel All. only daughter of Mr. and Mrs. John A.llman, of 55, Legh-street, and Corporal Edwin Weil', of, the R.A.M:C.,- son of Mr. Lairrence Weir, 22, Arthur-st. The bride, who was charm- ingly attired An ,a navy blue silk dress, and black velvet hat, trimmed with a white plume, and wore a gold wrist watch; the gift of the bridegroom, was given away by her eldest brother, Mr. William Allman. The bridesmaids.; were Miss Clara Allman and Miss Mabel All- man (niece of .the bride); the former .wearing a. Saxe blue costume, and a gold brooch, the gift of .the bridegroom. • The duties of best man were performed by Mr. David Humphries. A reception was held at the home of • the bride,' and later in the ,day the happy couple left for Blackpool; the' bride travelling in a navy blue costume. Many 'useful and valuable presents have. been received. by Corporal and Mrs. Weir. ; The bridegroonl,- NVhdi previous- to enlisting,. acted as secretary, .to the. Town Hall Ward' 'Liberal Committee, is' well known and' eeteemed by a large circle f friends</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>STORIES FROM THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>"VERY TRYING."</p>
        <p>A 'Turkish Shell That Upset The Whole-</p>
        <p>Show.</p>
        <p>• Private J. Plunaptori, whose-wife and family live at Thelwall-lane, Leitehfoid, and who is •serving with the South Lancashires in the Daidanelles, writing to emu:minor Henshall, Latchford, says:— "We landed here all right on. September 26th. They took us straight up to the trenches into the front firing line, and rhave been there ever since. I am servant to the batt*lion machine- gun officer, and although our battalion gets a rest a company-at a time, we are ahvays'-up in the firing trench. I can tell you it is very trying to a man to have to sleep with his dothes and boots and full equipmauet on for 41 days and nights, and very little chance of getting washed and shaved, only occaskonally.• ",The climate here (November 6th)', in the daytime is terribly hot, and the flies- are un- bearable; but the nights go very cold and some- times Wet and, if not wet, the dews that' fall are as bad as the rain. ' "There' are times When it is anything but comfortable (and I can tell you' it is rough at the best of times) when Johnny Turk starts to shell us, and vi'6 get more or less of it every day.... With the reply from our artillery and naval guns on the ships in the harbour it is deafening. I have had one very narrow escape as he dropped one in our dug-out while I was getting tea ready and upset the whole show. It wounded an officer and servant, and when we got up again and shook the dust from us I found all the tins of jam and milk full of pieces of shrapnel. But there were none in me, so I have something to thank God for. With best wishes to yourself and family and all friends at the chapel—Good night-and God bless you all."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>ORFORD "BOYS."</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>Give Their Experiences By Land And</head>
        <p>Give Their Experiences By Land And</p>
        <p>•T Sea. In the "Orford Parish Magazine" for Decem- ber there appear many letters from Orford " boys " who are serving in all parts of the world, and who thank the Vicar for the interest he is taking in them. From these letters we take the following extracts:— Jack's Lament. "I see,'. writes a Jack Tar from the neigh- bourhood of Sulva Bay, "we have found another enemy in Bulgaria; we will make them sorry for it. I bet our Fleet won't take long to smash all her coast up. It is a pity she has no Navy. I see also that our submarines have been at it again in the Baltic. It is a pity the Germans won't send their Fleet out and give our subs. a chance, the same as we give theirs." "It is a grand sight at night-time," writes another sailor in the' same region, " for the I bursting of shells lights the place up all around the area. It is said the Turks want more shells. Well, we are giving them tons, but in a different form than they like them. We keep them very busy. They don't get very much rest from us. What with the shells from the big ships, field batteries, machine-guns and rifles, they get a hot time of it. They don't seem to trouble us very much lately. They send us a shell or two now and then.</p>
        <p>•" Turkey For All "—And German Sausage. A humorous Tommy, writing from somewhere in the Mediterrane#n, speaking of the Christmas holidays and feast, sends the following messafe "for men aged betweeri 18 and 40 years " :-- " To all young men who are eligible and who are anticipating holidays and feasting this Christmas; my advice is to pay a visit to this part of the globe, where there is Turkey for all, and though only part cooked at present, we hope to have it well cooked by Christmas. Those who have any partiCular taste for wings, I am sorry \ to say; will be disappointed, as we had to clip them off at an early date. But any- one eligible who has not had Turkey and Ger- man sausages this Christmas will hang his head when the boys come home and tell their tale of the great feast." . , R.A.M.C. Work in the Field. • - "I will try to tell you something of the . R.A.M.C. on the battlefield," writes another of the Vicar of Orford's "boys." "We built our , wards with bushes, stones, sticks and earth by digging in the ground to form a dug-out. That performance took up the greater part of a day. During the afternoon it began to rain spots ea big as plates. Our clothing was of a very thin drill, for hot countries and not for wet ones, so you can guess how we were after about two hours' rain. - But we stuck to our post and finished our hospital. . Then up to the firing- line we had to go, each man carrying one stretcher. After a few stiff climlis' up hill and down dale, we were soon in the trenches amidst the scream of the shells and the cry of the wounded. It is wonderful how soon one can get used to the 'ping-ping ' of the bullets, and even take little notice of the shells, although they burst all round and even above you. We carry our wounded from the trenches, two men with each stretcher, over rough country, for about , five miles; it is very heavy work, for we are fired dn by snipers—both patients and bearers. If you get a heavy man you cannot go far with- out a rest, so it takes a good four hours to get him to hospital. But we do our best,- one and all. We have lost a few men wounded and dead. One Sunday morning we received a visit from a . German taube. He called our attention to the fact by dropping a bomb, wounding nine R.A.M.C. men. Thent,were four hospitals round about at the time, each flying a Red Cross flag. Set they dropped a bomb. There are many instances I could tell you of where they , fire on the ambulance. I do not think it is the : Turk but the German officer,, they have over. them. ..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE REGT. (VOLUNTEER).</p>
        <p>Warrington Company.</p>
        <p>Headquarters, Drill Hall. ORDERS FOR WEEK ENDING DEC. 18th, By W. PETER RYLANDS, Commandant.</p>
        <p>Lord Derby's Recruiting Scheme. To-day, 2.30 and 7 p.m. All men who can d4l so are requested to attend this final rally to-day, to assist in clerical and other work.</p>
        <p>Parades. Monday.—C.O.'s Parade 8. p.m. Thursday, 8 p.m.—Company Drill, under C.S.M.</p>
        <p>Trench Digging. Saturday, December 18th, 2.30 p.m. Site, Westerly aide Old River. Section B Reserve § *ill attend for lecture and in. struCtion.</p>
        <p>Section Commanders' Class. Tuesday, 8 p.m., under C.S.M. Sullivan.</p>
        <p>Semaphore Class. Drill Nights, 9to 9.39 p.m. Instructor, F. S. Rossiter.</p>
        <p>Section B Reserve: Monday and Thursday, 8 p.m., Squad Drill. Saturday, 2.30 p.m., Lecture on Modern Trench Digging.</p>
        <p>MUSKETRY.</p>
        <p>Christmas Prize Shoot.</p>
        <p>Tuesday, 7.30 p.m., Drill Hall. Orderly for the week, Private F. S. Davies, No. 1 Section.</p>
        <p>MEMORANDA.</p>
        <p>(1) Six more men are required to form No. 1 Squad Section B Reserve. (2) The.C.O. reminds all Volunteers that the, first principle of the movement is to assist in the enlistment of men for Ills Majest3r's Forces, and further comment is unnecessary in order to ensure a Parade in force to-day.</p>
        <p>11th December 1915.</p>
        <p>• The worst foe you have is the man who would kill all your eueritioa,</p>
