      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>ROYAL LANCASHIRE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>ROYAL LANCASHIRE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of members of Royal Lancashire Agricultural Society, at Preston, Mr. Fitzherbert-Brockholes in the chair, the report and balance sheet showed that the Society's profit on the Blackburn Show was £1,208. The money is to go to all kinds of charitable insti- Autions, including £227 to the Ruined Farmers' Fund. Mr. Windham Hale, in moving the adoption of the report and balance sheet, con- gratulated the Society on being the only association in England that had made a profit on this year's exhibition. Owing to the visit of the R.A.S.E. to Manchester, it was decided to abandon the 1916 show. In reply to a question, Mr. Hale said that if Lancaster wished to have the show in 1917 he should advise that it be accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>CANADIAN BUTTER FOR ENGLAND.</head>
        <p>CANADIAN BUTTER FOR ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>There has been a great development in trade between Canada and Great Britain during the past eighteen months, and in this development Manitoba has played no small part. Of late there have been large shipments of butter sent from that province to the Montreal market, where it has been purchased by English buyers. Owing to the war, England has not. been able to draw her supplies of butter from some of the sources open prior to August, 1914, and conse- quently she has turned to Canada. Large quantities have left Winnipeg during the prescni season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>HOUSING PULLETS.</head>
        <p>HOUSING PULLETS.</p>
        <p>"Pullets should roost in well built and sub- stantial houses, so that the food may go, not to maintaining the animal heat of the body, but rather to the production of flesh and eggs, writes "Middlesex," in "Farm and Home." More- over they should not be overcrowded in their roosting department, as nothing perhaps retards development more than this. They should have a constant supply, of pure, fresh air, without which they will grow and develop slowly. They should, moreover, be allowed as niuch freedom as possible, as the more exercise they procure the healthier will they be, and the stronger and hardier their constitutions. Undoubtedly the best system of housing them is on the colony plan—that is in portable houses, scattered up and down the farm. Under such conditions they will grow well, obtain a large quantity of the best kind of food, namely natural, and more- over do the land a great deal of good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>AMERICAN BLIGHT ON APPLE TREES.</p>
        <p>In many gardens this pest is all too common, and from what we can see in visiting gardens it is spreading rapidly. One of the best remedies for this loathsome pest is a mixture sold by our horticultural sundriesmen and known as Woburn wash. The stems and every part of the branches where these can be reached should be painted over with the wash, using a half-worn painter's brush for the purpose. Spray all the smaller twigs and branches with it. When using this remedy the operator should use old gloves and wear some old clothe, as this Woburn wash contains caustic soda. It may be that one application of the wash will fail to clear off the pest. Should this be the case then directly any of the pest is seen at once set to work and destroy it by brushing the parts of the tree where it is in evidence, with methylated spirit or undiluted paraffin. In the clearance of this pest perseverance is the great factor.</p>
        <p>VENO'S LIGHTNING COUCH CURE The Ideal family remedy. f" Contains no opium morphine. paregoric. or other harnlful drug. Cures at all ages. OUGHS.COLDS ,&amp; INFLUENZA Venn's is the surest and speediest cure for these winter ills, the best pro. tection against more serious dangers. 9 CICLODU RG HE SN S.</p>
        <p>Soon yield to Veno's—even Whoop!lg cough. And there is no trouble m giving it, children simply lore Veno's. _ _ Large Other sizes 1/11 and 8 " from chemists- and stores Trial everywhere. Refit*. sub- Bottle 1 2 :Mutes, they are NOT. "just as good as Vote%</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY DECEMBER 11, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY DECEMBER 11, 1915.</p>
        <p>.1. tt:'; 1</p>
        <p>LIYMM.</p>
        <p>Licensing.—On Monday, at Sale; temporary authority was granted to Robert Parker to sell at the Spread Eagle Hotel, Lymni.</p>
        <p>Lymm Grammar School—Prizes and certifi- cates will be distributed by Vice-Chancellor Stewart-Smith, K.C., in the Schoolroom, on Saturday, December 18th, at 4.45 p.m. Invita- tions have already been forwarded by the Gov- ernors and Headmaster to notable people in the village.</p>
        <p>Local Tri bu na I.—The Tribunal Committee chosen in accordance with the Lord Derby scheme of recruiting consists of the following: Mr. R. H. Grotobed, • Mr. W. Groves, Mr. J. H. Holt, representing the Council; and Mr. T. J. Ridgway, Mr. S. W. Gould. Clerk, Mr. W. Mullard.</p>
        <p>Motor Accident.—A. somewhat alarming motor accident occurred at the "Four Lane Ends" about five o'clock on Tuesday evening, two cars belonging respectively to Mr. H. S. Saville, of the Cross, Lymm, and Jiolt Bros., Broomedge, colliding with great force. The car belonging to the latter was extensively damaged, the front axle being badly twisted, and one wheel was completely wrenched off. Luckily, however, neither parties driving were injured.</p>
        <p>Sergt. J. H. Moston.—Writing to his father, Mr. Henry Moston, of the Anchor Inn, Sergt. Moston says: "We are all quite well and in good spirits. We have now come out of the trenches, and I-can tell you we can all do with the rest. We are using ,the pick and shovel all day long, but anyhow we get a good night's rest, which is something to be thankful for. We are also a lot safer here, being some considerable distance from the firing line."</p>
        <p>To • Better Times.—" I have left but a line to mention the Christmas Festival," says the Rec- tor in his December address to his parishioners, largely taken up with War topics, "and to wish you all happiness at heart at that season. We shall remember our brave lads fighting our battles abroad, and Ahall he with them in spirit, trusting that the great God of all, Who showed His love for the world at this time, will continue His goodness to us and bring us through our present tribulation to a time when 'Peace and good will to men,' will have a • more obvious fulfilment than in this sad year now so fast drawing to a close."</p>
        <p>Price of Coal.—A meeting was held on Mon- day night at the Council Chambers to consider a circular which had been received from the Board of Trade respecting the fixing of a maxi- mum price of coal, by the dealers in the dis- trict. Amongst those present were Mr. R. H. Gotobed (in the chair), Mr. Groves, Mr. Cow- per, Mr. Lockyer, and Mr. MuHard (clerk). All the dealers in the district, by invitation, were present, including Messrs. Travis, Hinton, Walker, Edwards, Holt, Allman and Mantle. After consideration the prices unanimously fixed were :—Household coal, in bag of lcwt., is. 4d.; best coal, in bag .of 6d.—Mr. Mullard eventually reported the result of the decision to the authorities in question.</p>
        <p>Recruiting Committee.—There are only a few hours more wherein to make the necessary journey to the local recruiting office to attest under Lord Derby's scheme, and after the many letters we have published from the Lymm boys in the trenches we feel that this last appeal will not be in vain. To the reader we ask the question "What answer is to be flashed back to them, out there fighting the foe?" Back up the Lymm boys and their ,:,omrades who are doing their bit. The last day. Haven't you answered the call?</p>
        <p>Until twelve to-night you con be an English- man, someone to be proud of, not only now but in later days.</p>
        <p>I The Call From The Balkans.—As will be noticed in the advertisement on another page, a sale, tea and entertainment is to be given at the Grammar School, Lymm, on Wednesday, December 15th, in aid of the Serbian Relief Fund. We should like to call the attention of our many readers to the great and pressing need of this gallant little ally of ours in the Balkans, and as no other effort has been made previously in the village for this noble cause it is to be hoped that the inhabitants not only of Lymm, but the outskirts, will assist in a way (no matter how humble) the organisers. We can assure the public that the entertainment is to be one of the best yet witnessed in the neighbourhood, and well worth a visit, for cinematograph films are to be shown, as well as other numerous forms of enjoyment. All the articles for sale are useful ones, and no one will regret a visit to the Grammar School on the date stated, we feel sure. The proceedings are to be opened by Mrs. Stewart Smith, of Lymm</p>
        <p>TROOPER JOHN WILD.</p>
        <p>Reported To Have Been Killed In Turkey.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant and Mrs. Walter Wild, of " Leckonby," Lymm, have, within the last few days received the tragic news that their son, Trooper John Wild, Mounted Rifles, Auckland. New Zealand, is reported killed in action. Trooper Wild, well-known and respected in Lymm and the surrounding districts, was 22 years of age, and at tile outbreak of war was residing in New Zealand. As the Motherland called he offered his services, and was eventually drafted to Egypt and subsequently to Turkey. The photograph which we reproduce was taken in Cairo only a few days after he had landed on Egyptian territory. His father, who is well- known in the village, had very grav* doubts as to his son being alive, for the last letter from the unfortunate soldier is dated July 31st, addressed to Trooper Wild's sister. In it he stated:—</p>
        <p>"I suppose you know now that I am in</p>
        <p>Turkey; in fact I have been there nearly three months, and it would not be so bad if we could buy a few little things to eat. Bully beef anti biscuits are all right in their way, but give me a change at times. We are having lovely weather out here I am thankful to say, but what spoils it are the flies, for they are always hungry and the most persistent lot you ever come across. They give , long points to those out in Egypt, and that is saying something. The Turkey "Season."</p>
        <p>"The Turkey season seems to be dying down now. We don't seem to get the good shooting and big bags we used to do when first we came here. You offered to send me cigarettes. Well, don't bother about them, as I usually manage to get enough, but I will bless you to y dying day if you would only send me some milk chocolate. You know we iike fancy tucker at times."</p>
        <p>Trooper Wild, a well-set young man and hardy .of frame, was educated as a youth at the Oxford College, Waterloo, Liverpool, where he made many friends. In consequence of no further news being re- ceived since the above letter his father wrote to several sources with a view to obtaining news of his son's whereabouts, with the result that he received the following telegram from the Minister of Defence, Wellington, New Zealand: "Regret to advise you cable received this day reports that Trooper John Wild, Auckland Mounted Rifle Regiment, is reasonably supposed to have died on the Bth of August last. Please accept my sincerest sympathy in the loss which you and New Zealand have suffered." His parents also have been in receipt of the following wire from the Private Secretary to the Governor of New Zealand, Wellington :—"I am directed to convey an expression of the sin- cere sympathy felt for you by their Excellencies the Earl and Countess of Liverpool in the sad loss which you have suffered."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>COUNCILLOR COWPER'S SON.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR COWPER'S SON.</p>
        <p>Graphic Account Of Affairs In</p>
        <p>East Africa.</p>
        <p>Councillor C. E. Cowper, of!' The Chestnuts," Lymm, has received the following interesting letter from his son, Herbert, at present in English East Africa. The son of the popular Lymm COUncillor has been in Rhodesia with the Chartered Company for about nine years. Danger of Lights.</p>
        <p>"I am writing to ask you if you would mind getting me a watch like the enclosed cutting, • Campaign' watches. They are visible in the ' dark, and are very useful when on guard at night, as it is impossible to have any fires, and to strike a match is dangerous. A patrol of the N.R.R.'s ,which went into German territory about 70 miles lost some men some time ago be- cause they were careless with lights at night. I also want a pair of field glasses, very good ones. "I am writing this from a place called which is 500 miles from the railway at and We still have another 100 mill* to go before we get to . I am in charge of the escort, comprising five white men and twenty- four native troops. We have two waggons, one with stores on and another with shells. So far there has not been any need for caution, but a month ago the Germans cut the telegraph line about 60 miles further up the road, so that we shall have to keep a sharp look out. Native Fighters.</p>
        <p>"The Germans have a large number of native troops, who fight in the native fashion, viz., they do not take any prisoners. They are armed with 11 m/m Mauser magazine rifles, which shoot a lead bullet slightly lighter than a Martini-Henry rifle. Our native troops are now being armed with the .303 magazine rifle. Previously they had Martini-Henry's, but these were continually jamming. One of the Dudleys.</p>
        <p>" One of the Dudleys—Arthur, I believe—was in the Northern Rhodesian Rites, b,ut the Lieutenant in charge at Kasama told me that he has now got a commission with the Naval Reserve Brigade, who are operating some small gunboats on the lake. It is not expected that any great advance will be made into German East Africa until the Germans are smashed up on the lake. It is a very big business keeping the troops supplied with rations, as they have to go 600 miles by native carriers, and each man possesses a load of ,501 b. Waggons are a very expensive way of doing it. as all the cattle die after about two months, and they cannot get the waggons back to the line. I hear that they are starting motor transports on. the road that we have just come up by, but how they are going to manage it in the wet season I .don't know. The rations we are getting are very good. We expect to be up at -- for about a couple of years."</p>
        <p>Potatoes. Potatoes are better cooked in their skins than peeled, because most of the valuable potash salts are near the skin. Baked in their skins they are also very good, and not spoiled by the cook,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Great Boom in Recruiting.—The boom in re- cruiting that has developed during the past week all over the country has been felt in Stockton Heath. Since last Saturday after- noon over 400 recruits have been attested and placed into their groups. The office at the Church Rooms has been open every night, and on Thursday alone 100 candidates were dealt with. The office will be open again this after- noon, and as it is anticipated that there will be a large rush, it would be well for intending recruits to apply early.</p>
        <p>Monthly Meeting of the Missionary Associa- tion.—The monthly meeting of the St. Thomas' Missionary Association was held in the Parish Room on Monday evening, when an excellent lantern lecture was given by the Rev. J. S. Woolcombe, the organising secretary for the North-West District of the Colonial and Conti- nental Church Society. The Rev. E. W. Clarke was in the chair, and the attendance was such that many people had to be turned away. The lecturer 'spoke of the work of the Society in the war zone, and his remarks were listened to with great attention. At the suggestion of the Rev. E. W. Clarke, he was accorded a hearty vote of thanks.</p>
        <p>Co-operative Society's Concert. —The Warring- ton Equitable Industrial Co-oDerative Society, Ltd., held one of their series of concerts in the Church Rooms on Thursday evening. Mr. W. H. Sherburn. presided over a fair attendance, and at an interval delivered a lecture on the benefits of the Co-operative movement. The programme was contributed to by Miss Vera Jones, "The Songster's Awakening" and "The Little Damozel "; Miss Milly Jones, "Slave Song" and "The Greatest Wish "; Mr. E. Pearce, " Castaway " and "For King and Country "; Mr. A. Dutton recited Our Folks," and gave a humorous selection entitled "Our Vicar," and Mr. W. J. Hopkins sang two humorous songs, both of which were encored The Misses Jones also rendered the duet "Somewhere a Voice is Calling."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>N'.T.C. ANNUAL MEETING.</head>
        <p>N'.T.C. ANNUAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Importance Of Attending Drills.</p>
        <p>The members of the Stockton Heath Detach- ment of the Knutsford Battalion of the Cheshire Volunteer Regiment sat down to a hot- pot supper last Saturday evening, at the Mulberry Tree Hotel, Stockton Heath. Between 40 and 50 members were present and a most enjoyable evening was spent. After the meal, the annual meeting was held, and the Secretary and Treasurer (Mr. H. B. Houghton) presented the accounts, which were passed. The Officer Commanding (Mr. Frank War- burton) impressed upon members the import- ance of attending the drills, pointing out that as it tended =. to discipline, attendance was essential in order that the Corps should take part in the various operations of the coming year.</p>
        <p>A musical evening followed. Items were contributed by the following members of the 'Corps : Messrs. J. W. Bibby, G. Sumner, J. Robertson, E. L. Robinson, H. B. Houghton and A. Shepherd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</head>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing December 13th : Monday. Lecture at 7.30 in the Hall Cafe on "Musketry," by Sergeant Allanson, officer specially detailed, Thursday. Drill at Headquarters, 7.45. Saturday. Uniform Parade and Drill. Fall in ' Victoria-square, 3 p.m. Members who parade without brassards will not be marked as " present " in future. A special squad will be formed for preliminary drill for men who have been attested. Application is invited on any drill night. The Officer Commanding', who is one of the Vice-presidents, recommends members to join the Appleton Rifle Club.</p>
        <p>To fie a reliable shot is one of the main prin- ciples of a soldier, and this can only be attained by constant practice. The annual subscription is gs. from Ist January to 31st December. Shooting at the Appleton Range commences the first Saturday in April and closes 30th September. The winter shooting commences at the Drill Hall the first Friday in October, and closes on 31st March.</p>
        <p>Arrangements can be made with the Secretary, Mr. R. M. Swinton, or Mr. A. Shepherd, for easy payments, weekly, monthly or quarterly, to suit the convenience of intended members. A Christmas Shooting Competition will take Place at the -Drill Hall on Wednesday, 22nd December. commencing at 6 o'clock. Handicap points added to the score. Entrance fee. 3d., about 20 prizes of seasonable kind, which will be presented the following evening. A Sectional Team Match will also take place. By Order. F. WARBITRTON, - Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 12th December. Parades. Tuesday, 14th December (in uniform). Fall in at Headquarters at 7.15 p.m. sharp. Saturday, 18th December (in uniform). Fall in at Headquarters at 3.30 p.m. sharp.</p>
        <p>Musketry. Monday, 13th December. Friday, 17th December. Fall in on each date at the Range, Bank Quay Bridge, at 7.30 p.m. sharp. For list of men required to attend see usual • list at Headquarters. Orderlies. Section-Commander C. G. Hutehings. Private W. E. Worthington. Arrangements have been made for the train- ing of men attested under the Lord Derby Group Scheme, and names of those willing to avail themselves of the opportunity should be sent to the 'O.C., do Mr. C. F. Read, " Glenholme," Victoria-avenue, Grappenhall, its early as pos- sible. Those men who have offered themselves for at- testation, and have been rejected, may be enrolled in the Volunteer Force forthwith, and it is hoped that every one in this district who has been rejected will show his willingness to assist in the defence of his King and country by at once joining the local Detachment. By Order, F. A. FROST, 0.C., (Platoon Commander).</p>
        <p>Headquarters, 11th December, 1915. N.B.—The Platoon Commander, Mr. F. A. Vrost, of Grappenhall Hall; has kindly invited</p>
        <p>the members of the Detachment to a supper at Headquarters on Tuesday evening next, the 14th inst., and they are therefore required to fall in, in uniform, earlier than usual, viz., 7.15 p.m. instead of 7.45 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>PAD GATE.</head>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>No Church Shirkers.—The monthly devo- tional meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday evening, presided over by Mr. Leach. A very interesting address was given by the Rev. W. T. Derry on "Types and Varieties in the Church." He gave some very humorous incidents from his own experience illustrating the types he had met with and said that he had found very curious curiosities in church life, and some very queer people. He asked that there should be no shirkers in the church, and that there should be no social cleavage The men upon whom the church depend, he said, are those who are doing the spiritual work. We live together in the church as co-workers, and it is the duty of everyone to do all they can to help on the cause of Christ. Miss L. Mason was the Soloist and Miss E. Bennett the accom- panist.</p>
        <p>Soldiers' Christmas Parcels.—A very ready response was made by the scholars of the Pad- gate Wesleyan Sunday School to an appeal which had been made to the children asking them to bring gifts, which would be sent to those who had gone from the school to join the forces. The total number who have joined is 17, and some are serting in Prance and Egypt, whilst others are at present training in Eng- land. Among the articles sent were the follow- ing :-50 packets cigarettes, 20 packets chocolate, 20 lots of ointment, 17 bars soap, 16 handker- chiefs, 16 sticks shaving soap, 16 pairs bootlaces, 17 calendars, 6 Radium pocket stoves. 6 unbreak- able mirrors, and various quantities of Oxo cubes, toffee, cakes, biscuits, mince pies, cocoa, condensed milk, sugar, raisins, sardines, salmon, tongue, tinned fruit, notepaper, envelopes. pencils, night lights, safety matches, nuts, socks, mufflers, magazines, &amp;e. A retiring collection taken at the close of the Sunday service to pay the postage on the above realised 305., and the parcels have all been dispatched during the week so that the brave lads may know that they are not forgotten at Christmas time by those who are left behind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>DARESBURY.</head>
        <p>DARESBURY.</p>
        <p>Interment of the Late Mrs. Baguley.—The death took place on Tuesday of last week of Mrs. Ann Baguley, of West Bank, Moore, widow of the late Mr. Clement Baguley. Mrs. Baguley was 69 years of age, and had suffered for many years from paralysis. In the presence of a large number of relatives and friends the funeral took place in the Parish Churchyard, Daresbury, on Saturday afternoon, the Rev. T. T. Lee-Jones conducting the funeral service. Floral tributes were received from "Son and daughter, George and Mary," "Dear brother," Mr. and Mrs. Percy Wright, Halton; Mr. and Mrs. W. de Prez, Mr. W. H. Wright, "Elsie," Mrs. Durrard and family, "K. E. P.," Nurse Booth and Mrs. Done, Mrs. Lunt, and Mrs. J. H. Horton, Mrs. Astbury, Miss Dykes, Belle and Lizzie Adamson,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>NE W TON.</head>
        <p>NE W TON.</p>
        <p>Concert for the Benefit of Wounded Soldiers. —Although the weather conditions were far from satisfactory last Saturday, yet large gather- ings witnessed the afternoon and evening per- formances in the Newton Town Hall, significant of the great interest which is taken in the work at the Garswood Hall Hospital. Both perform- ance's provided some clever, intelligent acting, which reflected great credit upon the promoters. The pianoforte solo, effectively played by Miss E. Gibson, began a very enjoyable programme. Mrs. Jarley's Waxworks, admirably organised by Miss Beatrice Watkins, assisted by Mrs. T. Airey, was a very fine exhibition of models. Miss Smith, The Holmes, Golborne, imperson- ated Mrs. Jarley, humorously introducing the models and remarking upon their virtues or otherwise, which they possessed. Miss Ida Fair- dough very ably took the part of Angelina, and was a pantomime in herself. This was followed by an exceedingly pretty scene, in which the .in- fants of St; Peter's Schools, under Miss Frost's training, splendidly rendered two action songs, "How'd you like to be a baby girl?" and "Babes in the wood." The Farm School Nigger Troupe, under the supervision of Mr. A. E. Simpson, added interest and pleasure to the performance. For the farce, "Transformation," by the Kirk- field pupils, congratulations, must be offered. Their acting—well-trained as they had been-- was of the best in every particular. Miss Alice Naylor, in the role of Joan of Arc, very im- pressively recited a poem bearing that title. The concluding item was the patriotic tableau. a most imposing display by the Kirkfield pupils, which owes its success to the care and attention devoted to it by Miss Hartley and Miss Peck. The evening's performance was augmented by musical items, including a song by Mr. Arthur Davies and a violin solo by Mr. Kersal. both appreciated. To Miss Watkins congratulations are due for her organising abili- ties, which brought to so successful an issue two admirable concerts. It is hoped that the funds for the benefit of our wounded soldiers in the loeal hospital will have been greatly in- creased thereby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>FELT HATS.</head>
        <p>FELT HATS.</p>
        <p>Local Photographers Learn All</p>
        <p>About Them.</p>
        <p>The making of a felt hat. This does not at first sight seem to be a photographic subject, but it is one of the privileges of members be- longing to the societies forming the Lancashire and Cheshire Photographic Union that they are given the services of ladies and gentlemen of all trades and of all shades of opinion, for the mere invitation to their Society's meeting. Mr. Butterworth, a Failsworth, showed in his lecture on Tuesday, before the.members of the Warrington Photographic Society, that he not only knew how to make a felt hat, but also how to describe the manufacture, and how to put the description in a vivid form. He had brought specimens of fur„ the best and the worthless. and the hat itself in all the stages, except the first, where the fur is blown on to the conical forme," before the fibres have become matted together, Or "felted." A Saint "Invented " Felt.</p>
        <p>The first felt hat is supposed to have been worn during the 17th century, although St. Clement was, according to the early writers; the first to make felt, by accident, of course. He was on a pilgrimage, and sitting down foot- sore by a colony of beavers, he managed to kill one. Having plucked its fur, he put the soft hair into his sandals. The heat of his feet, the dampness of the perspiration, and the pressure and movement of his body while walking, pro- vided all the essentials of felt-making, and at the end of his journey he drew out pads of felt that had kept his feet easier for days. One remarkable thing about hat making is the small amount of machine work. The hand, and a very skilful hand it must be, is almost the only tool used in the manufacture of the best felts.</p>
        <p>Mr. Butterworth also illustrated his lecture by placing on the screen photographs of the factory, showing the men at work on the different stages of manufacture. ' The lecture had a further useful element, for Mr. Butter- worth gave the ladies and gentlemen present some valuable hints as to the keeping of felt hats and how to remove grease and stains from them. ' He was heartily thanked at the close.</p>
        <p>Many a .fellow who has nothing to do but mind his .own business doesn't even do that.</p>
        <p>One Woman's Opinion. According to Mrs. Fred T. Dubois, wife of Senator Dubois, of Idaho, the real home maker is the American man, not the woman. Says Mrs. Dubois: "Who pays the rent, who buys the provisions, who sees to it that every need of mother and child is gratified? It's the father and husband. Men, unconsciously—and many of them, too, silently—carry out the kindergarten principle of the male bird story. They build. and forage, while too often the woman &amp;Mande more -and more.'l- -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>LANCE-CORPORAL ROBERT LEIGH.</head>
        <p>LANCE-CORPORAL ROBERT LEIGH.</p>
        <p>Enjoying A Well-Earned Furlough.</p>
        <p>Lance-Corporal Leigh visited his home at Thelwall on Tuesday, having come direct from the firing line. Interviewed by our representa- tive, he stated that he left all the men in the best of health and spirits, all of whom are pre- pared to 'fight on • until the foe is completely vanquished. He explained that when he left them they were away from the trenches doing pioneer work, which is a very pleasant change, with more comforts. Lance-Corporal Leigh,</p>
        <p>- - being a local "Terrier," was mobilised by the corps at the outbreak of war, eventually being drifted to the Front about ten months ago. Whilst serving the colours at the Front he was promoted to the rank of lance-corporal. In civil life he was engaged as a blacksmith at Hollins Green. and worshipped ivhen home at the Parish Church, Thelwall. He returns to the Front on Tuesday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S LIBERAL ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Splendid Rally In Primitive Methodist</p>
        <p>Schoolroom.</p>
        <p>A • public tea and entertainment, at which many people were present, was held in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom on Thursday night, under the auspices of the above Associa- tion. The tea, well catered for and served by Mrs. Edwards and members of the committee, was partaken of by a large number of workers in the district. At 7.30 p.m. Mrs. Gotobed, who occupied the chair, made a few brief remarks, wherein she stated that they were not present at the proceedings from a political point of view, but simply to keep in touch with one an- other and to help in the cause of alleviating the distress in the present crisis. She expressed the hope that the struggle now 'taking place would speedily come to a close. . An entertainment was then given by promi- nent artistes of considerable note from the vil- lage and surrounding districts, which earned appreciative applause. Included in the programme were many pleas- ing vocal efforts, sung in a manner quite in accordance with the reputations of the renderers A humorous playlet entitled "The Burglar" was capitally acted by Mrs. and Miss Priestner, of Lymm, and by special request a patriotic reci- tation was given with fervour by Mr. A. Priest- ner, entitled "Young Williams." At the con- clusion of a _very enjoyable evening a vote of thanks was expressed by Mrs. Gotobed to the friends responsible for such a successful gather- ing, after which a collection was made, on be- half of the local Red Cross Hospital, which realised a substantial amount.</p>
        <p>The entertainment terminated with. the sing- ing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>AFFLICTED SERBIA.</head>
        <p>AFFLICTED SERBIA.</p>
        <p>Effort To Assist To Give Prompt Relief.</p>
        <p>The Rector (the Rev. G. B. Thurston), in the " Lymm Parish Magazine" for December, refers to the dire plight of the -Serbian refugees. A year ago, he says, our hearts and sympathies went ,out to the little country of Belgium in its distress, the first victims of German brutality. Now the turn of Serbia, another small nation, has come and, even com- pared to Belgium, the sufferings of the civil population stand out in bold relief.. We can read in the papers of the horrors of the flight</p>
        <p>of this hapless and brave people, the whole route from PrizSend to• Monastir lined with corpses of human beings and the carcasses of animals dead of starvation, while thousands of old men, women and children are lying on the rocks and in the thiCkets beside the trail, exhausted and without food, awaiting the end. At night the women and children, ill-clad and numb with cold, struggle pitifully about meagre fires of mountain shrub, to resume in the morning the weary march towards some supposed.goal of safety. It is quite. clear that any relief from such a condition as this must be prompt and speedy, and in answer to the urgent appeals of the Serbian Relief Committee. in London, a meeting of ladies was held at the Rectory on Monday last. A tea and sale of useful articles will be held at the Grammar School on Wednesday, December 15th, from 2.30 to 9.30 p.m. Mrs. Stewart • Smith, Lymm Hall, has kindly con- sented to open the sale. Contributions of all kinds will be received gladly either by those responsible for stalls, or at the Rectory. Gar- ments will be sent off at once arid any surplus of money beyond what will be needed for the supply of materials for the working parties. We trust that everyone in the neighbourhood will do what they can to assist :n this scheme of relief.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="0" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>TRADE OF THE FUTURE.</head>
        <p>TRADE OF THE FUTURE.</p>
        <p>Commercial Travellers And The Coming War Against Germany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>ORGANISE AND EDUCATE!</head>
        <p>ORGANISE AND EDUCATE!</p>
        <p>A special meeting, followed by a smoking con- cert, under the auspices of the Warrington and District Branch of the United Kingdom Com- mercial Travellers' Association (Incorporated), was held on Saturday evening at the Lion Hotel, the headquarters of the branch. Mr. W. P. Ashton (chairman and hon. treasurer of the branch) presided in the. absence of Mr. W. P. Rylands, the president, and was supported by Mr. H. Walsh (vice-chairman), Mr. T. Percival (hon. secretary), Mr. P. O'Brien (a past chair- man) and Mr. Fred Coysh (general secretary and Parliamentary agent of the Association, of London). _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. W. P. Rylands, in expressing his regret at being unable to attend owing to absence from home, hoped the meeting would be successful, and a pleasant evening result. Apologies were also received from the Mayor, Sir Gilbert Greenall; Bart., C.V.0., Sir Arthur H. Crosfield, Bart., Mr. J. A. Ransome, Mr. Walter Milner, Sergeant-Instructor T Richardson (a member of the committee and auditor), and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>HOW TO OUST THE ENEMY.</head>
        <p>HOW TO OUST THE ENEMY.</p>
        <p>Meet Them And Beat Them With Their Own Weapons. Mr. Coysh, who was welcomed by a capital attendance of members, spoke at length on the advantages socially and materially, of member- ship in the U.K.C.T.A. lie particularly re- ferred to the effect of the war on commercial travellers and ,to the splendid organisation of German activities and abilities. Was it not possible for us to emulate them or were we to be content to run along in the old haphazard way, and continue to see our trade continually diminishing without making any effort to check it? The Association had endeavoured to initiate various movements for the betterment of the commercial traveller individually, and through his success to further the prosperity of the employer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Learn Languages!</head>
        <p>Learn Languages!</p>
        <p>The question of languages was a most im- portant one, and stress was laid on the fact that' we had a , most inadequate supply of travellers who were qualified to go out to the various countries and deal with the problems locally. to speak the language of the country and to deal with the buyer in his own weights, measures and prices. It was necessary for the employers to consid4r the actual requirements of the various foreign markets, for whilst British' goods gener- ally were invaripbly preferred, it was asking great deal for the foreign buyer to take just what we made without consideration of his local customs and requirements. The Germans had beaten us in this respect, and their travel- lers had been able to go to South America and speak Spanish, to go to China and speag. Chinese, to England speaking English, France speaking French, to Russia speaking Russian, and with his goods fully marked, weighed and measured in the methods of the people with whom he sought to do business. The Associa- tion had provided opportunities for this sort of work to be done, and he appealed to the War- rington members, as elsewhere, to follow this up strenuously. He declared himself •an optimist, fully convinced of the ultimate suc- cess of Great Britain and her Allies in this present world struggle and again urged everyone to equip himself for the commercial• struggle which would occur after the war. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Insurance and Subscriptions.</p>
        <p>Speaking on accident insurance as a part of the Association's work, Mr. Coysh gave details of the necessary organisation, and said com- mercial travellers, whilst being able to do most things , for themselves, might with advantage, avail themselves of the expert knowledge and resources of other people in this direction. As regard uniform subscriptions, Mr. Coysh considered the present subscriptions were quite adequate for the work they had in hand. The great advantage of the Association was that it was always behind any individual member who might be in difficulties. lie gave instances supporting this, which the members could fully appreciate. Travellers and Public Life.</p>
        <p>Dealing with the public work of the Associa- tion, Mr. Coysh pointed out that commercial travellers as a class took less part in public work than any other section of the community, more particularly when their fitness for public work was considered. One seldom heard of a commercial traveller being a member of a Town Council or any other local authority—other people had to decide what he should pay in rates but the Association had been able in many direc- tions to remove grievances a;,-nst which the commercial traveller was justly entitled to complain. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Social and Benevolent Work.</p>
        <p>Speaking on the social work of the Associa- tion, Mr. Coysh referred to the Benefit Society (of which there is a branch in Warrington), and said this was one of the best institutions of its kind in the world—it was well managed, finan- cially sound and every commercial traveller should join it. (Hear,. hear.) The schools and Benevolent Institution were also worthy of every consideration, and he appealed for adolitional and persistent support so as to enable them to be carried on successfully, and so con- tinue the valuable work they 'lad initiated. The schools at Pinner were being extended, and the Warrington Branch had undertaken to endow a cot to be called the "Warrington Cot." He hoped in the near future another cot would be added, as many of the scholars up for election came from Lancashire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Hope of the Future.</head>
        <p>The Hope of the Future.</p>
        <p>Mr. coysh, in conclusion, said there was nothing of which to complain of or to be afraid. We were going ,through a very difficult and strenuous time, but the record of the work done and the benefits now offered should secure the loyalty and continued support of every member through this struggle. Help them to develop the powers of the Association which have been of great advantage to commercial travellers and which, if properly supported in the future, will achieve still greater results. (Hear, hear, and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>WORKERS AND THEIR WORK.</head>
        <p>THE WORKERS AND THEIR WORK.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. O'Brien moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Coysh, and spoke eulogistically of the work of the officials, particularly that of the secre- tary, Mr. T. Percival, who had, he said, evidently missed his vocation—he should have, been a lawyer. (Laughter, and applause.) Mr. O'Brien referred to the work of the legal and special committees, and spoke of the valuable work they had accomplished, work naturally of a delicate nature and consequently not adver- tised.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. G. Hayes seconded, and pointed out the excellent work done by the branch. There were times when members needed all the assi.t- ance the Association could give, and this was generously granted without exception. They had a splendid set of officers, who gave their time and money without demur. The resolution was carried with much hearti- ness, Mr. Coysh briefly replying. Mr. H. Walsh (vice-chairman) proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman (Mr. W. P. Ashton) for presiding, and pointed out the unique offices which were held by him. He was chairman of the branch, chairman of the Benefit Soicety and treasurer of the branch at the same time. Mr. W. G. Hayes seconded, and the resolution was heartily carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>CONCERT.</head>
        <p>CONCERT.</p>
        <p>An enjoyable smoking concert followed, the artistes being Mr. Leonard Ankland, Mr. .T. B. Poole, Mr. J. Rabbitt and Mr. Martindale. Mr. C. Trow was at the piano, and in addition to . accompanying, gave a humorous -musical sketch, which was much enjoyed. The artistes vied with each other in making the concert a success, and the hearty response with musical honours to a vote of thanks, proposed to them by Mr. 11. Mercer (Widnes), seconded by Mr. P. Howard, and responded to by Mr. J. Rabbitt on their behalf, clearly showed their efforts wen-, Much appreciated. • The meeting concluded with the singing of the National Anthem..</p>
        <p>Man was made to mourn, and woman was made to make him.</p>
        <p>A superior manner is one thing; _a uperior verso' is andizer.</p>
        <p>.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Old-Age Pensioners And Cost Of</head>
        <p>Old-Age Pensioners And Cost Of</p>
        <p>Living.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Runcorn Board df Guardians was held at Dutton Workhouse last week, Mr. C. E. Linaker presiding. The Fareha.m Board of Guardians submitted the following resolution for support: "That the Local Government Board be asked to allow Boards of Guardians to grant out relief to old age pensioners without disqualification, on account of the increased cost of living. The Chairman observed that the proper way to deal with this difficulty would be for he Old Age Pensions Committee of the country to petition the Local Government Board to increase the old age pension. The letter was allowed to lie on the table.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>RURAL COUNCIL MEETING.</head>
        <p>RURAL COUNCIL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Antwis presided over a meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council, held last week.</p>
        <p>The suggestion to store the steam roller was again referred to, and Mr. C. E. Linaker said that it was impossible to store the roller for the winter, but the Council intended to use it 'as little as possible. Any roadmen, however, who were wanted for the Army or for farm work, would be released.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>EMPHATIC TALK.</head>
        <p>EMPHATIC TALK.</p>
        <p>The *Kind That Carries Con\ ietion To</p>
        <p>Every Reader.</p>
        <p>Conviction must follow such emphatic proof as this. Here is an Earlestown case. Read it and see if doubt can exist in the face of this evidence. - _</p>
        <p>On November 14th, 1913, Mrs. E. Holden, of 186, Crow-lane West, Eltrlestown, said: " believe it was a cold which brought on the sharp pains in my back. I began to suffer irith these pains after my last confinement. There was a continual ache right across my kidneys, especially when I stooped. I knew my kidneys were out of order by other signs. "I was not feeling at all well, so tried Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, and after taking these pills for a few days they drove away all the pain and made me quite well again. I can speak highly of Doan's Pills, for they acted first- class in my ease. (Signed) E. Holden."</p>
        <p>_ On March 3rd, 1915—orer twelve months late Mrs. Holden said: " I still Ifeep very well In.. Doan's Pills did me lasting good." Motherhood bring life's sweetest joys—but great dangers, too, for it is a strain on the kidneys, and even with every care, few mothers entirely escape dropsy, rheumatic pains, a weak back, or some bladder weakness. It is neglect of these symptoms that leads to so much suffer- ing in later years. Doan's Backache KidneY Pills are especially for weakness of the kidneys and bladder, and gently lead the kidneys back to health and activity. They should be uses without delay on the appearance of any such clear signs of kidney weakness as backache. rheumatism, dropsy, sediment, gravel, and irregularities of the bladder. Price 2s. 9d: a box; of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford- Street, London, W. Don't ask for backache or kidney pills—ask distinctly for Dpan's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mrs. Holden had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>FARMERS' HELP FOR ALLIES.</head>
        <p>FARMERS' HELP FOR ALLIES.</p>
        <p>Lancashire And Cheshire Lead The \\ ith Relief Fund.</p>
        <p>The farmers of Cheshire and Lancashire still hold the record in the matter of county con- tributions to the Agricultural Relief of Allies Fund. Between them the two counties have given about £6,000 for assisting the farmers of allied countries whose homesteads lay in the path of the Hun. Northamptonshire comes next with £1,828, then follows Cambridgeshire with £1,696, Kent with £1,518, Notts with £1,274, and Yorkshire is sixth with £l,OOO. The jumble sales of Lancashire and Cheshire .are now being imitated in other parts of the country. At Ashford (Kent) over £l,OOO has been raised by this means.</p>
        <p>Indigestion and "Nerves"</p>
        <p>Extremely Severe Case Cured by Dr. Cassell's Tablets.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Holmes. of 87 Bolton Brow; r3owerb: Bridge. says: "1 hae got into a low, run. down state, with m 'life' in me, am though I bad reedlea treatment I only g( more depressed at neurasthenic. No fof agreed with me; wh:. ever I ate oauee, wind and palpitatio: and the splitting headaches I endured were really agonising at times. I got no sleep at night, and / so nervous that I dreaded to be left alone. "I had soffered for over a year when I gt.. 4 Dr. Geesell's Tablets. Soo= after I began to feel brighter I could sleep at night.. and I grew stronger and better daily. All the bead aches and indigestion left me. sad precentor I found myself well and strong."</p>
        <p>; to . ;Ari. • -• 1 TEL ic-f,l/4 • ,4": ii(i e-r" Ks! • • at- • . - 1g Mrs, Holmes. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Dr.Ca 'sells Tablets.</head>
        <p>Dr.Ca 'sells Tablets.</p>
        <p>Dr. Cassell's Tablets are a genuine and tested 7:smedy for all forms of nerve or bodily weakness in old or young. Compounded of nerve-nutrients and tonics of indisputably proved efficacy. they are the recosnised modern home treatment for NERVOUS BREAKDOWN KIDNEY DISEASE . NERVE PARALYSIS INDIGESTION SPINAL PARALYSIS STOMACH DISORDER INFANTILE PARALYSIS MAL-NUTRITION NEURASTHENIA WASTING DISEASES NERVOUS DEBILITY PALPITATION SLEEPLESSNESS VITAL EXHAUSTION ANEMIA PREMATURE DECAY</p>
        <p>Specially valuable for Nursing Mothers, and dur- ing the Critical Periods of Life. Chemists and stores in 211 parts of the world sell Dr. Cassell's Tablets. Prices: 11-, 13, and 3/-, the 3/- size being the most economical. A Free Trial Supply will be sent to you on receipt of name and address and two penny stamps for postag• sae packing. Address: Dr. Cassell's Co., Ltd., 41.8. Chester-road, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>INFANTILE MORTALITY.</head>
        <p>INFANTILE MORTALITY.</p>
        <p>Warrington As A Pioneer In Progressive Work.</p>
        <p>COMPARISON WITH SOUTH-</p>
        <p>PORT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph made a flattering reference to filo work of the Warrington Health Committee, at the Town Council meeting on -Tuesday. • The committee reported that at a meeting on November 16th, a letter was read from the Secretary of the Trades and Labour Council with respect to the Notification of Births (Ex- tension) Act, 1915, and the Town Clerk was in. struCted to reply to it. Dr. :JOSEPH, in the absence through illness of Alderman James Evans, chairman of the com- mittee, explained that the letter in question enclosed a memorandum on the welfare of mothers and infants, and drew attention to the fact that the Notification of Births Act was now compulsory throughout the country. "No words of mine are needed," said Dr. Joseph, "to emPhasise the extreme importance- of this sub- ject.- We hear a great deal at present about the need for a -high .birth-rate, but the impor- tant point is not the mere number of children born but the number that are xnised to healthy adult life. As it does' not appear ,to be fully known to the public what we are doing with regard. to this branch of our work, I think it is approPriate for me to givn you a few details. Now in the first place although the Notification of Births Act was only made compulsory throughout • the 'country in September of this year, I might remind you that we adopted it in this town more than five years previously. In our administration of the Act we have ap- pointed certain qualified nurses as health visi- tors, who, as soon as possible, visit in the homes and where necessary give advice with regard to the health both of the mother and infant. This, of course, only occurs where no doctor is in at- tendance. At first only the infants under one year of age were dealt With, but two years ago we appointed a third health visitor in order that • we might exercise supervision over chil- dren between 12 months and five years of age— that is until they. began to attend school and come under the notice ..of the School Medical Officer. I would like to mention that. in a recent article on the 'subject Published only a few -weeks ago, which some of you may .have seen, it is stated that thisnspect of the question *the care of. children, between the ages of one and. five years—is neglected . all over the country. This you will see is not a fact, for we have dealt with. it for the past two-years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>instruction For Mothers,</head>
        <p>instruction For Mothers,</p>
        <p>a Now• for a further branch of the work. We have lit this town tWo centres, one in Catherine- street, Bewsey, and the other in Academy,street. where mothers can attend with their children fOr inStraCtion and "advice. These centres, which are -doing excellent work, were in- angnrated and carried on. by the, ladies of the Gnild Of Help' in close 'Co-operation with our health department, and our health visitors at- td and gh'o•O short coarse of lectures. ''With regard to the amount of work being done;-1 will only say that during last year alone more than 10,000 hoihe visits were paid by the heath visitors. in connection with the welfare of Mothers and infants, irrespective of the work at the' Welcomes. In a special report issued by the 'Local Government Board last year a com- parison is made between the average rates of infantile mortality for each county borough in Lateashire in•tbe years 1902 to 1904, and in 1911 to 1913. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>Second to Southport.</head>
        <p>Second to Southport.</p>
        <p>." It is there shown that Southport is the only 016 out, of the 17 county boroughs that has a greater reduction in the death-rate during that period than Warrington. (Hear, hear.) Fur- ther, .of the same 17 county boroughs there is otilY'-one town with so high a birth-rate that has so low ~an infantile death-rate. I don't want to worry the CoUncil with a mass of figures, so for further details I must refer -yott to the annual reports fox • recent .years of the Medical Officer of :Health. After studying these statisticoi I fed sure that you will all agree with Inc that the improvement there shown in pur infantile death- rate is in some measure at any rate due to the - efforts we have Inifde in dealing with the sub- ject under our administration of the Notifica- tion of Births Act. A scheme for the establish- ment of a fully-equipped maternity centre was under consideration before the outbreak of the war, and although we are not in a position to carry this project out at once, it is hoped to complete it in the future.- That the urgency for the provision of this centre is not quite as great as it was some tithe ago we owe our best thanks to several different agencies, all 'co-operating in this time-of stress. Thanks For Warrington Doctors. ,</p>
        <p>" We have to thank the medical practitioners in the town who are giving such a large amount of . free medical advice and ' treatment to dependents of soldiers. .We have to thank the Guild of Help for the energetic work of their members; the. Soldiers and .Sailors Relief Corn-</p>
        <p>, -mittee, and other voluntary organisations, and</p>
        <p>'llion we haveithe generous allowances made by c..ertain* firms in this town to the families of employes who have enlisted. But don't think for moment that we are now resting satis- fied with the result of our past labours. This question of the health of the mothers and in- fants and the young Children of the town is continuously receiving our attention, and should it appear necessary we would not hesi- tate to bring forward any scheme, whatever ex- pense was involved if it was essential to their welfare." (Hear, hear.) ,Pioneers of Progress.</p>
        <p>Dr. Joseph went on :to say that Warrington had, in social and health matters, been pioneers in reference to questions of reform and progress. It. was said that " a prophet is not without honour save in his own country," and he .could pot help thinking 'that they were in that posi.</p>
        <p>._. . - n at the present moment. Warrington was Iknown—some municipalities acknowledged it— s having been pioneers in 'various movements lor the welfare of the people, and he was, there- sore, pleased to have been able to make his ,statemont to. the Council. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
