      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>GIFTS TO THE POOR.</head>
        <p>GIFTS TO THE POOR.</p>
        <p>£3,000 For Warrington ; £1,600 For The Infirmary.</p>
        <p>A HAPPY SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>As was reported in "The Examiner" last Saturday, an amicable arrangement has been , come to in the matter of the Kitchingman ' bequest, under °which the testator, the late Mr. Joseph Kitchingman, a solicitor, of 'Liver- pool, with 'Warrington associations, left his residence, Seabank Nook, Liscard, to Warring- ton, to be used as a convalescent home for 'our- poor, and 1;1,000 to the Warrington -Infirmary for the endowment of a bed to the memory of his mother. In the event of difficulties arising as to the use of the house for the , purpose stated, it was to be sold and the proceeds divided between the Victoria Hospital, Likard, and the Warrington Infirmary. As a matter of fact, difficulties did arise as to the .use of the honk as a convalescent home, and the question arose as, to the amount to which the Corporation were entitled in respect of the sum left for the maintenance of the house for the benefit' of the poor of Warrington. The Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), who had been largely instrumental in causing Mr. Kitchingman to look favourably upon War- rington when he was making, his will, because of the considerate treatment shown to him in the purchase of Buttermarket-street property for street-widening purposes, has again been largely instrumental in bringing about a most satisfactory compromise. Under this compromise the Corporation abandon the house, but are awarded .C 3,000 and the Infirmary take their £l,OOO plus .2600, half the amount • realised for the house. The income from the £3,000 will be invested and the interest devoted to sending poor convales- cents not to Liscard, but to any home selected in any part- of the country. The money comes in each case free of duty and with interest added. at the rate of 4 per cent. from the date of the testator's death. • Mr. Justice l'eterson, who in the Chancery Division last week ratified the arrangement, expressed his satisfaction with the happy end- ing to the litigation..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>AN EXAMPLE TO OTHERS.</head>
        <p>AN EXAMPLE TO OTHERS.</p>
        <p>The Matter wa's referred to at the Town Coun- cil meeting on Tuesday evening by the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock). The result, said his Worship, was that Warrington had been very fortunate and had done very well out of it. He honed the .poor people of Warrington would benefit considerably by the bequest.. (Hear, hear.) Alderman Bennett expressed the opinion that the committee had made a good bargain in the matter. He did not know if Mr. Kitchingman was horn at Warrington, but, at any rate, his mother was. He wished More .of his fellow- townsmen, whether during their life-time or at their death, would think about the needs of the town in this manner. (Hear, hear) Councillor Wilkinson explained that the ob- ject of the committee was to invest the money and the. income would be devoted, if not to the upkeep of a home at Liscard, to the sending of the poor people of Warrington to the various convalescent homes. They felt that in doing this the money would go further and at the same time fulfil the spirit of what 3.1 r. Kitch- ingman desired.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>OUR FINANCES.</head>
        <p>OUR FINANCES.</p>
        <p>Public Services And " Relief Of The Rates."</p>
        <p>MOTOR 'BUS DEFICIT.</p>
        <p>The accounts of the Borough of Warrington for the period ended March 31st, were audited by Mr. F. Lambert Stevens, the district auditor, and his report has been submitted to the Town Counci 1.</p>
        <p>lie states that at the beginning of the year the aggregate balance in hand was £89,802 14s. 9d., and during the year £371,239 14s. 2d. -hail been received. The sum expended during the year was £393,408 9s. 1&amp;., and net balance in hand at March 31st, 1915, was £67,633 19s. 10d. The amount of loans outstanding at the end of the year was £1,314,499 11s. 4d., in respect of which £3,346 Os. sd. was set aside As sinking fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>CONTRIBUTIONS IN AID OF RATES.</head>
        <p>CONTRIBUTIONS IN AID OF RATES.</p>
        <p>The following contributions in aid of rates have been matte by the undarmentioned under- takings: viz.:—</p>
        <p>2 S. d. Tramways ' 2,500 0 0 Water</p>
        <p>Gas Electricity leaving the following unappropriated balances after making due provision for loan charges, viz.:—</p>
        <p>s. d. Tramas'ays Water Gas 24,995 10 5 Electricity</p>
        <p>On motor omnibus account there was a deficit of £217 19s. 6d., which I understand will be charged to the rate account in the current year. Expenditure amounting to £2,023 7s. has been charged to the Tramways Reserve Fund, which has reduced the balance standing to the credit of that fund to £7,976 13s.</p>
        <p>The rest of the reserve funds were as follows viz.:--</p>
        <p>d. s. Water Gas • .Electricity 9;368 2 5</p>
        <p>••••:•••:••••••••:•••••••••:•••: • • • Children' 8 Corner.i • • v • • * Conducted by UNCLE JACK. + t44.4-,:•**•:••:.4.40:••• *...+4.44 4</p>
        <p>Children, Join This Corner! Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best! Always Be Glad! . Uncle Jack, " Examiner," Warrington,</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS IS COMING.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,-</p>
        <p>A fortnight to-day Christmas Day will be here, and we shall be celebrating the greatest festival in the Christian year. Fourteen days and the time which is hallowed by every con- ceivable association that has good in it will be your and mine to enjoy, accOrding to our own particulaf opportunities. I know you will all be counting the, days as they fly past.. Now it is fourteen more days to Christmas; to-morrow "Thirteen more days!" will be the, cry. Then twelve, eleven, and so on down to one, and Christmas Eve will, have arrived with all the exciting expectations aml anticinations con- nected with the visit of Santa Claus. I know that you will revel in looking forward to this happy time, that you will cheer the passing of Time to bring Christmas to you. It is part of YOU! happiness, as children, that you can with light' heart desire to have Time pass quickly along. Soule day yol will be as old as your Uncle Jack, or Uncle Ned, who, however is still young enough and patriotic enough to go to fight for his country ,instead of writing nice stories and nicer poetry for you. When that time comes you will not wish for Time to be in a very great hurry, even when Christmas is coming. Still such considerations may lead us to sad thoughts, and I have no intention of making my numerous ,nephews and nieces' Sod, especially of this season of the year.</p>
        <p>The Next Competition.</p>
        <p>On the contrary, I want to add to yo ttr happi-</p>
        <p>ness; and I will try to do so in a way that will please you all. We will have a grand Christmas competititin in order to find out how 'you would like to spend the coming holidays. • There will be a good book prize for the boy or girl who will write me the best letter on</p>
        <p>" How I Can Be Happy At Christmas." • I am in the habit of getting very many letters now from my young people, who say certain things; bpt do not give their reasons for saying them. I like people to give their reasons for what they say, and I should like to know yours. Please, send your letters so 'as to reach .me by Tuesday at the latest; addressed:— "Uncle Jack," ".Examiner." Office, :Warrington.</p>
        <p>Your loving, UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>P.S.—My friend the Editor 'tells me that in another part of to-day's " Examiner " there is a special article telling all about Quistmas, its customs, its mistletoe, its Christmas trees and, above all, its Santa Claus, who is really St. Nicholas. You can say "St. Nicholas, "St.. Nicholas," over and over again, until it sounds like " Santa Claus." That is how people made it into " Santa Clans " by saying it fov centuries! Try it! The aiticle has been written by a gentleman on the Editor's staff, ever so much cleverer than your old Uncle Jack, but I should like you to read it. You will then know more than you do of what Christmas is and what Christmas means. I shall read it and I hope you will advise Your fathers and ,mothers and even your other uncles to do so.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>PUBLIC LIGHTING.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC LIGHTING.</p>
        <p>The amount transferred to the Gas Depart- ment for public lighting was £5,536 7s. 7d., of which £2,069 10s. 54. was in respect. of expendi- ture on maintenance and repairs Of _lamps, and wages for street lighting, and the remainder, £3,466 17s. 2d. is the amount paid for gas. On the basis of an estimated consumption of 31,166,736 cubic feet, at is. 11d, per thousand, this gives a net profit of £4BO, in addition to whatever in the natUre of profit. is 'included in the price of is. lid. per thousand cubic feet (the price charged to large consumers).</p>
        <p>The amount transferred to the Electricity Department for the like purpose was £649 9s. Id., in respect of which £l3B M. 7d. was expended on maintenance and •attendance, leaving £5lO 19s. 9d. as the amount paid for current. The number of units 'supplied is stated to be 79,171, which works Out to a rate of 1.5 d. per unit. :Referring to the contribution paid through the Institute of Gas Engineers to the British Commercial Gas. Association, the amount this year is kBB 12s. 6(1., which is at the rate of 2s. (A. per million eubi e feet of gas manufac- tured. After hearing- your Gas Manager's ex- planations of the services rendered, I do not consider the'cluirge excessive. The net profit on the gas undertaking was only 0,988 19s. 2d., it§ against £8,619 2s. 2d. in the previous year, the receipts having_ decreased and the expenditure increased.</p>
        <p>The accounts have been neatly and accurately kept, end your officers have rendered me every assistance; and readily compiced with my .requirements.</p>
        <p>The successful liar must hive a perfect Con- fidence in his own imagination.</p>
        <p>The same force that moves a ton along a high- way will pull a 32-ton ,canal boat.</p>
        <p>If qome men didn't boast t.f their hattesty the world would never know they had any.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
