      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>+411•4400.4.. 4.46 4:4 0.144,04+0040 4,044•4414k4C044 +41,4404104,...40,40404+4+40 +4144 +4o+ 04. *444 44. 041 off. 44•448.0 90,0+ 4**,1.4 ++ 411</head>
        <p>+411•4400.4.. 4.46 4:4 0.144,04+0040 4,044•4414k4C044 +41,4404104,...40,40404+4+40 +4144 +4o+ 04. *444 44. 041 off. 44•448.0 90,0+ 4**,1.4 ++ 411</p>
        <p>W • MAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>short* join the Colours. It very wisely called in the aid of several highly-educated and com- petent women, who drew up schemes for train- ing girls in a number of the skilled London industries. The result- is that women have now a chance of learning the better paid trades, and a new chapter of industry is beginning. Courses for women and girls are given at the Borough Polytechnic in the mechanism of the motor ear. Superintended by a skilled motor car engineer, the pupils learn to repair and, clean the machinery, to take it to pieces, and .to recon- struct it. The lessons- are as perfect as is .pos- sible to make, them, and are given in a motor engineering_ workshop. . There are also " work- shop " courses, where women can be prepared to take up skilled employment in the metal and wood industries. Another course teaches women and girls to do all the odd jobs about a house,' and at the Grocers! Trade Schools many; girls have prepared themselves to serve in grocers' ,shops. British Toys. In -order to arouse interest in British Arts' and Handicrafts, a most successful exhibition was lately held at the ' Central 'Hall. minster. Everything with the exception of the , various collections, lent by their owners, had been designed by British brains,: and made by British hands. Beautiful sculpture, enamel work, stained glass, Woodcarving, leather work, needlework, woven. goods, pottery and other crafts were shown. Chinese lacquering, silver ' work and jewellery of all kinds also, found ft place in the exhibition. As regards toys, these should be something that children .can play with at all times, and not be too, costly. for every day use. The Germans seem to have known instinctively the kind of toys that give delight to their possessors. These, toys were by no means beautiful, but they gave pleasure, be- cause they appealed to the childish imaginatim, Moreover, they were cheap. Made by, and for the million, a good profit was secured, and the Central Empires gradually captured the toy markets of the world. British designers—many of them are women—and Makers, must work on sueh lines if they are • to capture the home market. The Overbodice.</p>
        <p>A very pretty blouse, suitable for soft woollen materials, had its sleeves Ciit to form a rather deep yoke at the neck, and a shallow one in front. The turnover velvet collar ended in deep points; and from each Of them depended a silk tassel, which reached the bust. The cuffs of this winter's blouses , are as various in shape and size as the collars, and give a dainty finish to the long sleeves that still reign supreme.</p>
        <p>Apropos of new collars for 'blouses, a novelty of .last week bears a strong resemblance to an aeroplane, after which it is named. Made of embroidered .white lawn, it stands away from the neck in two sharply tapering ends, whose correct' position is just beneath the ears. The front of this blouse is arranged also to resemble as nearly as possible a flying machine. A new feature of this season is the overbodice, a very practical and economical idea. It assumes many shapes, and may • have -a basque, or be drawn in to a shaped band at the waist. These over. bodices give _warmth to a flimsy blouse, and when made of brocade, silk, velvet, or satin. are suitable for smart occasions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>A British Canteen in Paris.</p>
        <p>The British canteen at the Gare du Nor. Station in Paris is one of the most useful of war institutions ,in that city. The Women's Emergency Corps founded it in February. it is worked by ten English ladies, each -of whom contribute to the expenses, and they are on duty in three shifts during the twenty-four hours. An immense cellar placed at their d,s posal has been converted into a warm and pleasant club room, capable of serving as dormi- tory, dining and sitting-room, kitchen and storeroom. Hundreds of soldiers and sailors, British, French and Belgian, use it daily, and both they and their officers , appreciate thoroughly what is done for them. This con- sists of preparing and serving a mid-day meal for nearly three hundred men, distributing hot coffee, fruit and cigarettes to the wounded who arrive in hospital trains, and preparing and serving tea and supper to even a greater num- ber. Men on their way to and from the Front sleep in the .canteen, and always find a hot breakfast ready at whatever hour they may have to Wave.</p>
        <p>Comforts for French Soldiers. The women of Paris of all classes are busily engaged in organised work for their menfolk in the trenches. The whole feminine community has in fact been mobilised, and vast. stores of garments and comforts have been dispatched to the brave soldiers who keep up a ceaseless fight with a merciless foe, despite the cold and damp of the northern winter. The American Colony in Paris adds its quota of help, and its feminine members are as tireless in their efforts as the Frenchwomen themselves. Hundreds of crates filled with warm winter clothing have been dis- patched to the Front by American ladies. Women fill nearly, if not quite, all 4 the public departments of the city of Paris. It is gratify- ing to the sex to find that postal women prove thoroughly satisfactory, and that they make efficient public servants. The same may be said of all the public • services now staffed almost entirely by women. . •. Women and the L.C. Council. • .</p>
        <p>The London County Council has not lagged behind in recognising the importance of train- ing women to take the place of men who will</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>HOME NOTES. Profitable Recipes And Useful Suggestions.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES. Profitable Recipes And Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>Fish Balls.</p>
        <p>Prepare a pint of cola salt fish, after it is freshened and boiled, and chop, it fine. Have some fresh-boiled potatoes, mashed- line, a cup- ful of broken butter, and two even teaspoon- fuls of 'made mustard, with salt to taste.' Beat up an egg lightly, and mix these last-mentioned ingredients, adding a little cream or sweet milk; then add the chopped 1!..41, mixing all thoroughly together. Make into balls on a floured board, and fry in boiling lard like dough nuts. When uniformly browned, remove from the fat with a skimmer, drain, ,put on a hot dish, and serve. Carefully prepared, these fish balls are exceedingly appetising • and nutritious. • Economical Cooking.</p>
        <p>The "hay box" for cooking is comparatively well-known, but it is pointed out that discarded newspapers are as useful as hay and cheaper. A demonstration, of economical cookery waS given lately in London by Miss Florence Petty, who with the aid of a box closely packed with newspapers and used on the " hay box " prin- ciple cooked several nourishing and appetizing dishes at the cost of a few pence. It is essential that the newspapers should be tightly packed around the saucepan, which should then be covered with . a pillow-case stuffed with paper and the whole shut in by a closely- fitting lid. Some potatoes, after the saucepan containing them had been confined to the box for an hour, were found to be nicely cooked. Good Winter Soups.</p>
        <p>Among the delusions of the cook is the idea that it is impossible to make good and tasty soup without flesh meat, . says " Farm and Home." Delicious soups can be made without meat, and when so made the flavour of the vege- tables is preserved and the soups are improved. In a town there may be sqme difficulty in obtain- ing quite fresh vegetables:, but in the country this should not be so. The farmer's garden, to help in this way, should always be well looked after. It need not be large, because one can see very goo&amp; results even in cottage gardens, but it should oacontain the ordinary green vege- tables now so good, especially cabbages and kales, which Stand . any weather. , Leeks also should be grown, and the turnip-rooted celery, which wants no blanching, is much easier to grow .than the ordinary celery, and likes best a moist spot. A few herbs should also be grown; these are best in a.dry border, where the mistress can gather them for herself. Shalots and savory, parsley, tarragon, chives, and a few other, herbs should be omitted, for on them depends the right flavouring of soups and stews. In all gardens one must study the nature of the soil. Carrots are very useful, but it is hopeless to try to grow them on cloddy ground. Leek Soup. Shread finely 2 leeks, half 'head of celery, 1 small bunch herbs, blanch in boiling water, then strain off and let the cold water tap run on them for some minutes, then drain on a cloth. Crush I.teacupful tapioca and soak it for 1 hour, add to .1 quart of boiling vegetable stock', boil</p>
        <p>gently for 20 minutes, then add vegetables, and boil 30 minutes, season to taste, and add 1 gill cream or piece of butter, .serve croutons.</p>
        <p>• Tomato and Barley Soup. 21b. of fresh tomatoes, 1 teacup of pearl barley, lioz. of butter, 6oz. of onions, 2 quarts of water, a little salt. Brown the butter, put in the onions; cut into small pieces and fry till tender, pour on boiling water; add tomatoes (Akinned by scalding them) and barley, and stew 4 hours; strain if desired. Some milk added a few minutes before serving improves all sorts of vegetable soups. Potato Soup.</p>
        <p>Take four moderate-sized potatoes, boil and mash theni; 1 large onion, a small, head of celery. Cut up the onion and celery and boil in milk, when done add the potatoes. Pass all through a hair sieve; season with white pepper and salt; bring it to the 'ordinary thickness of a thick soup with milk if necessary. Serve. hot. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>• Maybe the old-fa Aimed rawhides didn't educate boys 80 well, but it trained them better.</p>
        <p>" Married life is frequently unhappy because a woman can't always distinguish betwepn a man's peace of mind and a piece-of hers."</p>
        <p>Our idea of a wise man, says an American paper, is one who isn't foolish enough to try to convince a woman by arguing with her. .</p>
        <p>Super-Angelic. George 11. Lorimer, the author of The Letters of a Self-made' Merchant to his Son, is no friend, as his writings show, to the high-flown. -A practical man, Mr. Lorimer .stakes -his all upon practical things. Discussing social veoeer at a a mid-Lenten debate in, PhiladeliThia, Mr. Lorimer said that in the education of girls the practical did not receive Sufficient prominence. "A young friend of mine in Cynwyd," Said the shrewd humorist, "got married last month. He said to me the other day: " ' When I married Mamie I thought she was an angel. But,' he. added, I soon found out my mistake.'</p>
        <p>Disappointed?' I asked. " Disappointed? Nit! he cried. .1 I found she was a.good cook.'" ; •</p>
        <p>The Proud Widow. 1 " That famous editor and statesman, Charles Emory Smith," said a Philadelphian journalist, "was a modest man. He believed in modesty— even in journalism. He thought it paid no better for a newspaper than for a man con- tinually to be bragging. I (nice drew up a prospectus for him. There were several blatantly boastful paragraphs in it, and Mr. Smith ran his pencil through them all. • " If I let this go,' he said, it would be pretty nearly as bad as the epitaph that the young widow carved on her aged husband's tomb. This epitaph said: "'Sacred to the memory of John James Greer, aged 84, who departed this life bitterly regretting that he must leave fOr ever the most beautiful and best of wives.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>FLAGS AND BANNERS.</head>
        <p>FLAGS AND BANNERS.</p>
        <p>I know you will all be interested ni Bags and banners. They are said to be as.old as the earliest contests of rival tribias or nations. It. would be natural for each body 'of combatants td bear sonic distinctive .mark as a precaution against being mistaken by their fellows for the enemy, and a flag o'r banner which could.be seen above the heads of the column it was borne</p>
        <p>before would soon he recognised as the best mode of distinguishing friends from 'foes, The Roman legions-. bore the device of art eagle, and that royal bird is borne 'as the' flags of Russia and Austria, because the sovereigns of both empires claim to be the successors of the Ctesars, as indicated in the titles of Kaiser and Czar. The two-headed eagle was first borne by, , the Emperor Constantine as a sign of his sovereignty of the Eastern and Western Empires. In mediteval times it was assumed by Charles the Great. A lion • was emblazoned on the ensigns of the Jews wheh they were an indepen- dent-nation, and the same' animal has figured in the Royal Standards of 'Persia, England, Scotland, the Venetian Republic in the 'Middle Ages, aid other Powers. The Norman kings of Erigland bore two lions on the 'flags, a third beitig added by Henry 11. A lion rampant. that is, Tearing upon his hind legs, was the device of the kings of Scotland. The letters S.P.Q.R., which appear in representations of Roman-civic processions, are the initials of Senatus ponelus quo Domanau—the Senate of the people of Rome. ,</p>
        <p>The tricolour of France; as adopted by the National Assembly •in 1789, was formed by the combination-of the colours of the city of Paris, which were blue and • red, with that of the monarchs, as represented in the white flag of St. Louis. At the beginning of .the Revolution the Constitutionalists adopted a green rosette. as the emblem of their principles; but this was soon superseded by one formed of the colours of the municipality of Paris. On its being pointed out by Lafayette that these ,were also the colours of the Duke of Orleans white was added and the tricolour thus formed bectime in, 1703 the national flag. Policy dictated its retention by reason of its association with many great vic- tories when the empire was proclaimed, the only change -being the placing of N surmounted by an Imperial crown in the centre of the white. Louis XVIII, restored the white flag, but after the Revolution of 1830 it was again replaced by the ,tricolour, which has remained the national flag to .the present .time. The Belgian flag is the same with the difference that the colours are arranged horizontally. The Italian tri- colour is red, white and green, arranged as in the French flag, with the. eros of Savoy in the centre 'of the' white. The thrnuin tricolour ie black, yellow and red.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>Convertible Dress and Coat.</head>
        <p>The Convertible Dress and Coat.</p>
        <p>An idea that will appeal to the girl engaged in some busy occupation of the day, and to whom time is of importance, is the coat and skirt of velveteen, that may be converted into a smart afternoon frock; simply by removing, the eoat. The skirt is made partly of ninoii, or some, soft thin silk, and is given a deep hem of the velveteen: This is entirely hidden by the coat. The corsage is generally a Meuse of the ninon or silk with an overbodice. of velvet to match the skirt. Many such combinations may be devised in, other materials if preferred. A coat that 'serves the double purpose of a street and an evening wrap is among the economies of this winter. ' Fortunately for the woman of, limited means elaborate evening wraps are now the exception rather than the rule, and the com- bined coat is not a difficult economy to achieve- A shade of cloth, wool velours, or faced cloth, should be chosen that whilst not too light for day wear is yet suitable for the evening.</p>
        <p>The New Millinery. The Directoire high-crowned hat is more be- coming than the high-crowned postilion, or topper, which really suits only the few. The newest winter hats are decidenly coquettish, anti all are worn well forward. Only in a Very few instances is a hat placed at an angle, and then always a *light one. The small hat has many rivals, but it will be in the forefront of fashion throughout this winter. The turban is very much in evidence. Sometimes it is adorned, or disfigured, by a very high quill springing from from A rosette of velvet. These hats play be worn at a .slight angle. Some charming and some startling new shapes tave their origin in Paris. One of these looks altogether modern yet it. was worn in France by ,gentlemen who flourished during the 16th century. To be com- plete it should . be accompanied by a frilled outstanding collarette of velvet or faille to match the hat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Thinking in Bed. . When . Harriman, the . great • railroad king, died, someone said that thinkrug in bed was the cause of his death; but this is Edison's ex- planation: "Why did Harriman think in bed? Because he ate too much. 1 eat one-fourth as much as. Harriman. did. The result is that 1 am asleep thirty seconds after my head hits the pillow. Harriman over-ate, and by over-eating poisoned his lower intestines."—" The Life- boat." • Causes of Flatulence.</p>
        <p>Flatulence: is caused by gas, arising from the fermentation or putrefaction of food in the in- te§tines, i.e., it is a form of indigestion. Some of tlie best-known causes are; 0) Rapid eating; (2) eating too soon after severe exertion, when the system is too tired to cope with the work;, (3) bad combinations of foods; (4) going too long without. food; (5) overeating, resulting in dis- tension of the intestines; and (6) chills. coarse turnips, cabbage and cauliflowers are vege- tables that, lead to this trouble unless .young, well chosen and well cooked. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>For Those Who Sit.</head>
        <p>For Those Who Sit.</p>
        <p>The Health Department of New York City has prepared ...rules for clerks, book-keepers, stenographers, and others whose work is in- doors and of a sedentary nature. We quote a feW of these rules:—</p>
        <p>Constantly keep the windows open. .Don't ruin your eyes. Watch for the early signs. of eye strain.</p>
        <p>_ . _ Spend part of the lunch period outdoors. Bracers are harmful—this applies to the use of tea, coffee, and alcoholic beverages. Study your job. Offen the unhealthy posi- tion is merely a faulty One mistimed by you. You shottld pay careful attention to fresh air, correct position, •proper and AuffiCient lighting, rest and recreation, and finally to food and drink.</p>
        <p>Doctoring. " Your stomach needs ,a long vacation," re- marked the cultured Doctor Tubb. " It's had no rest or recreation since first you learned to swalloiv grub. Day after day you .overload it, into its maw the pies you pour; it needs the rest you have long owed it, so you must fast ten days or more." "Go to," I said, while strong emotions disturbed my soul; "methinks you're dense; I -called you in for pills and 'potions—l do not pay for common sense. Can't you pre- scribe a jug of bitters to cure -me of my ills and woes, and let me have my pies and fritters, and sirloin steaks and things like those P" But, with a sternness truly Roman, the doctor says, "That. will not go; an outing for your tired abdomen—that is the only cure I know." There is no comfort now in sickness, since doctors have so honest grown; they say, "if you'd get well with quickness, you'll have to leave the pies alone; you'll have to diet like the dickenit, each day ten miles you'll have to walk, and go to slumber with the chickens, and leave your bed at four o'clock."—Walt Mason, in "Farm Journal."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>aljurrij itiL</head>
        <p>aljurrij itiL</p>
        <p>CHRIST CHURCH SALE.</p>
        <p>Nearly £lOO Raised For Parish Organisations And Charities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>.1 Al tR-TIME DANGER.</head>
        <p>.1 Al tR-TIME DANGER.</p>
        <p>The annual sale of work on behalf of Christ Church:. Latchford, was held in the Parish Rooms, Latchford, on Saturday afternoon. In the -presence of a large congregation, the sale was opened by Mrs. W. N. liutchings. Amongst others present were the Vicar (Rev. G. C. Briggs), who presided, the Rev. H. V. ,Pigot (rector of Grappenhall),.Mrs. J. Charlton Parr, Mr. J. P. Reynolds, the Misses Broadbent and Miss Bessie Dutton (Moore). • The Objects of the Sale.</p>
        <p>• Explaining the objects of the sale, the Vicar said that every one of the parish organisations, the choir, the Sunday school; the mothers' meeting, the girls' society and the Band of Hope obtained a grant from the proceeds. Not only did the parochial organisation 8 benefit, but they made grants to outside charities,- which in- clmled the Warrington Nursing Association and several medical missions. He referred to the loss that the parish had sustained by the death of Miss Fanny Smith, who for 15 years had con- trolled the Church Workers' Association. He added that • the parish was very fortunate in its number of workers. He did not think that there was C parish that was more happily situated in that respect. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>Need For Workers.</head>
        <p>Need For Workers.</p>
        <p>In declaring the sale open, Mrs. Hutchinson said that in this time of stress, when there was so much for them all to do, they must be pre- pared and seize the task which lay nearest to hand. As many energetic and loyal workers in every place had left their ordinary occupations to undertake war work, there was a'danger that some of the ordinary, yet urgent work, would be neglected. 4, Mrs. Hutchings was accorded a vote of thanks on the motion of Mr. J. P. Reynolds, seconded by the Rev. 11. V. Pigot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>Musical Items.</head>
        <p>Musical Items.</p>
        <p>During the afternoon the Latchford Orchestral Society, under the able conductorship of Mr. Alfred Grounds, played a number of pleasing selections. A concert, arranged by Miss M. Reynolds, was given in the evening, and was highly enjoyed. The programme included musical monologues by Miss Collin, songs by Master Neville Davies, pianoforte solos by Mr. F. M. Darby and violin solos by Miss Reynolds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>Stallholders and Proceek.</head>
        <p>Stallholders and Proceek.</p>
        <p>The stalls were in charge of the following:— Refreshment Stall: Mrs. Fairclough, Mrs. Harrison and others. •</p>
        <p>Fancy Work Stall: Mrs. George Dakin, Miss Fairclough (Barryfield), Mrs. Cock, Mrs. John Reynolds.</p>
        <p>Toy Stall: Mrs. Atkinson and Mrs. Venn. Flower Stall: Miss Mart Gornall.. Plain Work Stall: Mrs. Briggs and the ladies of the Church Workers' Association.</p>
        <p>As a result of the sale £6B was raised and‘the donations amounted to £24 108., the total being 292 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>DAY OF PUNISHMENT.</head>
        <p>DAY OF PUNISHMENT.</p>
        <p>Special Advent Sermons At The Parish Church.</p>
        <p>. At the Parish Church on Sunday evening, the Rector, the Rev. Canon Willis, preached tho first of a series of sermons which lie intends to deliver during Advent, entitled "The Day of the Lord."</p>
        <p>At the outset the Rector said that the Bishop had asked the clergy to make the most of Advent, and had asked them particularly to preach special sermons, and to bring out the meaning of "The Day of the Lord." Ile (the Rector) proposed dealing with the subject of "The Day of the Lord" in three phases., viz. : (1) As a time of punishment; (2) As a time of salvation; (3) As a time of reward. - Speaking of the day of punisliment in the time of the early Jews, he said that there were three puniShments inflicted upon the people, the flood, the Egyptian bondage and the later captivity. He also referred to the punishment which was inflicted npon the enemies of the Jews, mentioning the ten plagues, the over- throw of the Assyrians and the overthrow of the Babylonians. Referring to punishment in Christian times, the Rector said that the first was the destruction of .Terusalem. In more modern history God had inflicted a terribh, punishment upon the Church when Archbishop Laud and Charles I. were beheaded for their' loyalty to the Church. Though neither of thes- two were blameless, it did not alter the fact that thOy,gave their lives for the Church. Going on to speak of the War, the Rector said that it was the judgment of God upOitt he;people, sent with the intention of purifying the nation. It we,- a terrible chastisement, but be believed that it was creating a great change upon the people of our land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>CHRISTMAS DECORATION'S.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS DECORATION'S.</p>
        <p>ItN. J. 11. U. Hollinfpxorth Hopes lo</p>
        <p>Have Them .</p>
        <p>We hope to decorate our church as usual, and trust we shall receive the necessary gifts to use for this purpose. Christians Communion counts if made within the Octave of Christmas. Wa trust that all communicants will bear this in mind.—" St. Barnabas' Church Parish Maga- zine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>MISSIONARY V% 0111 i.</head>
        <p>MISSIONARY V% 0111 i.</p>
        <p>Those At Home Must Keep Up The •.</p>
        <p>Supply Of Munitions.</p>
        <p>Services on behalf of foreign missions were held at St. Peter's' Church on -uStinday, when the preacher, morning and evening, A;as the Rev. 11, T. Wright, who has been working in Uganda. In the morning he spoke of the way in which the Disciples commenced to spread GOWN kingdom, by going out to all nations preaching the Oospell and teaching the people. The work had been continued, and was being carried on to-day by our missionaries out in China, Africa, India, &amp;c. • The missionaries were doing very (rood work, but they badly needed :Support, which all at home could give. Many, no doubt, could not go out to the mis- sion fields .and so give personal help, but could do their share by praying for the missionaries, and also giving liberal, it should be borne in mind that money, which was urgently 're- quired in the mission field, was not given to the natives fOr their own free use, but was .spent in building . churches, &amp;c., for them.. The speaker then told of the way in which the Witch doctors had got a firm hold of the natives, and how hard it was to make converts. By hard work and perseverance, however, several hundreds of natives had, in many provinces, been baptised, and some of them had even be- come teachers of their fellow men. ,</p>
        <p>During his evening sermon the Rev.. H. T. .Wright spoke chiefly of the habits, dress, and religion ,of the different kinds .of natives. Speaking dr the help which was needed in the mission fields, he said that just as our troops on the Continent were useless if we did not send munitions to them, so were our mission- aries useless if we did not do likewiselor them. The difference was ,that whereas the munitions of war had to be forged and made, the .muni-</p>
        <p>r-cif ifie •</p>
        <p>Body-building power proved equal to from 10 to 20 times the amount of Bovril taken.</p>
        <p>tions which were needed for our missionaries were always ready, if the people at home would only be willing to .send them.</p>
        <p>‘0,,,,••■0P</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>IRREGULAR ATTENDANCE.</head>
        <p>IRREGULAR ATTENDANCE.</p>
        <p>Mobilisation Of Spiritual Power A Growing Need.</p>
        <p>Without a doubt there has been and still con; tinues to be, an increased irregular attendance at public worship so that in this way—there is the loss of spiritual power—individual and collective loss. There is a growing need, it is sad it should be so, of a repetition of our appeal 1 for—what we may call "Mobilisation of our Spiritual Power." Pew, we believe very few, are opposed to the rnobilis.ation of our physical. material and financial powers. These are all needed and are being called for. May the call receive unstinfed, unqualified and unreserved response., All the manhood and the .womanhood of the Empire are required for the carrying on to a successful issue the great. conflict as well as munitions and moiley.—" St. Ann's ' 'Parish Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>".THE GREATEST FIGHT."</head>
        <p>".THE GREATEST FIGHT."</p>
        <p>"The Greatest Fight" was the subject of a special sermon preached by the Rev. A. J. Chessum at the Primitive Methodist Church on Sunday evening: Speaking from the text, "I have fought a good fight" (2 Tim., iv., 7), the preacher declared that the four essential needs for victory in the "greatest fight" were _strict. obedience to the great Commander; an intelli- gent issue of the great issues at stake; a know- ledge of the powers that were at hand, and last, but not least, an unfailing faith in ultimate victory. During the service the chair rendered the anthem ."0 for a closer walk with God," Mrs. A. E. Hoyle being the soloist. A solo was also sung by Miss Jessie Woodland. . As the congregation was assembling Mr. F. H. Banner, who presided at the •organ, played a number of recitals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <head>"LITTLE DRINKERS."</head>
        <p>THE "LITTLE DRINKERS."</p>
        <p>Ilt.,,ponsibillly Attaching' To Their Example.</p>
        <p>An open meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. was held on Sumthy afternoon. Councillor J. T. Locker, who was in the chair, spoke of his pleasure at being able to preside. Referring to the good influence of the P.S.A.'s, he men- tioned the great- sacrifice that the young men all over the country were making. Mr. B. J. Pickering, of Swinton, gave an interesting addresss on " Our Own Responsi- bility." The Government, he said, had tried to curtail the drink traffic by shortening the hours of consumPtion. In his opinion they had not done enough for he thought that the country ,Should have followed the example of Russia and Prance. Curtailment aid not meet the require- ment of the cases which we saw around us, such as women falling into temptation, and thus, in many cases, ruining their homes for this and future years. England was not doing her duty, but 'could do so by Tollowiik the King's example. The, "little drinkers" .thought .they did little harm, but they could not speak certainly of the evils of .their ,xample. They should also protect their listers, .by giving them help and sympathy. Mr. A. Healey and Mr. Yearsley took part in the meeting, and on behalf of the members, Mr. 9. Farrar, .secretary, heartily welcomed Private t. Knowles, who was spending a short leave in Warrington. Mrs. J. Smalfshaw rendered 'very, effectively the solo "0, Rest in the Lord." Mr. W. IL Payton presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>SCIENTIFIC BARBARISM.</head>
        <p>SCIENTIFIC BARBARISM.</p>
        <p>Wreckage Of A Civilisation</p>
        <p>Built On Sand.</p>
        <p>At the Friends' Meeting House, Butter- market-street, .orr Sunday, an address was -given ou The Emphasis ,of Armageddon," by Mr. Albert Mann, of Liverpool. Quoting Thomas a Kempis—" For occasions (10 not make a man frail, but they show what he is "—the speaker said Europe stood revealed. From Loudon to Petrograd, from .Paris to Sofia, the mask • of civilisation had been re- moved, and there stood exposed a scientific barbarism. This world-war was .a revelation, and it had emphasised the failure of Christen- dom to apply the principle of Love to inter- national politics. If Christ taught 'anything it was the universality of Love, without excep- tion or exclusion. We had thought that we believed this, but the faithlessness of Europe had been emphasised by war in a way that no other catastrophe could have done. We had been called to sacrifice, to an offering that would sanctify, as a sacrifice makes sacred both victim and priest, Europe's piled up armaments had been a standing contradiction to its Cathedrals and Chapels. If any should have played the .role of peacemaker it. was the professed Children' of God. Materialism, the unspiritual values given to national and social affairs, had been laid bare. We had trans- lated stlce6's iii terms of money, and Christ- ianity had proved itself a feeble factor in view of much social selfishness.</p>
        <p>The pagan method had been tried, and it had failed. 'The .principle of Love, in social, national and international relationships, had yet to be given a trial. Amidst the wreckage of a eiviliSation built upon sand and rock, we need to lay fairer and truer foundations than have yet been planned.</p>
        <p>lie that seeks even the highest good for him- self alone shall be disappointed.—George. Merriam.</p>
        <p>The Matrimonial Circle. Contains hundreds genuine advertisements, appeal- ing all classes ladies and gentlemen (many in good social position); post free, 6d., sealed envelope.— Editor, 18, Hogarth-road, Earls Court.</p>
      </div>
