      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Pre-historic Times. Last week's paragraph respecting a lecture on local life in pre-historic days reminded me of a remarkable discovery that was made some twenty years ago in a district in which I then lived, and in which the old astronomer referred to in this -column in Saturday's issue was intimately concerned. The curiosity, of some of his friends had been excited by the singular appearance of a well-defined circle, and they obtained the landowner's permission to explore this. As SOlllO had surmised, it proved to be a most interesting pre-historic burial place, con- taming several urns of rudely-decorated pottery full of calcined human remains, among which , were a few small ornaments. With the aid of Professor Boyd Dawkins, the urns were restored and varnished so as to preserve them, and now, with explanatory inscriptions and enclosed in a large glass case, they form one of the most valuable and attractive items in the town's musetim.</p>
        <p>Thriving on Bacteria. In conversation with an older man than my- self, I touched upon the question discussed at last week's meeting of the Warrington District Council. He was disposed to make light of the state of things in Padgate, as disclosed in the course of those proceedings. Said he: "I think we've more need to be 'feared o' Germans than o' germs just now. They're making a bit too much o' that germ theory, to tell you what I think. I was one of a family of twelve— father, mother and ten children. We were brought up on a little farm, and all the water we drank flowed through an open channel across the fields where the cattle and poultry were. Yet we never had a doctor in the house; I've never tasted physic in my life, and my brothers and sisters are all living, and they're well and hearty." I congratulated him on the extra- ordinary record, and suggested that logically his argument lett one to infer that such insanitary conditions as he had described were preferable to modern methods. To this he cautiously replied that he was not exactly arguing; he simply gave an indubitable fact from personal experience, and must leave me to make what I liked of it. Recollections of Mesmerism.</p>
        <p>In last week's column on this subject I read hold Mesmer's disciples gradually came to sub- stitute gentler means for his violent methods, being able to produce the like p..enomena by undemonstrative manipulation. As a youngster patronised the entertainments—yes, that word correctly describes them—of three pro- fessors of the art. The least known of these used to place a disc in the palm of each sub- ject's hand, with instructions to gaze earnestly upon it; and in most instances this was sufficient to induce mesmeric sleep—or .whatever it was. Others were wont to take up a commanding position on the platform, and, wildly gesticulat- ing, to draw from the seats men and women who, however unwilling apparently, seemed in- capable of resistance, and were IIIIRT3 to per- form all sorts of antics and behave as if under the influence of powerful emotions. Under the impression that occupants of the seats were liable to be treated thus indiscriminately, I was always careful to avoid the fierce glare of the formidable professor's dreaded eye. Eventually I found that the people with whom he seem- ingly took such unwarrantable liberties were patients travelling with him for systematic treatment, and I began to sympathise somewhat with sceptics who questioned the bona-fides of the principals in thp show; but after a while I made the acquaintance of an amateur phrenolo- gist who unmistakably possessed that same gift of mesmerism. After all, are we not quite familiar with it as hypnotism? There is now- adays little mystery and no doubt at all about it.</p>
        <p>Last of the Law-Sordeapts. The reference to a sergeant-at-law in last week's Law Court stories set me- thinking of one of the last of the holders of this now extinct title. Father of the present eminent K.C. of that name, the late Sergeant Tindal Atkinson was Judge at the Halifax County Court when I attended the sittings as a reporter. As from experience thus gained I testified years ago in a sketch published in that town, Judge Tindal Atkinson was an in- veterate punster, never neglecting . any. oppor- tunity that offered- for a play upon words such as was ever dear to the heart of the lamented Tom Hood. When a defendant who was sued before him on account of a quantity of "slink beef," pleaded that the stuff was "too far gone for sale," this fun-loving old law-sergeant could not h.: ' a life of him suppress a sort of stage whisper hat it was "high meat at a low drice ", when another objected to pay for ti'inir" which he had ordered because it was ' in quality that lie and his friends had n it at one another, could lie help .iiggesting that they had been "playing old gooseberry" with it: I know that punning is , now reckoned bad form, and I do not forget the " 311:ado " and its Gilbertian gibe .at the " Judicial humorist "; all the same, we re- port-rs of the Tindal Atkinson regime were gi.ateful f^r even these small mercies. They distinctly relieved the severe gloom and other- wise almost intolerable monotony of the aver- age County Court sitting.</p>
        <p>" Legal " Rominiscences.</p>
        <p>An, ag the barristers practising in that Court were the late Mr. Lawrence Gene, who was a popular lecturer; the late Mr. J. Heaton Cadman, who ultimately succeeded to the judgeship; and Mr. Tindal Atkinson, the son of the genial lawyer to whose memory I pay my poor tribute. Somehow it used to strike me as extremely funny when in the course of pro- tracted argument, son repeatedly addressed father as Your Honour," and was throughout scrupulous in the employment of conventional phrases and in the observance of legal etinuPt*-. It is inconceivable that in public the lations could have been different; still liked to think that the odd situation ;Aril lie Honour pretty much as it did my- self. We Pressmen sat just behind counsel and -s could not help overhearing the asides whereby the lawyers actively engaged poked fun at each other. Once toward the close of an eloquent peroration Mr. Gane exclaimed with charaeteristic fervour: "I care not were he as rich as Crcesus, as patient as Job, and as up- right as Daniel," &amp;c. "Here, I say," chaffed Mr. Tindal Atkinson, his opponent in the case, "Where did you pick that up? Seems familiar; I'm certain I've heard it before quite lately. Not bad; but still not thine own, eh?" Made in Germany.</p>
        <p>Let me contrast with the foregoing this advertisemtet from a Berlin paper (an old one of cou'rse):—" A young lady k2f a highly esteemed and noble family, as beautiful as' Helen, as thrifty as Penelope, as economical as the Electress Marian of Brandenberg, as sprightly as Madame de Sae, a singer , like Mlle.-Breval, a dancer like Cerito, a pianist like Rosa .Rastner, a violinist like Teresita Mialnollo, a harpist like Bertrand, a sculptor like Princess Marie d'Orleans, as austere as Lucrece, as charitable as St. Elizabeth of Hungary, as devout as Nightingale, having at command a large fortune, lacking masculine accivaintances, seeks a husband by the news- paiier's. agency." Talk about phrase-making! Assuredly,-had this come his way while he was in the flesh, this would have turned the late Mr. Lawrence Gane olive green with envy. Seriously, the example goes to confirm our rapidly-growing convicti n that the Germans "(strafe 'em) are very deficient in humour.</p>
        <p>Promising.</p>
        <p>Old Gentleman (engaging new chauffeur) '1 suppose I can write to your last employer for your character?" Chauffeur : " I am sorry to say, sir, each of the last two gentlemen I have been With died .in my vervice."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 11, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 11, 1915.</p>
        <p>servatives—those are the people who have! estates in Surrey and drink champagne; and then a Mr. Carstang—he represented the girls; a kind of female being that (lid not exist in the village, but for whom the worthy inhabitants, strange to say, felt a peculiar longing. After many long speeches, a great deal of noise and terrible depreca- tions thrown at one another by the candi- dates, the time came when the villagers should decide whether they would rather have an estate in Surrey with champagne, beer to give away, or girls and—er—nothing else. Every available space was used for putting up weird-looking posters, teeming with red, blue and violet paint. Many stated in flaming letters that wonderful things were in store for the villagers, who worked themselves into such a pitch of ecstasy, that when one of the candidates appeared before them, they would yell and shout, and chant a -humdrum kind of song. Of course, this state of affairs could not exist in modern times, now that civilisation has permeated the earth and men have grown wise, but such were conditions at that period of this world's history.</p>
        <p>After this confusion and excitement had lasted for many days the time bad come for the villagers to , decide which of the three they wanted as their representative. A number of tin boxes with boles cut in the top were placed on a table, and those who wanted to vote advanced, made a cross on a slip of paper opposite the name of the one they wished to vote for, and dropped this slip into the box. The crosses were counted, and 10, and behold! nearly all the villagers had chosen the man representing the, girls—why, we really cannot imagine! Now, in conclusion, it might be stated that although we voted for our sweethearts and wives, nothing further has taken place. The honourable member, Mr. R. Carstang, may represent them, but there it ends; we see neither girls nor women, and what all the villagers are now agitating for is that our representative take steps at once tawards securing for us the most coveted of all things—our wives and sweethearts—in being rather than in representation. L.S.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>RUTHLEBEN INTERNMENT CAMP.</head>
        <p>RUTHLEBEN INTERNMENT CAMP.</p>
        <p>Recent Experiences Of A British Prisoner In Germany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>MAKING THE BEST OF ADVERSITY.</head>
        <p>MAKING THE BEST OF ADVERSITY.</p>
        <p>BY C. E.</p>
        <p>A friend has just reached England from the' large Ruthleben Civilian Camp, near Berlin, in Germany. This camp is on the site of the large race course. My friend has been a prisoner for several months. He has just been exchanged because of the condition of his health, brought on by the internment, and he has been in hospital for some months. The imprisonment has aged him considerably, and 1 tear undermined his health. He is of opinion that, in the case of a number of his prisoners, the effects on their consti- tutions will be of a permanent character. This friend is a member of an Austrian family, married to an English lady, has spent most of his life in this country, and long ago became a naturalised Britisher, and is proud of the fact. He happened to be in Berlin when the war broke out and was interned. He was offered his freedom if he would repudiate his British citizenship, and again assume his Austrian nationality, but indignantly refused to do this, ad as a con- sequence has spent thirteen weary months in this camp. There were at one time over four thousand inmates in this camp and the majority were British subjects, with a proportion of French and Russians. Of the 13ritishers there were students in the German universities, clerks engaged in business houses trading with Eng- land, sea captains and sailors, whose boats were in German ports when war broke out, and British subjects who were taking cures at the German spas. Our friend describes the condition of the camp as being in many respects very bad, especially the sanitary arrangements. The food was poor and insufficient; this was specially felt by the younger and more active members, who developed healthy appetites by games. The majority of those interned were of good position, who had resources or relatives 'at home who supplemented their rations and funds. The discipline was of the usual severe German type, and petty irregularities were severely punished. My friend tells me that a flag staff had been erected in anticipa- tion of the Emperor's birthday. Some thoughtless young rascal on the evening before the celebration cut the cord which, of course, rattled to the ground, with the result that it could only be replaced by bringing the pole down. For this the whole four thousand were kept in the inner enclosure, put on meagre rations, and smoking was forbidden for three days. On one occasion when cheers were called by one of the officers for the Em- peror, an indiscreet youth uttered an expres- sion derogatory of the character of the Emperor. This was overheard. He was kept a special prisoner in solitary confinement for three months, and has lately been handed over to the military authorities to deal with the offence. The sequel is Not yet known. My friend had to sleep, along with five others, in a space rather less than a horse stall. His berth was at the bottom, prac- tically on the ground, and this he considers the cause of his subsequent illness. I was pleased to hear that, while the discipline was very rigorous, and in some instances cruel, the general life of the camp was made fairly healthy, and no impediment was put in the way of the inhabitants doing what they could by association to make their life endurable. n this they were evidently successful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>CAMP MAGAZINE.</head>
        <p>THE CAMP MAGAZINE.</p>
        <p>My friend has lent me several copies of a magazine compiled in camp, entitled " In Ruthleben Camp," which are intensely interest- ing. We can gather from ,these a fairly cor- rect idea of one side, of the life in this camp. I say one side, because it is evident that noth- ing would be allowed to be printed in such a magazine that would reflect in any way upon the treatment of the prisoners. Bearing this in mind, it is evident the authorities gave to the inrnates considerable freedom in devising methods ,of spending their time. The maga- zine shows evidence of considerable 'ability amongst the contributors. It is printed in the town, and is freely illustrated by sketches by the pencils of the twisoners, and shows that there have been amongst them artists of great ability. On one of the numbers we find a group of what are intended to be helpful criticisms and suggestions'', whieh come to the editors in response to an appeal. Here are two helpful criticisms : I consider the magazine too flippant in character, and too much space is allotted to lurid descriptions of mere exhibitions of physical prowess. One of my young friends (Mr. Ackel Ight) has written the enclosed charming essay, "Does barbed wire fix a limit to the range of one's imagination?" You would be well advised to print this, for it will raise the tone of your misguided periodical.</p>
        <p>P. REE SIIARD.</p>
        <p>Honored Mister,—What I've got ter say is this 'ere. Your paper's alright as far as it goes, but it don't go no farther than a blamed Mother's Meeting Report. What us chaps wants is something sporty and spicy. What about Latest betting News? What about tips for the Cricket Champion- ship? Ain't there no blooming Scandal in this 'ere Camp? Why, s'help me, there's a bloke what spars in Barney's Ring, that has the neatest left in Ruthleben, and I ain't so much as seen his name mentioned in your Nippy rag. Yours disgustedly,</p>
        <p>No MAMMY'S DARLING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>WEEK THAT HAS NO PARCEL.</head>
        <p>A WEEK THAT HAS NO PARCEL.</p>
        <p>The following Perverted Proverbs for Prisoners are good : It's a long week that has no parcel, It's never too late to shave.</p>
        <p>A quid in the hand's worth two in the office, Be sure your non-corn. will find you out. Travel in haste and repent in—Ruthleben. Soap springs eternal in the barrack sink.</p>
        <p>One writer let his imagination run riot, and makes the following propositions which he thinks ought to be included in any complete concentration camp : We should like to offer a few humble sug- gestions for similar enterprises which would seem to demand attention and give plenty of scope to our energetic promoters and financiers.</p>
        <p>For instance, an up-to-date Roller-skating Rink, with polished maple floor, free skates, exhibitions and expert tuition, would meet. a great demand, combining as if would exercise and amusement. Adjoining could be built an Oriental Dancing Hall, furnished with a spring floor and a red, white and blue Hungarian Band in constant attendance. The total outlay need not exceed' .17,500 ! We pride ourselves in suggesting the Turkish Bath and Hydropathic Establish- ment, it is surprising that nobody has yet thought, of it. A Winter garden, with a few palms and perhaps a fountain, a lounge and smoking room, with Rekersque waitresses and a soda fountain, should not be difficult to arrange. Recent developments, have made an In- ebriates' Home an absolute essential. The laying. out 'of 'a Deer Park might prove difficult,. but think bow popular it would be if well stocked with old dears. Then again, Ruthleben °positively Cries aloud for its Maclaine Taussauds. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>ARKER.</head>
        <p>ARKER.</p>
        <p>trouble would be that our supply of cele- brities is so immense.</p>
        <p>A BANK HOLIDAY REVERIE. The editorial for the August Bank Holiday Number is good : It's August Monday! We can't lie in bed for that extra half-hour, because there's a bath party at eight o'clock. We can put on our Sunday togs, but why should we? We can't take our morning walk past the six- mile limit because we can't get further than threequarters of a mile, and besides there is no pub. at the end of the camp. We can't walk down the High-street throwing the glad eye because there is nothing to throw the glad eye at, and there isn't a single shop in Ruthleben at which one can buy a lady-teaser. We can't take 'Arriet to to 'Appy 'A mpsead because there is ,no 'Arriet and no 'Amstead, and we can't take the missus and the kids to the seaside because there isn't any missus and there are no kids. And tho'- we've plenty of sand, the sea is a very long Way off. And there is no Cycling Race Meeting because Dick Hal- pin's is the only bicycle in the Camp, and there is no Race Meeting because tho' we've got the Race Course we've no horses to put on it. We can dance in the evening, but ! who wants to waltz with a great lout of a man—and all the pubs. are closed. It's worse than living in Glasgow for a year of Sundays. But are we down-hearted? Of course not!</p>
        <p>On one page there appears a photograph of a bonny two-year-old child. The note under it has something of pathos as well as fun in it. "This belongs to someone HERE. The plump little Devil, or, at least, his photo- graph, has been found in our Camp Parcel Office!</p>
        <p>"1 Daddy, please claim me!' "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>PARCELS FROM HOME.</head>
        <p>PARCELS FROM HOME.</p>
        <p>I knew, of course, SOMETHING about .Parcels. I had received advices of parcels -which never turned up. I had waited hours •—cold, frosty , hours. I have seen more :fortunate individuals returning from that queue wreathed in 'smiles and laden with parcels (their explanation that they were :for their friends has always seemed to me deficient in originality), but never have I :seen such stacks of parcels as burst upon my vision on this occasion.</p>
        <p>There arc a few sentences which look as if they were intended for copy book headings : "If you attend the lecture you. will sleep -</p>
        <p>"There are many bottles but no beer." "On the trousers of Charles there are many patches."</p>
        <p>"My brother Charles says that we shall soon</p>
        <p>go." "Your brother Charles is a liar." "Time does not always fly." "I shall return to my parents with much pleasure." ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>LEAST OUR COUNTRY COULD EXPECT.</head>
        <p>THE LEAST OUR COUNTRY COULD EXPECT.</p>
        <p>In August last there appears to have been an attempt from a part of the minority of the Camp to send a request to the Home Authority that souvenir badges should be pro- vided to commemorate the internment. We :read that a crowded meeting representative 'of all British interests in the Camp was held, and I give what I think will be considered a very sensible speech of a Britisher : If the letter asking the Foreign •Office about a Ruthleben medal expressed the opinion of the majority, then it was time , this Camp was run as a lunatic asylum. At a time like this, when our Government and the American Embassy were overbur- dened with work, We should not make a nuisance of ourselves by putting forward childish and ridiculous requests. We were only a nondescript collection of people, technically British subjects, without a single othei connecting-band, and we had done nothing whatever to deserve recokni- Hon. The least our country could expect of us was that we should act with a certain dignity, and not behave in any way which would reflect unfavourably on our nation, or would be likely to make us a laughing- stock amongst friends and enemies. This is very well said—those at home please note and imitate this spirit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>MOST COVETED OF ALL THINGS.</head>
        <p>MOST COVETED OF ALL THINGS.</p>
        <p>One more extract in conclusion. A clever klescriptive article will help us to appreciate the Mark Tapley-like spirit in which the .inmates strive to banish their boredom, and forget their hardships. The grey men wear- ing shiny hats referred to are the German authorities. After a posture of aloofness has been established by general introduction, in which the name of the settlement Ruthle- ben is changed to ." Restlife," the writer goes on to say :</p>
        <p>The inhabitants took delight in filling up the puddles .in their village, in .opening shops in a yard they called Bond-street, in building up a stage in a cold place under the staircase-looking erection, where people would appear nearly every evening before a large crowd of villagers and play the fool, or talk nonsense. They loved forming societies, electing committees, and disband- ing them again directly afterwards, build- ing wooden huts—if they had plenty of money—trying to play pretty tunes to please one another. They even approached the chief one of the grey beings with the shiny hats, and stated, as they were living where the horses used to live, couldn't they run round the circular road [the racecourse] where these beasts used to race. After some time, the grey men allowed them to have half of the circle, and the villagers used to frolic on this place all day. It must also be stated that there were no females among the citizens, and most of them employed their time knocking a ball around with a bit of a stick or other harm- less amusements. Food was given them by the Men in grey, and by people who used to be their friends, and relations across the waters, who also supplied them with money. The most important beings, however—or those who thought themselves most import- ant—were the so-called chiefs or. captains. The villagers held these great ,men in awe and durst do nothing they forbade. But why continue the account, of this prosperous townlet, when we are .all suffi- ciently acquainted with its charms. Suffice it to say thot the life, as described above, after a lone.period of varied and multi- farious' experiences, -despite the improve- ments in the village, the local gossip termed " Rumours," and other remarkable inci- dents, scarcely satisfied the longings of the villagers for wore exciting times, and so one of the many thousand Societies in the place, termed the "Debating" owing to its desire to bait Unfortunate individuals on to a platform to gas and to relieve their pent- up feelings—much to the suffering of the listeners—by giving vent to long-winded speeches, deeided it was necessary for a Parliament to be formed to represent the people—in :what way? • We would rather not say. But three theMbers were proposed, a Mr. Cohen, to stand for the Liberals in the village, that is,. whose one desire to give their goods [Why .and be liberal with their means; a Mr. Boss, who stood for the Con-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>EASTERN HOME LIFE.</head>
        <p>EASTERN HOME LIFE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mounfield Tells How Women Fare In The Orient.</p>
        <p>In the seventh of a series of lectures which are being given in Friars' Groun Sklooltoon, Mr. A. Mounfield dealt w Tett some features cf Eastern home life. A woman's lot, he sai.l, in Eastern lands had little to relieve its monotony. Some of its regular features were the carrying of water from the well, the grinding of corn and the baking of bread. The grinding mill con- sisted,of two stones placed one above the other, the upper stone being moved backwards and forwards by a wooden handle. The women would sit and grind, singing as they pulled the stone. This is the mental picture Jesus had in the saying "Two women shall be grinding at the mill, one shall be taken and the other left." There was a merciful old law which said that if a mill stone were taken, in pledge it must be restored before evening because it was a necessity of the home. A similar law forbad the keeping of a garment. It must be returned at even because the cloak that covered the body by day was used as a bed covering at night. Fires and Cooking. The bread which the women baked was in the form of thin cakes. They had an oven formed by a few stones placed together. This was heated by the burning of dried grass or thorns. In the words of Jesus the grass was "cast into the oven." When the stones were hot the meal and water were poured on them and the result was a thin cake of bread.</p>
        <p>They had no steady fires in summer, but in winter a fire was made in a vessel or brazier. The fire would be /lighted outside in the yard and when the smoke had subsided the glowing embers were carried into the house. This was the process which was going on when Peter warmed himself in the gateway of the High Priest's house, and the servants who derided him were probably waiting for the fire to burn red before they carried it into the chamber. The rooms had no chimneys and the fumes from the fires coloured everything, first a de- lightful brown and afterwards a most satis- factory black. From this reason Eastern houses never needed to be decorated. When the .Psalmist said "I am like a bottle in the smoke" he had in mind the leather bottle hung on the cottage wall, shrivelled and blackened—a very depressing object to look upon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>Eastern Beds.</head>
        <p>Eastern Beds.</p>
        <p>Very little of an Eastern woman's time would be required for bed making. Their bed was a mere scoViling or quilt. Among the poor the day garment covered them; but the more favoured ones had thicker coverings. These were place,d in a small chamber, the " closet " of the Sermon on the Mount, during the day and spread in the bed chamber by night. In the evening they were often spread upon the flat roof as cushions, and the family would sit and sing- in the cool of the day. This explains the passage "Let the saints sing aloud upon their beds," that is, sing psalms when others may be singing foolish ditties. Woman and Her Lord.</p>
        <p>In every sphere of life woman is made the drudge and burden bearer. If the farmer takes his produce to market his wife must carry it. If he is wealthy enough to buy a donkey, he rides upon it, and his wife walks behind carry- ing the merchandise. If there are visitors at home the woman hides from view, as Sarah did in the tent thousands of years ago. Hospitality.</p>
        <p>The subject of Eastern hospitality! said the</p>
        <p>lecturer, had many pleasing aspects. to-day, as when Abram entertained visitors, the master of the house makes himself the servant of every caller. For three days any visitor was cared for and no questions were asked as to his busi- ness or destination. Every village had its pub- lic guest chamber, where the traveller could find free lodging and food. In country places the door of every house stood open and any way- farer could enter and be refreshed.</p>
        <p>It ought to be pointed out, however, that in Bible days there was different usage in the cities. The outer gate of the great man's house was closed and the visitor who knocked had many inquiries to answer before the door was opened. It .was a slow process to gain admis- sion. This will explain the.metaplor "Knock and the door shall be opened to you," that is to say, there will be no long delay and questioning. It also helps us to gee the meaning of the pas- sage "If any man will hear my voice and open the door I will come in and sup with him."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>Eastern Feasts.</head>
        <p>Eastern Feasts.</p>
        <p>When the wealthy man/ gave a feast his chosen guests were placed on his right hand and left at the head of the feast, and the rest on two sides of an open square. The Pharisees when invited tp feasts took places at the top, assuming the chief places as by right. Jesus with delicate irony advised them to take places at the 'bottom and wait until the master of the house invited them to "Come up higher." He was reminding them in a telling way that in the Kingdom of God men do not choose their own places and that places of honour are for the humble guests. The rich men of the East keep a wardrobe of costly garments and adorn their guests when a feast is prepared. These are put on as the visitors enter and are taken off when they leave. In the Parable there was a man at the table who "had not on a wedding garment." This meant that he had despised the courtesy Of the king and refused to wear the robe that was offered.</p>
        <p>The story of Simon the Pharisee and the woman and the Parable of the Ten Virgins were also discussed at length as illustrations of other features of Eastern home life.</p>
        <p>• A Scottish • Summary. An unnecessarily gloomy view of the new war bonds is token by "The.. Aberdeen Evening Gazette" when it says: "The money will be fully returned to us some day; but that. e-xeept we are very young will only be after we have been a long time dead." Our Scottish contem- porary is on safer ground when it asserts that certain classes "would invest more freely in war loans if the vouchers wore "made liquid."-- " Punch,!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>MORRIS DANCE.</head>
        <p>THE MORRIS DANCE.</p>
        <p>A Relic Of Our Methods Of Merry. Making In The Days Of Old.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>ITS HISTORY MEAN I NG.</head>
        <p>ITS HISTORY MEAN I NG.</p>
        <p>OUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>appeare by particular for the fur- nishinge of same. vi.</p>
        <p>Six cavaliers and three boys on horseback .with a " gyaunt " display of Morris dancers 'ought' certainly to have .Made a great impres- sion at the-Mansion House. The latest allusion we have been able to find in church accounts comes. from Great Marlow: Down to 1629 this parish had a set of dresses for the dancers, which it lent out to neighbour- ing parishes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>BY ARTHUR</head>
        <p>BY ARTHUR</p>
        <p>F4ince the far-off day. when Jubal began to " handle the harp," it has been the habit of mankind to manufacture happiness, and when we are asked to explain the meaning of the horse's head (which cropped up in our study of souling), it is only possible to say that it was one of the numberless -capers of the past to be classed with the clown's cap and the jester's bells.</p>
        <p>Only the. student of the past knows how many such things have vanished from English life. More old habits have perished in the last two generations than in the two preceding centuries. We make merry at Christmas, it is true, but we no longer have the sports of Twelfth Night, of Easter and .Whitsuntide, of Maying and Midsummer Eve. Who -ever saw a Harvest Queen, or a Lord and Lady of the Sheaves, a Bear Baiting, or a Rush Bearing? The Fair survives, but we do not risk our reputation by attending it. For all of which and much more, we have to thank the school- master.</p>
        <p>But • this is a digression. The point to be remembered, when we study the amusements of the past, is that the common people were unable to travel. The spell of New Brighton and the gentle innocence of Belle Vue were alike unknown. They must make their own fun, save when the fun organised itself and travelled' from village to village for their exhilaration.</p>
        <p>The Morris Dance belonged to the category of organised fun, and was the predecessor of the circus.</p>
        <p>A GUESS AT ITS ORIGIN.</p>
        <p>Of its origin, or even the meaning of the name, we .are afraid that little can be said. Dr. Johnson, who must have known the dance in all its glory, tells us that " the Morris Dance, im'which bells are jingled, or staves or swords clashed, was learned by the Moors, and was probably a kind of Pyrrhic or mili- tary dance." We do not quite follow the learned doctor in asdibing the dance to the Moors, but it was clearly the belief of our ancestors that it came from Spain. A passage from Blount gives the same information, and is interesting as telling us what the dance was like in his day. There were, he says "usually five men, and a boy dressed in a man's habit, whom they called the ;Maid Marion, or per- haps Morian, from the Italian Morione a bead piece,, because her head was wont to be gaily trimmed up. Common people called it a Morris Dance."</p>
        <p>Whatever other characters appeared, there was always a Fool and a Maid Marion. In later generations they were Tom (Tom the piper) and Bess, two characters borrowed from the ancient New Year 14'estivn1 of Fools, and later still, Robin Hood and Little John.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>IN HENRY THE SEVENTH'S TIME.</head>
        <p>IN HENRY THE SEVENTH'S TIME.</p>
        <p>The earliest glimpses we get of the Morris Dance do not give much information as to the form of the performance, but they are inter- esting as showing their favour at Court, and their connection with the Church.</p>
        <p>In the Privy Purse expenses of Henry VII., under date 1495, is a New Year entry "For playing of the Mourice dancers £2." , A similar charge is entered for February 4, 1502. .Henry_Vlll., in the • early years of. his reign, also • enjoyed the dissipation of the dancers. Writing in 1510, one of his retinue, Richard Gibson, tells how the Moryshe men came out of an artificial hill on the top of which was " a golden stoke, .branehyd with roses and pomgarnats crowned," devised by Sir Henry Guildford.</p>
        <p>Though this does not describe the dancers or their doings, it is worth quoting as showing that four centuries ago the " Moryshe men " were thoroughly respectable fellows, whose stage effects were of the most elaborate kind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>CHURCH AND THE DANCE.</head>
        <p>THE CHURCH AND THE DANCE.</p>
        <p>Very much more 'remarkable is the dis- covery that Morris dancing was 'fostered by the Church. In the churchwardens' accounts of Kingston-on-Thames during the years of Henry VIII., there are a series of entries which show that through a long 'period the dancing was carried on at the charge of the Church. These are sonic of the items : X s.d. 1508. For pa3,-nting of the Mores' garments for sarten gret leveres 1508. For plyts 'and of limn for - the Mores' garnients 0 2 11 1508. For Orseden for the same 0 010 1508. For bellys for the daunsars 0 012 1509. For silver paper for the Mores. daunsars • 0 0 7 ' 1519. Shoes for the Mores daunsars, the frere, and Mayde Maryan, at id. a peyre 0 5 4 1521. Eight yerds of fustyan for the Mores daunsars' coats, .O 16 0 1521. A dosyn of gold skynnes for the Morres 0 010</p>
        <p>These extracts incidentally show us that among the characters of the play was a "friar" and a Maid Maryan, that the dancers dressed elaborately for their parts, and. had " bellys."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>CURIOUS CHURCH WARDROBE.</head>
        <p>A CURIOUS CHURCH WARDROBE.</p>
        <p>An entry of 1636 reveals that one of their plays was Robin Hood, and that the stage dresses in the possession of the churchwardens were " a fryer's coat of russet, with a kyrtle of worsted welted with red cloth, a rnowren's cote of buckram, and 4 Morres dauncers' cote of white fustian spangels'ed, and two gryne saten coats, and a dysardd's cote of cotton, and 6 payre of garters with bells." Thus early do we find the dancer bedecked with bells.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>ENTER THE HOBBY HORSE.</head>
        <p>ENTER THE HOBBY HORSE.</p>
        <p>We reach the middle of the 16th century before we find anything in the nature of a horse mentioned in connection with the Morris dance. It is found at Reading in the church accounts : s. d.</p>
        <p>A.D. 1557. Item. Payd to the myn- strels and the hobby horse uppon May Day Payed to them the Sonday after May Day 0 020</p>
        <p>Two illustrations exist which show us the 16th century hobby horse. One is a painted window at Betley in Staffordshire, the other is a painting by Vickenboom. In both of them the horse is in the form of an appendage to the actor. Ths head is fixed in ilont and the tail behind. The man walks upright and holds a rein, and the fore and hind parts of the animal are joined by a cloth which hangs down to his feet.</p>
        <p>The characters shown in the painted window circle round the May Pole. There is Robin. Hood, ,Friar Tuck, Maid Marion, 'the piper, the, fool and five dancers. All have bells, either at the ankles, wrists or knees.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>BRAVE DISPLAY.</head>
        <p>A BRAVE DISPLAY.</p>
        <p>Several London churches have references in their accounts to charges for dresses and bells for the dancers. .A very Curious entry at St. Giles', Crivplegate, shows that the thing was done in great style on Midsummer Eve :- 1571. Item, paide in cfiarges by the appoint- ment of the parisshoners, for the setting() forth of a gyannt Morris- dainsers with vi ealyvers and iii • boies on horseback, to go in the watch befoore the Lade 3faiore -uppon Midsomer even, as may</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>BANGING FEAT.</head>
        <p>A BANGING FEAT.</p>
        <p>With Maris dancing as a popular pastime it was natural that some of the more nimble ones would strive to excel. In Queen ,Eliza- beth's day the championship SOCMS to have fallen to William Kemp, a 'well-known comic actor. This agile performer, who must have known Shakespeare very well, assayed the feat of dancing all the way from London to Nor- wich., He danced to music and covered the journey in nine days, mUch to the admiration of vast crowds, and to the advantage, we should imagine,. of his /own pocket. He was entertained royally upon the way, and danced a triumphal entry into Norwich. The journey was made in 1599, and in 1600 he published an account of it under the title " Kemp's Nine Days' Wonder. Performed in a dance from London to Norwich. Containing the pleasure paines and kind entertainment of Mr. Kemp betweene London and that citty in his late Morrice."</p>
        <p>Nine days appear to have been the period occupied in travelling, but there were days of rest between. These seem to have been spent in contests with the natives, and he tells curious stories of those who tried to " dance him out" in tests of endurance. He tells with some admiration of one fat lady who danced a whole mile with him along the road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>IN THE BAD OLD DAYS.</head>
        <p>IN THE BAD OLD DAYS.</p>
        <p>In the Stuart period Morrising was a com- mon Sunday pastime, and Richard Baxter, in his lament for the desecration of the Sabbath, tells us that the effect of King James' "Book of Sports" .was to make the day one of "un- couth spectacles, stage plays and revels . . . the reader (minister) was fain to stay till the piper and players would give over; and some- times the Morrice dancers would come into the church in all their linen, and scarfs, and antic dresses with Moirice-bells jingling at their legs. And as soon as common prayer was read did haste out presently to their play again." Stubbes, who lived earlier, had drawn a similar picture. "They have their hobby- horses, their dragons, and other 'antiques, to- gether with the gaudie pipes and thunderyng drummers and strike up the devill's dance withal. Sn they march to the church, invad- ing it, even though service be performing, with such a confused noyse that no man can hear his own voice. Then they adjourn to the churchyard; where booths are set up, and the rest of the day spent in dancing and drink- ing." Stubbes was a strict Puritan, it is true, but his picture is not overdrawn. When the Puritans gained the ascendancy sports declined, but with the return, of Charles 11. the bells began to jingle again. There is little more to be gleaned from literary sources concerning the .Morris dancers. ,They appear to have performed little plays and sketches, and filled in the gaps with copious supplies of buffoonery. There was always the fool and Maid Marion, with the Friar, as a butt for their jests. The audiences would render energetic help, for our ancestors took their pleasure cheerfully and usually went to bed tired.</p>
        <p>Perhaps Our readers will like to see a description of sonic, Morris dancers of just three centuries ago: It occurs in " Cobbes' Prophecies," published in 1614 : It was my hap of late, by chance, To meet a country Morris dance, When cheefest of them all, the foole, Plaied with a ladle and a toole; When every younger shak't his bells, Till sweating reet gave folaug smells; And fine Maids Marian, with her smoile, Bhew'd how a rascall plaid the roile ; - But, when the hobby-horse did wihy, • Then all the wenches gave a tilty ; But when they gan to shake their boxe, And not a goose could catch a foxe, The piper then put up his pipes, And all the woodcocks look't like Snipes, And therewith fell a show'ry streams, Of Morris dancing in modern times there is little to be said. • The May Pole dance, to the accompaniment of 'jingling bells, has survived until the present. generation. But danciitg to bells is not Morris dancing in the old and true sense. We have no longer the Fool and Maid; Friar Tuck has gone, and with him Robin Hood and his merry comrades. The circus seems to be the successor to the Morris, and the clown is the only reminder of Tom the</p>
        <p>- -It is a pity that Maid Marion has gone, for she was originally, as the name suggests, the Queen of the May. As such she would be the successor of the Roman Flora, and of very respectable antiquity. . She is gone with the rest.</p>
        <p>They were a noisy crew, but lovers of old England will join us in wishing peace to their memory.</p>
        <p>" SOULING."</p>
        <p>Referring to our recent articles on souling a correspondent kindly calls our attention to the following paragraph in "Notes and Queries," written pigmy years ago :</p>
        <p>" In the -parish of Lynim it is customary, for a week or ten days before the sth of Novem- ber, for the skeleton of a horse's head, dressed up with ribbons, etc., having glass eyes in- serted in the sockets, and mounted on a short pole by way of a handle, to be carried by a man underneath covered with a horse cloth. There is generally a chain attached to the nose, which is held by a second man. In houses to which they can gain access they go through some kind of performance, the man with the chain telling the horse to rear, open its mouth, etc. The object, of course, is to obtain money. The horse will sometimes seize persons and hold them fast till they pay for being. set free; but he is generally very peace- able, for, in case of resistance being offered, his companions generally take to flight, and leave the poor horse to fight Wont."</p>
      </div>
