      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>JEALOUSY-WITHOUT CAUSE.</head>
        <p>JEALOUSY-WITHOUT CAUSE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. M.: "How those people dress like they do on theireincome, I can't imagine. We can't do it, and I know Jack earns more than Mr. H—. Just look at the costume she is wearing. It is taildr-made, and must have cost three guineas:' Miss D.: " Yes ! They always dress W. 011. You can recognise the ' cut ' of a good tailor in the overcoat Mr. H. has on."</p>
        <p>Mrs. M.: "How do they manage it? The other day both of them had those fashionable brown Raincoats on, and they looked so stylish and dressy. I intend having one as soon as I can afford it, and I must have a new costume for Christmas, although how to Manage it I really don't know—our tailor charges so much. Where do the IL's go to—do • you know?" Miss D.: "That's perhaps a little secret. It a firm of high-class Tailors in Horsemarket- street. We get all our things from the same place, and I don't mind letting you into it. Now those brown raincoats which Mr. and Mrs. H. were wearing only cost one guinea, and look worth three, both Ladies' and Gentlemen's. You must see their 'Napp' Coats; they are—' the perfection of tailoring '—only 305., and you can have a costume, tailor-made and to your measure from 305."</p>
        <p>Mrs. M.: "It seems wonderful, such low prices for garments like you wear—is it pos- sible?"</p>
        <p>Miss D.: "Well, we have dealt there for years, and Jack swears we can't get as good a fit any- where else, and their ' cut 'is elegant. Why, we not only 'save pounds, but are properly tailored into the bargain. . They make Gent's Suits and Overcoats to measure from 205., Costumes from 305., Raincoats from a guinea, and Boys' Suits and Overcoats from is. 11d." Mrs. M.: "Who are they?"</p>
        <p>Miss P.: "Suits Ltd., the Master Tailors. Their local branch is at 17, Horsemarket-street, Warrington. Call, you will save money—and dress as well as Mr. and Mrs. IL do." Mrs. M.: "Thanks very much. I really must have one of their Costumes and Raincoats, and Jack also Junst buy one of their.. Overcoats or Raincoats." "</p>
        <p>Miss D. : "Don't forget the address, dear, 17. .11orsemarket-street, Warringten, the Master Tailors."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>LANCASHIRE VOLUNTEER BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE VOLUNTEER BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>The War Office, in .a letter to the Central Association--:-dated 19th November, 1915—wel- come the pronokil that the me a who en7ist under Lord Derby's scheme for one day with the Colours and are then passed to the Reserve until called up should be encouraged to take advantage of the means of obtaining the military training furnished by the Volunteer Training Corps. Officers commanding units of the Lancashire Volunteer Brigade should therefore offer every facility to .these recruits to obtain training. Such men \rill, join the units as ordinary m6mbers, but subscriptions or entrance fees should, wherever possible, be waived, and they should not be asked to purchase uniforms but should be provided with the G.R. Brassard. Special sections should be formed, drawn from men of a particular section-number under Lord Derby's scheme, so that when men are called U p to join the Colours thercan all join the same regiment and so retain the esprit de corps of their Volunteer Corps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM.</head>
        <p>STOP THAT RHEUMATISM.</p>
        <p>" There is no need to suffer a day longer with your rheumatism," says Dr. Geo. E. Flood, the great Specialist. "The famous Barkola Pre- scription has cured thousands, and will cure you so that you remain cured, if you will let it." Go to your Chemist and ask for a bottle of Barkola Concentrate (get the name right), and tele a few drops four times a day, in water. The pain and swelling subsltie from the. very first dose. Nothing but Barkola Concentrate is needed, as the mixture is complete in itself. It costs as.</p>
        <p>If your ,Chemist cannot supply you, don't waste time, but send postal order for the amount to The Barkola Co., Ltd., 1, Dorset-street, Canon- bury, London, and a bottle 4 will be sent by return.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <head>_ IN FL V\ DEIN FIELDS.</head>
        <p>_ IN FL V\ DEIN FIELDS.</p>
        <p>In Flanders fields the poppies blow Between the crosses, row on row, That mark our place; and in the sky The larks, still bravely singing, fly Scarce heard amid the guns below.</p>
        <p>We are the Dead. Short days ago We lived, felt dawn, saw sunset glow, Loved and were loved, and now we lie In Flanders fields. Take Up our quarrel with the foe: •</p>
        <p>To yuu-from failing hands we throw The torch; be yours to hold it high. If ye break faith with. us who die We shall not sleep, though poppies grow In Flanders Field.—", Punch."</p>
      </div>
