        <p>CYCLIRG AND MOTORING.</p>
        <p>Riding As A Cure For Varicose Veins And Other Troubles.</p>
        <p>HEALTH-GIVING PASTIME.</p>
        <p>,(Queries and correspondence should be addressed to the Editor. Replies cannot be sent by post, and all queries , will be answered in our columns.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>HEALTH-GIVING PASTIME.</head>
        <p>A HEALTH-GIVING PASTIME.</p>
        <p>Recently I had something to say about the many benefits to be derived by those of middle age from the regular use of a cycle. Glancing 4.)ne evening last wpek through the readable little book "Cycling for Health," I came across a very interesting account of how Sir Frank Bowden, Bart., was restored to health. It is one of the_ most striking instances of which I have heard. To use his own words " Not long before I commenced cycling I had returned in a very feeble condition from a tropical English colony, where my constitution bad apparently been irreparably injured by the debilitating climate and two long periods of chrortic diarrhcea with all its attendant evils. 'Finally cured of the principal disease by an old woman's, remedy, after all the doctors in the colony had failed, I arrived in England troubled by an inactive liver, sleeplessness towards morn- ing, bad circulation, varieose veins, .rheurna- tism, hemorrhoids, and general debility, a tendency to- catch cold without any apparent cause, and to perspire at the slightest exertion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0245" n="art0245">
        <head>CURE EFFECTED.</head>
        <p>A CURE EFFECTED.</p>
        <p>"Following a Harrogate doctor's advice, I took to cycling, and kept at it steadily during the winter of 1886 and the early spring of 1887, in and near Arcachon, in the South-west of France. Within three months I had thrown away my elastic stockings, and have never used any since. In four months most of my ills had disappeared, and I finished up my stay in France with a ten 'slays' tour to Bayonne. During my journeys II walked three or four miles daily in this very hilly country, without a reminder, that I had .had varicose veins. Months later, when I stopped cycling for a time they returned with some of the other complaints in a milder form, and doubtless would have become worse had I not driven them away by becoming a regular all-the-year-round cyclist. Before I became a cyclist I often had very painful attacks of rheumatism ; lasting for days, but now I never get any." Who dare assert after this that cycling is not the most healthy and health-giving exercise that there is?</p>
        <p>CLEANING THE BEARINGS.</p>
        <p>In cycling, as in most other pastimes, it is the little, things which matter. For example, there is nothing more destructive to smooth</p>
        <p>1 , ~ In our Service we - • • have a thorough system of inspec- ting repairs so as . to ensure accuracy. . ••••••••••••••• A Then why not . . get our Service • to work for you? ~, .., ~ ir THE COUNTY MOTOR CO. Et St.t.:ckton Heath Garage -WARRINGTON Tel. 63 Stockton Heath. 41, `• VC te, r lila ?</p>
        <p>r,4f, WWI</p>
        <p>,VOLUNTEER SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH v. NOTTINGHAM.</p>
        <p>In the knock-out competition arranged by the Society of Miniature Rifle Clubs through the Central Association, V.T.C., the teams drawn were Stockton Heath Platoon, Cheshire Regi- ment, and No. 1 Co. Nottingham Athletes, Volunteer Force. Each shot on its own range in the presence of an umpire, and the match resulted in a win for the Stockton Heath Platoon by nine points. The conditions were eight men a-side, who had made themselves efficient, 10 shots at a target with a one-inch carton (10), and a two-inch black at 35 yards range, open sights, and no slings. The follow- ing are the individual scores.:— Stockton Heath Volunteers.—A Shepherd 98, R. M. Swinton 97, T. I. Fletcher 96, G. R. Row- son 95, J. F. Ferguson 94, M. A. Piercy 93, E. L. Robinson 90, J. 41. Fletcher 89; total 752. Nottingham Volunteers.-44r. Morris 97, Mr. Whitby 97, Mr. Darby .94, Mr. Whitehorn 94. Mr. Pearce 94, Mr. Walker. 93, Mr. Chapman 93, Mr. Dexter 81; total 743.</p>
        <p>MACKWORTH PRAED CHALLENGE , TROPHY.</p>
        <p>Open to all Voltulteer Corps. First Round.—Warrington Y.T.C., 753; West Bermond 712 ; Warrington' wsey, on by 41 points; Warrington average equal 94.125.</p>
        <p>INTER-COMPANY SHOOT. Warrington Company v. Stockton Heath Corn- Pany.—Ten rounds deliberate; 25 yards, open sights. -Result :—Warrington, 739; Stockton Heath,72l; Warrington won-by 15 points. Wft wington average 92.375.</p>
        <p>No man is happy unless be can prove it. There's many a slip 'twixt the start and the finish.</p>
