        <p>'''R*Ft-e"</p>
        <p>- • laudl• ;V: •g:4 31°- - • •‘• . .• , „ , ' ' "- •ri ".^ • • „... , .4 •-c • ..UT r , . - , atro,l72l)lrj ig :• 11kr;: "' • CYCLE &amp; MOTOR CYCLE TYRES</p>
        <p>are produced by keen business ability, high quality materials, expert labour and scientific machinery, in the Largest RUBBER FACTORY in the BRITISH EMPIRE. It has been the Prime Favourite of Cyclists from the time of its first appedrance. THE NORTH BRITISH RUBBER Co., Ltd.</p>
        <p>53, SOUTH CASTLE STREET, LIVERPOOL. Factories: Castle Mills. Edinburgh,</p>
        <p>running than faulty cone adjustment or a few defective bearings. Of course, a really bad case soon becomes obvious by its melancholy squeak. but there is no need to wait for the audible 3tage. If a bicycle has been in use for several seasons the bearings require a thorough over- hauling. Unscrew the various parts, taking great care that none of the elusive balls escape. Then each can be wiped and examined. Bear- iAgs are quite ineipqpsiv,e; so any doubtful ones should be rejected without hesitation and re- placed. Many amateur mechanics find it no easy matter to restore the balls to their rightful places, but if the ball races are well smeared with grease all difficulty vanishes. In screwing up the cones, it is most desirable to avoid either under or over screwing. Both result in damage by unnecessary friction, and both can be easily avoided by holding the cones in position until the nuts are tightened.</p>
        <p>A MOTOR CYCLIST'S WONDERFUL FEAT</p>
        <p>Everybody realises the immensely important part which motor cyclists have played, and are playing in the war. There is a remarkable story related in a contemporary of the recent exploits of a motor cyclist in France. It is a story that will appeal particularly to all followers of the sport and pastime, and at the same time it proves the daring and pluck of Tom Simpson, a Fulham man, who has just had bestowed upon him the Medallle la Guerre, personally presented by General Joffre. It had reached Simrison's knowledge that fifteen miles from their main body, seven French soldiers had become isolated in a position that, temporarily safe from the Hun onslaught con- demned the men to the pangs of starvation. It looked as though they would have to be sacri- ficed, as the only road to the isolated position was fully exposed to 'heavy shelling and rifle fire of the 'foe. But Simpson decided to take the risk of reaching his French comrades, al- though he was told that, unless by some miracle, the attempt would be futile—it meant certain death. Loading his side car with the neces- saries for relief, :Simpson started on his task, and marvellous to relate, passed safety through a curtain of bullets and shrapnel that spread over the full fifteen miles! He succoured the sequestered seven, and actually rode back to the main body unharmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0248" n="art0248">
        <head>HEDGE TRIMMING AND TREE PRUNING</head>
        <p>HEDGE TRIMMING AND TREE PRUNING</p>
        <p>Mr. W. E. Riche, the secretary of the Roads Improvement Association, draws attention to the fact that now is the time for hedge-trimming and tree-pruning. Owners of, and residents in, property having road frontage, especially at corners and junctions are particularly asked, in the interests of public safety, to have all over- hanging or view-obstructing hedges and branches cut down. This work is exceptionally urgent and important this year consequent upon military traffic at night, and the general reduc- tion of lighting throughout the country, and the fact that many wad improvements, which would in most cases have obliterated all danger, have been postponed. The Roads Improvement Association has found property owners very reasonable in meeting the needs of the public in this matter, although, curiously enough, they themselves are frequently unaware of any danger until their attention is directed to it. Road users, therefore, who are acquainted with any places made dangerous by the overgrowing of trees, shrubs, &amp;c., are invited to send full details to Mr. Riche, 15, Dartmouth-street, Westminster. All reports will be carefully in- vestigated, and such action as may appear desirable taken to remove danger.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0249" n="art0249">
        <head>PETROL WASTAGE.</head>
        <p>PETROL WASTAGE.</p>
        <p>,Owing to the exorbitant prices which are now being charged for petrol it behoves every user</p>
        <p>to exercise-- the greatest economy. It -would doubtless surprise many to learn that probably quite a large proportion of the spirit which they buy and pay for so dearly, is not used in pro- pelling the machine at all. There are, a score of ways in which petrol is wasted. The most common cause is a leaky petrol pipe. Very often if the pipe immediately below the tap is examined it will be foupd to be damp. This means that there is a leak somewhere, and it should receive attention at once. Don't forget that a leak goes on all day and all night, so that, however small it may be, there must be a con- sidefable wastage. A faulty carburetter, is like- wise a very frequent source of trouble. If there is a leak, or if the carburetter is at fault, it ,stould receive immediate attention. A tem- porary repair of the former may be made with the aid of soap plastered round the leak. But this should only be regarded as a temporary measure, not as a permanent repair. Some motorists are very careless in filling their tanks, and often waste enough to drive the car five or six miles. Excess flooding of the carburetter is another frequent source of wastage. All that is necessary—and this only sometimes—is to hold the " tickler " down for a second, slightly to raise the level of the petrol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ADULT SCHOOL BILLIARD LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Games: 6 players; 75 up. P. W. L. F. A. Pts. Sankey A. 5.2 2 0 845 712 • 4</p>
