      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>Vkt</head>
        <p>Vkt</p>
        <p>. - - . , . - , 1.T151 . . . _ , . • . . .</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 11, 1915. IN THE COURTS. I BRAVERY REWARDED.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY MORNING SCENE.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Monday, be- fore the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Aldermen Smethurst and Bolton, and Messrs. S. Wright and W. Furness,, , Albert Clark and Andrew I Monks, both labourers, residing at 35, Hoyle-street, War- rington, were charged with being drunk on Sunday in Haydock-street. Monks was further charged with assaulting a constable. The police evitience showed that at 12.10 on Sunday morning the prisoners were kicking and banging a door in Haydock-street. When arrested by a constable, Monks struck him a blow in the back of the neck and several on the ribs. With assistance, the prisoners were taken to the Police Station. _ _</p>
        <p>Clark expressed his regret and offered to sign the pledge. Clark was fined 10s. and Monks 19s. 6d. The alternatives were 7 days and 14 days respec- tively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>105th TIME.</head>
        <p>THE 105th TIME.</p>
        <p>Brothers Competing For An Unenviable Record.</p>
        <p>Patrick Tracey, of no fixed abode, was charged at Tuesday's Borough Police Court with being drunk in Bridge-street on Monday. The Chief Constable said that this was the 105th time that the prisoner had been before a Bench of magistrates. The last time was in May, when he was sentenced to six months' im- prisonment. Ile was endeavouring to beat his brother at Salford, who had been sentenced a few times more than he.</p>
        <p>Tracey was ordered to find two sureties of £5 each or go to prison for two months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>"BRAG."</head>
        <p>"BRAG."</p>
        <p>Under A Gas Lamp On A Frosty Night.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, be- fore Councillor Henry Roberts (presiding), Messrs. J. Rymer Young, T. Locker, C. R. Burgess and W. T. Williams, Arthur Emery, ironworker, of 190, Wellfield- street, Warrington, and Arthur Charnall, iron- worker, of 79, Kimberley-street, Warrington, were summoned for playing a game of chance, namely, " brag," on November 25th, in Powys- street.</p>
        <p>They pleaded guilty. The evidence showed that the two defendants. with' another man, were playing " brag " at five minutes past midnight under a gas lamp on a frosty night. A constable watched them play three games. The third man got away. The defendants were fined 15s. each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>SOLDIERS' CLOTIIES.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' CLOTIIES.</p>
        <p>The Danger Of Buying Unsaleable Articles.</p>
        <p>William Ward, a labourer, of 59, Orford-lane. Warrington, was summoned at Tuesday's Borough Court for buying from Alexander Suchlock (a soldier), a jersey and also for assisting Suchlock to sell a pair of boots. A police officer said that on November 29th he went to the defendant's house to take the soldier, who was an absentee, into custody. The soldier was without boots, and said that the defendant had taken them and sold them. At first the defendant denied buying the jergey, but lathr admitted buying it for is. The def- fendant also sold the boots for 4s. 641. and gave the soldier 3s. 641. The defendant denied that he knew the boots were Army boots, and also denied that he made is. profit.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable pointed out that the military _authorities suffered considerably from the sale of military equipment. The defendant was ,fined 14s. on each sum- mons, the alternative being 14 days' imprison- ment on each summons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>TOO BAD FOR A GERMAN.</head>
        <p>TOO BAD FOR A GERMAN.</p>
        <p>A "Yorkshire Lad" And His Suspicious Calls.</p>
        <p>Larrett Parkin, a collier, of no settled abode, was charged at Wednesday's Borough Police Court with loitering in Winwick-road on November 30th with intent. to commit a felony. He pleaded not guilty. Relating the facts of the case, the Chief Con- stable said that the prisoner was before the Bench tht previous week on a charge of begging. The charge was then adjourned and inquiries had be made concerning his antecedents. At about ro'clock on November 30th the prisoner went into the Red Lion Hotel, Winwick-road. He there observed three men in the place and then asked the landlord if he could have a gill of beer for Id. At this time he had Is. Bd. in Ihis possession. He next visited the Old Ball Inn. He saw two soldiers and asked for 2d. The next place he visited was the shop of Mrs. Hudson, a provision dealer, of Winwick-road. He found Mrs. Hudson in the shop and asked for a copper, saying that he was a miner going to Wales. She declined to give him anything, and they next heard of him at the Bay Horse "Hotel. There, he saw three men at the bottom of the stairs and asked them or id. for a gill of beer. This man could only have one inten- tion in going to these various places, and that was an intent to commit a fglony. Evidence bearing out this statement was given by Mr. Joseph Edwards, of the Red Lion Hotel; a barmaid from the Old Ball Inn; Mrs. Hudson. of Winwick-road ; Mr. Roberts, of the Bay Horse Hotel, and Detective-Sergeant Reddington. It was stated that when arrested the prisoner had in his possession a silver-plated " Oxo " spoon, which he said that he had fund. The prisoner said that he was willing to join the Army if the Beneh would alloNi him, and claimed to be " a Yorkshire lad." The ,Chief Constable said that the prisoner's record was so bad that they would not have him in the . Army. There were 13 previous con- victions against him, most of which were of a serious nature. The Chief. Constable added . " They would not have him in the German 1 Army, let alone the British." The prisoner was sentenced to three months' hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>SERVANT'S THEFT.</head>
        <p>SERVANT'S THEFT.</p>
        <p>Failed To Profit By A Previous Warning.</p>
        <p>Theresa Goddard, a domestic servant, pleaded guilty at the Warrington Borough Court on Wednesday to stealing a number of articles, valued 355., the property of Mrs. Stokes, of 1, AThert-street. The Chief Constable 'Stated that some ten weeks ago the prisoner went to the house of Mrs. Stokes, and the later took her in. On November 4th Mrs. Stokes went out and left the prisoner on the premises. When she returned at night she found the property. missing. On Sunday last a police officer visited a certain, house in the town and there the girl admitted that she had taken- the goods. .She had on a previous occasion been before \the Bench on a charge of stealing a gold ring. The magistrates on that occasion let her off. A fine of 20s. was inflicted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>CHILD NEGLECT.</head>
        <p>CHILD NEGLECT.</p>
        <p>Absent Soldier's Wife Sent To Gao For Three Months.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, Jane Patten, of 99. Guardian-street, Warring- ton, formerly of 3, Heaton-s'quare, Offord-street, pleaded guilty to neglecting her two children. Mr. Arthur Browne, who prosecuted for the N.S.P.C.C., said that the defendant was the wife of A soldier, who was at the Front. The children were now at a home in _West Derby. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Dr. Peacocke said that he found the children's room in a filthy condition. There was no cover- ing of any kind in spite of the inclement weather. The conditions would cause an</p>
        <p>Continued in Next Column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>Plucky Rescues At The</head>
        <p>The Plucky Rescues At The</p>
        <p>Scotland-road Fire.</p>
        <p>BOY'S CANAL DIVE.</p>
        <p>At the opening of the Warrington Town Council meeting on Tuesday night, The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) handed certificates and cheques to six men for gal- lantry in saving two children from a burning shop in Scotland-road last July. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The rewards, made by the Society for the Protection of Life from Fire, were : Percy Allanson, certificate and cheque for £3 35.; Sergeant F. Clegg, silver medal and cheque for £2 25.; Police-Sergeant Kenwright, silver medal and cheque for £2 25.; J. W. Armstrong, cheque for .21 is.; Thomas •Bramhall, cheque for £1 is.; W. Bridge, certificate. His Worship also handed over the Royal Humane Society's medal to a fifteen-year-old boy, Thomas Miller, for an attempt to rescue a boy from drowning in the canal. All the recipients were applauded as the Mayor praised them and presented the awards. It will be noted that two members of the Police Force and three other men were in- volved in the Scotland-road rescue. One of the latter, for some reason, did not get a cheque in addition to the certificate; but those who did subscribed half a sovereign among them and presented it to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>HOW TWO LIVES WERE SAVED.</head>
        <p>HOW TWO LIVES WERE SAVED.</p>
        <p>The Fire puperintendent's report, said the Mayor, was as follows : "The fire occurred in a small attic in the occupation of Mr. A._Rutter. It was filled with stationers' paper, bookbinders' stock, and fire- works, and was caused by children playing with matches there. On hearing the cries of the children, their mother (Mrs. Rutter) rushed upstairs to try and save them, but she was beaten back by the heat of the flames and the dense and overpowering smoke, her hair and bodice being on fire. The flames were extin- guished at great personal risk by W. Bridge. Three men (Messrs. P. Allanson, J. W. Arm- strong and Thomas Bramhall), who were work- ing on some new buildings .adjoining, were quickly on the scene, but when they attempted to get upstairs they were driven back by the dense smoke. -They then tried by means of ladders to enter the front and rear bedrooms, but could not succeed. At this stage they were joined by Sergeant Clegg, of the. Fire Brigade, and Sergeant Kenwright, of the Borough Police Force. "Finding themselves unable to effect an en- trance through the bedroom window, the men turned their attention to a skylight in the roof. This they smashed, and Allanson got inside the attic, which was still full of smoke, and finding one of the children he passed him through the skylight to Armstrong, who carried the child down to safety. Meanwhile, both , Clegg and Kenwright had joined Allanson in the attic, notwithstanding that it was still a source of great danger. "Kenwright asked if all the children had been rescued, and Allanson answered "Yes, one." Kenwright then said, "There is an- other," and Clegg, after groping through the smoke, pushed open the door of another small room and discovered the second child, and with Kenwright's assistance handed him through the skylight to Bramhal/, who carried him down. In order to effect the rescue it was necessary for the rescuers to wear cloths round their nostrils and mouths, and the promptitude with which the men got to work was undoubt- edly the means of saving the children's lives. (Applause.) The risk the men ran was con- siderable." (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>BOY'S PLUCKY ACT.</head>
        <p>BOY'S PLUCKY ACT.</p>
        <p>The Mayor also presented Thomas Miller, aged .15 years, the son of Mr. William Miller, of 3, Beatrice-street, Warrington, with a certi- ficate of the Royal .Humane Society. Thomas Miller, who is an apprentice moulder, was playing in Myrtle-grove, close to the Old Quay Canal bank, Warrington, on Sunday afternoon, October 24th, at about 4.30, along with two other boys, named George Welsh and Joseph Leslie, when a girl named Josephine Leslie ran up to them and said, "Hurry , up, there is a little lad in the canal drowning." They all ran to the indicated spot, and Saw the boy in about the middle of the canal, which would be about seven to eight yards from the bank. He appeared to be com- pletely exhausted and was struggling very faintly. Miller had already taken, his jacket and waistcoat off whilst running to the spot, and the boys who were with him helped to strip him. Just before he dived in the boy threw up his arms and looked as if he was sinking. Miller dived in, swam out to him, got hold of the back of him, and brought him to the side. George Welsby and Joseph Leslie got hold of the boy and dragged him out, and afterwards assisted Miller out of the water. The latter.afterwards dressed himself, and two young men came up and started using artificial respiration. Shortly afterwards Dr. Jago canie up and said the boy was dead.</p>
        <p>'The Mayor commented that whilst the child's</p>
        <p>life was not saved, the attempt reflected great personal credit on Miller, who at personal risk tried to save the child. (Applause.) Councillor Gough said that he had great pleasure in moving a vote of thanks to the societies who had recognised the gallantry of these men and of Miller. It was very pleasing to know that they had men in the town of such gallantry. (Hear, hear.) Dr. Joseph (the deputy-mayor) seconded, and the motion was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>Many a man attracts no more attention than a thermometer on a pleasant day.</p>
        <p>The Farm Labourer's Joke. The alien enemies interned at a certain camp often play jokes on farm labourers who pass their barbed-wire entanglements. The other morning a specially stalwart ploughman passed along the road, and one German, thinking to have a laugh at him, shouted out : "Kitchener vants you! Kitchener vants you!" "What's that?" asked the countryman, turning in surprise. "Kitchener vants you!" said the German again, in great glee. "Does he? Well, he's jolly well got you," replied the countryman, with a grin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.—Continued.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.—Continued.</p>
        <p>mense amount of. suffering. Their backs had broken out through vermin and filth. The defendant maintained that the children were well fed awl that she paid another woman to look after them whilst she went to work. She was sentenced to three months' imprison- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>EMBEZZLEMENT CHARGE.</head>
        <p>EMBEZZLEMENT CHARGE.</p>
        <p>Vanman Who Disappeared With £,7.</p>
        <p>Wm. Frederick Bond, of Wimborne, Dorset- shire, pleaded guilty at the Borough Police Court on Friday to.embezzling sum of money belonging to the Latchford Steam Laundry. Mr. W. H. Swallow, who prosecuted, said that the prisoner was engaged by the foreman as a vanman in June last. His duties were to de- liver and collect parcels and to collect weekly accounts from customers. On June 28th he collected about £7, and he had been missing since June 29th. At that time a week's wage was due to him, which made the actual deficit £5 17s. 11d. Bond afterwards surrendered to the Dorset police. He was fined £5, the alternative being one month's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>JUVENILE OFFENDERS.</head>
        <p>JUVENILE OFFENDERS.</p>
        <p>Fire boys were summoned at the Juvenile Court on Tuesday for stealing a number of packets of cigarettes from a shop at 37, Guardian-street, Warrington. The cigarettes were said to have been taken on Sunday after- noon. They were each ordered to receive four strokes of the birch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>w4MI</head>
        <p>w4MI</p>
        <p>• - ••• • - • '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Fog And Rain Interfere With</p>
        <p>Fixtures.</p>
        <p>The rain and fog which prevailed practically all over the North of England on Saturday spoiled many of the fixtures of the Northern Union. In one or two instances the match- was never started, and in other cases short time was played. Only fifty minutes was played at Runcorn, where, in spite of a heavy downpour of pain, the Runcorn and Leigh teams turned out The teams were very evenly matched, and; the game resulted in a pointless draw. Your old War- ringtonians turned out with the Runcorn* team. They were Jolley (full-back), Baker (three- quarter), Daintith (half-back), and l'earnley (forward). The results of the matches prayed are as follows:</p>
        <p>Batley, 14 points; Halifax, 0 points. Bramley, 16; Hull Kingston R., 0. Bradford, 6; Brighouse, 2. Leeds, 11; Huddersfield, 2. Hunslet, 8; Hull, 8. Oldham, 2; Wigan, 2. Rochdale H., 7 Broughton R., 0. Runcorn, 0; Leigh, 0.</p>
        <p>NTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>Matches Again Abandoned.</head>
        <p>Matches Again Abandoned.</p>
        <p>Owing to the inclement weather on Saturday the matches of the Intermediate League which should have been played on the Wilderspool ground and at Victoria Park were—for the second week in succession—cancelled.</p>
        <p>The League table therefore remains as last week:—</p>
