      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <head>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>HEATHSIDE v. SACRED HEART.</p>
        <p>Floathside : Griffiths; Lingard, McElroy, Rut- ter and Foster; Dillon, Varley or Bate-; Dare, Harter, Booth, Herbert, Dolphin and Dowling. Reserves.—Bacon and Guest.</p>
        <p>BEAMONT v. ST. JAMES'.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool. Beamont : Barnett; Bagul(--, Tranter, A. Makin and P. Hill; W. Titchard and W. Witton; H. Pemberton, S. Pemberton, T. Percival (captain), J. Iligham, F. Jones and S. Lunt.</p>
        <p>Reserves.—J. Nolan, A. Starkey and A. :ewitt. •</p>
        <p>BEAMONT JUNIORS v. ST. JAMES' JUNIORS. At Victoria Park. Kick-off 2.30. Beamont : J. Anderton ; 'E. Makin, W Brookes, A. Acourt and J. Darbyshire; J. Cole man and R. Bibbey; J. Higgins, H. Monks IN Smith (captain), G. Armitage, C. B. Healey am: A. Hackett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>A Wet Afternoon And Only One</p>
        <p>Game.</p>
        <p>Last Saturday rain caused all the fixtures in the Association 4st to be cancelled with one ception, namely, Emmanuel In,stitute and the R.A.M.C. from Chester. These two teams braved the elements and played their fixture in Victoria Park, the ground being in a very sodden state. Rain was falling all the time that the game was in progress. Under the circumstances some good play was shown, the visitors being a shade the better lot, and the final score of four goals against three in their favour fairly repre- sented the run of the game.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Association Club and the boffins Green players assembled at the head- marters of the first named club, and after wait-</p>
        <p>ing a reasonable time to see if there would be any prospects of playing, the captains agreed to postpone the game until to-day. The game will be played on the Hood-lane ground this afternoon.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League the Warrington Central and the British Aluminium were the only clubs engaged in this competition. Owing 'to the non-appeaTance of ttn official referee the representatives of the clubs soon decided to postpone the fixture to a future date to be arranged.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District Junior League here were no games played to a definite issue.</p>
        <p>The Mersey Juniors and the Stockton Heath Scouts. met on the Arpley grounds. Neither club was fully represented, the teams having only eight players each. A start was made and at the end of ten minutes' play Walters scored for the Juniors. A few minutes later it was decided to abandon the game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the above league was held at headquarters last Tuesday night, Mr. Simpson presiding over a full attendance of delegates.</p>
        <p>The Secretary reported that owing to the bad weather there were no games played last Satur- day, and the fixtures were arranged for January 15th.'</p>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCH.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. CHESTER R.A.M.C.</p>
        <p>Played at:Victoria Park in wietelied weather. Holt kicked off for the visitors, who were the first to? become aggressive.: In the first few ! minutes the home goal was in danger, but Crozier cleared well. Several attacks were made by the Institute forwards, and from one of these Parr sent the ball along to Oakes, who, after beating Grimshaw, sent in a good shot, which gave Kay no chance, thus opening the home team's account. Aroused by this early reverse, the soldiers attacked 'hotly, but the sound de- fensive play of the home backs kept them at bay; For a time Emmanuel had slightly the best of Matters, but were unable to make much impression on the visitors' backs. The Institute left-wing broke away, and after the forwards had 'dribbled well up the field, Stubbs secured end, with a fine shot fram 30 yards' range, left the visiting custodian helpless, and added a second. The home team now began to take Matters easy; and the soldiers seized aAgood opportunity on the left wing. A good shift re- sulted in the ball hitting the upright and de-</p>
      </div>
