        <p>Ward Teas to Provide Comforts for Soldiers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>NEED OF THRIFT.</head>
        <p>THE NEED OF THRIFT.</p>
        <p>The good work that the War Sewing Classes of the Warrington Women's Liberal Association are doing was amply demonstrated en Monday and Tuesday at the John Crosfield Memorial Hall. On each day a tea—followed by a concert —was held, and large ,numbers. of ladies were present. The object of the teas was to raise funds with which to meet the expenditure ot the War Sewing Class. _ _</p>
        <p>Monday's tea was organised by Mrs. Halliwell, the *Orford Ward captain, who was ably assisted by the ward auxiliaries. Sir Arthur and Lady Crosfield were to have been present, but owing to their detention in London on mili- tary hospital and other• business they were unable to come. Their places were well filled by Mr. C. E. Parker, president of the Liberal Association, and .Mrs. Parker. After the tea had been served Mrs. Parker took the chair, and presided over a room full of ladies.</p>
        <p>and reproducing the' necessities of life con- stantly being consumed. If this property of the manufacturer was taken wealth would vanish, and the trade of the country. would soon be brought to a standstill. :Although we were one of the richest countries in the world, the money in the bank was only a small proportion of the wealth of the country. We must' clearly pay for the war out of our savings, that could only be made by keeping our shipping and our manufactures in fullest • occupation. lie thought the country which could hold on the. 'longest financially was going to 'win the war. He did not believe in people stinting them- selves of those things which were necessary, but it was essential that they should save, and not spend money on things which were unneces- sary. By so dohng the people would help their country to bring about victory. Care of Belgians.</p>
        <p>At Christmas envelopes would be distributed and collections would be taken on behalf of the Belgians who were still resident in this country. About three million were left in Belgium, and although the Germans had destroyed the Belgian cities and the means of livelihood, the Germans would not provide food for them. During the evening a number of vocal items were contributed by Miss Hewitt, the Misses Jones, Miss Perdue, Mr. Fieldhouse and Mr. Davies. All were well sung and were heartily received by the audience, several items being encored. • Miss Wardle. was a most efficient accompanist. Just before the close of the proceedings Mrs. Parker presented Mrs. 'Beswick, of Orford- avenue with a plant, given by Mrs. James Critchley, and won in a draw.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>FIVE YEARS AGO.</head>
        <p>FIVE YEARS AGO.</p>
        <p>In a short, but heipiui address, Mrs. Parker, who was received with loud applause, said five years ago they were in the midst of a big struggle in an endeavour to get Sir Arthur Crosfield to the head of the poll, and the series of teas then held was a big affair. Some of them expected at that. time that the struggle would be repeated at about the present day, but it had not proved so, and they all knew the reason why. They were now engaged in a far greater struggle, and had to do all they could to bring that struggle to a successful con- clusion. They had this added interest: they were confident that, this time, they were going to win. (Applause.) They knew that England would be victorious and would settle down to her former happiness again.</p>
        <p>'Lady Crosfield And One's First Duty.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Parker went on to say that she (lid not think that there was a woman present who had not some near relation at the war, and then, explaining the reason for Lady Crosfiel(Ps absence, she read the following letter, which had been addresSed to Mrs. Osborne from the Highgate V.A.D. Hospital, London : " I was deeply grieved to have to telegraph you as I did yesterday (December 3rd) evening. It is only at the shortest of short notice that we have learned from the War office that 22 patients (two . more of them have just been added), are coming into the hospital next Mon- day. In my capacity as Commandant it is really absolutely necessary that I should be there when they arrive and remain there all next week. I had so looked forward to meeting all our friends at the meetings to be held in the Memorial Hall next week, that I am ever so sorry and disappointed that I cannot be present to see and welcome them. But I think that you and they will agree that one's first duty at such a time as this is to do all one can for the bene- fit and comfort of those of our wounded com- mitted to our charge. We have all been doing our very best at the hospital, and the authorities are more than pleased with it. Dr. Garrett Anderson, who is at the head of the Central Hospital to which we are affiliated inspected our auxiliary hospital this afternoon and was loud in its praise. I send warmest good wishes to all our friends who are present at the meetings next week, and my great regret is that circum- stances prevent my being with them. lam sure these meetings will be as great a success—and will be as heartily enjoyed—as the meetings held previously. And I am sure that the artistes will be as kind as ever and contribute—as they always ,do—immensely to their enjoyment. Hoping very much that you are feeling better and that you will not tax your strength too much in the future.—Yours sincerely, Domini Crosfield."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0300" n="art0300">
        <head>Messages of Approval and Sympathy.</head>
        <p>Messages of Approval and Sympathy.</p>
        <p>Continuing, Mrs. Parker said .that as Lady Crosfield could not be spared from such a useful position, she was sure thaVhe meeting did not wish to bring her divay; , At Mrs. Parker's sugOsiiori, it was agreed to send a message to Laditfosfield telling her how heartily the meeting approved of the work that she was doing. ,</p>
        <p>It was also agreed to ,send a message of sym- pathy and good wishes to Mrs. Osborne, who, owing to indisposition, was unable to be pre- sent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0301" n="art0301">
        <head>Prize Knitters.</head>
        <p>Prize Knitters.</p>
        <p>At this stage Mrs. Parker presented a prize to Mrs. HOfton, of West-street, for knitting the best pair of soldiers' socks; 'Mrs. Horton has knitted 45 pairs of socks sine the commence- ment of the war.</p>
        <p>Speaking a few words on the work of the Sew- ing Classes, Mrs. Parker said that the teas were held not to talk politics, but to encourage people to make comforts for soldiers. One of the ways that they 'could all help in the war was by pro- viding comforts for the brave boys which were not provided by the Government. The working class was held every Monday at,2.30.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0302" n="art0302">
        <head>WOMAN'S BURDEN.</head>
        <p>THE WOMAN'S BURDEN.</p>
        <p>in the evening</p>
        <p>Mr.-- Parker addressed the meeting. He con- gratulated -Mrs. Halliwell, Mrs. O'Brien and the other ladies upon the excellent meeting. It was . very . nice to hold such gather- ings and to be able to meet and listen to pleasant songs *hen there was sO•much to de- press them. The thing that had struck him. about the war was the inequality of the incidence of the burden. - On some it had fallen very heavily, on • others very lightly. There were a great number of people who, in their finances,- possessed less than beforq; then there were a good nuinber,who had more money than they had before. Some had the heaviest burden of all—to take the risk of sending those who were the nearest and dearest to them to the Front. (Hear,. hear.) -He often thought that it. was not those 'at the-Front who had the heaviest burden to bear; there was a certain amount of excitement in warfare that took the thoughts away from danger.. In this country ,the heaviest burden was, being borne by the women who were left at home. They were fear- ful that every, day some message would come telling -them-that.lheir men were wounded—or perhaps worse. He. thought ,that the women of England had done their part nobly in this great strife. When they 'thought of what was hap- pening in so many ways, they realised, how bravely that burden had been borne. He thought that the women. had the right to pride themselves on the way that they .were concealing their grief and the brave manner in which they were bearing the weight which was pressing so heavily upon them. . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0303" n="art0303">
        <head>Good Effect of the War.</head>
        <p>The Good Effect of the War.</p>
        <p>• Speaking of the effects of the war, Mr. Parker said that, it would be many years before the country , returned to the , prosperity of the early autumn of, 1914, but the war that had done such terrible harm had brought out more sacrifice and more sympathy than any other thing.' There had been a great demonstration of that sisterliness, and that brotherliness by which all classes were helping each cther. If they , could only 'make this, not a passing phase' in' their life, but to make it a perpetual habit of their lives to help 'others to bear their burdens, then he thought something had been done td .make up the ravages of war.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0304" n="art0304">
        <head>Germany Getting Worn-out.</head>
        <p>Germany Getting Worn-out.</p>
        <p>He wished he could tell them when the war would .be over; hut there were.. signs that Ger- many. was getting worn out.. • 'Though he did not think it would be over in It month or two, be would hp very much surprised over next summer. He did not think it was !possible for the ,Powers to go on longer than that; the money would not last. No country, not •.even our own, could go .on very long at the present. irate. of. expenditure. All • over the 'country it was recognised as.-a:.fact that we lad to save. He was glad to. hear that a savings bank had, been stated by their sewing classes. It was said by some that the rich men ought to pay for the war. They Could not dO so as theii.' money was "locked up." The wealth of the rich was not in Money; it was ,irtainlY. tied' up in firms from which it could Mit be released Without national ruin. It was represented almost wholly by land, wheat, ships and workinx capital, of various kinds. !One Jtian :possessed leather ana the'applianceS and tteconimodation for making more leather; other possessed goods and similar facilities for putting —their capital to reproductive 'purposes. and so , they succeeded iii keeping:uptheprOductive capacity</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0305" n="art0305">
        <head>TOWN HALL AND BEWSEY WARDS.</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL AND BEWSEY WARDS.</p>
        <p>. Tuesday evening's gathering, which was held for the Bewsey and Town Hall Wards Sewing Classes, was quite as successful as that held on Monday. Again an excellent tea was served to a large company, followed by a concert which reached as high a standard as that of the pre- vious evening. The Ward Captains, Mrs. Collin (Town Hall) and Mrs. Nurse (Bewsey), had charge of the arrangements. Mrs. C. E. Parker, who presided, expressed her great pleasure at being able to oceupy thq chair.' After Lady Crosfield's letter had been read, the meeting" agreed to send a message to the llighgate Hospital telling her that the meeting was proud that Lady Crosfield held such an honourable position as Commandant. A message of good wishes was also sent to Mrs. Osborne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0306" n="art0306">
        <head>PRIZE WINNERS.</head>
        <p>PRIZE WINNERS.</p>
        <p>The chief thing, said Mrs. Parker, that the women had to do to-day was to sympathise with each other. Many of them probably had some- one who was dear to .them out at the Front. The women had to remain behind; they, who could not go out to fight, had to work and keep their homes going, and in that way serve their country., (Applause.) The pirizes for the best knitted socks were won as follows:</p>
        <p>Town Hall Ward.—Mrs. Lingard (prize given by Councillor Lindley). A special prize given by Mr. C. E. Parker (president of the Liberal Association), was won by Mrs. Holding. Bewsey Ward.—Mrs. Bennett (prize given by Councillor Gough). During the evening a short address was given by Mr. C. E. Parker, who emphasised the need for thrift.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the musical programme —which was contributed to by Miss Perdue. Miss Winstanley, Miss May Isherwood and Mr. Geo. Walker, COUNCILLOR LINDLEY AND THE NEED FOR THRIFT.</p>
        <p>Councillor Gough, in moving a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Parker, said he was glad to see so many fellow-workers of the Town Hall and Bewsey Wards present. Mr. ad Mrs. Parker had materially helped to find the wool to make these "comforts;" which were very necessary for the men at the Front. The artistes who had entertained them were second to none in Warrington. He was glad to know that they had so many good fellow-workers and so many helpful artistes.</p>
        <p>Seconding this motion, Councillor Lindley thanked the artistes and all who had helped to provide the evening's, enjoyment, especially Mrs. parker, who had lent valuable assistance as an accompanist. Mr. Parker's lecture on thrift was very interesting, but he (the speaker) would go further and express the opinion the, Government would force the mole to 1,,N more thrifty by taking away som7 of their luxuries, lie thanked all who had assisted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0307" n="art0307">
        <head>TIME FOR SYMPATHY.</head>
        <p>A TIME FOR SYMPATHY.</p>
        <p>The motion was carried with loud applause. . In reply, Mr. Parker expressed his apprecia- tion of the efforts of the artistes. With regard to ,Mrs. Parker and himself, he did not think Mrs. Parker would have been present under ordinary circumstances. She was there becauso she thought it a time when we should draw together and strive to help each other in service and in sacrifice.</p>
        <p>Asking the audience to sing the National Anthem, Mr. Parker reminded them that the King was the personal representative of this country, and that when they sang "God Save the King" they were really asking for the suc- cess of the country. The preceedings terminated with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0308" n="art0308">
        <head>GRAMMAR SCHOOL OLD SOY'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>GRAMMAR SCHOOL OLD SOY'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Cadets Attend Funeral.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the death occurred of Master Frank Earle, son of Mr. and Mrs. James S. Earle, of the Brooklands Hotel, Lovely-lane, Warrington. He was 16 years of age, and was educated at the Boteler Grammar School, which he left a short time ago, to begin work in the offices of Messrs. J. Crosfield and Sons, Ltd. Indeed, he had only been at work for about a fortnight when he was seized with sudden ill- ness, and died from appendicitis in a local nursing home. He was a member of the Grammar School Cadet Corps. Amidst scenes of deep • sorrow and in the presence of a large number of relatives and friends, the funeral took place •on Thursday afternoon. The coffin Was borne from the house to St. Barnabas' Church by eight members of .the Grammar- School Cadet Corps, about 70 of whom were present. At the church the first part -of the funeral service was read by the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth (vicar of St. Barnabas'), the Rev. Horace Gray (headmaster of the Grammar School) assisting. The opening sentences were ,read by .the Vicar, and the hymn "Hark, my -soul,- it' is: the Lord," was sung. The burial psalms were sung, after which the Rev. Horace Gray read the lesson. The hymn "Jesu, Lover of my Soul," was sung. The Vicar read the prayers, and Mr. ' Darby (the Parish Church organist) played Chopin's Funeral March. The ,choir, which was in attendance, .was composed of lads from the Grammar School Cadet Corps. At the graveside the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth read the committal Prayer. -• • • The chief -mOurners were :—Father and Mother, J. J. Earle and Sydney Earle (brothers), Mr. and Mrs. A. Roby (brother-in- law and sister), Mrs. Dearden (aunt), Mr. and I Mrs. Fred Adams '(uncle and aunt), Mr. and Mrs. P. H. Brown. (uncle and aunt), Mr. W. Smith (Uncle), Reg. Brown. (cousin), Mr. Sam. Plumpton.- . Beautiful floral tributes were reeeived from: Father and Mother, 'Brothers and Sisters, " Arthuf and Lily," "Aunt Jenny and Ethel," "Aunt Alice and Fred," "Aunt Sally and family," "Uncle I. Smith and family" (St. 'Helens), "Cousins Connie and Elinor," Head Teachers and Staff, Boteler Grammar School; Boys of Form IV., Boteler Grammar School; Mr. E. Burrows, "Billy and Arthur," Mr. and. Mrs. T. Partridge,. Mr. and Mrs. A. .Denhani; Mr. and Mrs. T. Throssell. Mr. T. Foster, Mr. G. Plumpton and. family, Mr. and Mrs. Hutch- in.;Mi, Mr. and Mrs. Watts. "His Football Friends,". S. H. Smith and Friends, • and Miss Elsie Reid. •.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <head>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>" THE PEARL GIRL."</p>
        <p>Another Courtneidge Production At The Royal Court.</p>
        <p>As the name implies, this successful and bril- liant musical comedy is all about the most precious of gems, yet truly there can be pearls in more sense than one; the chorus and prin- cipal ladies connected with this most pleasing production are veritably of the best. During the whole of the week crowded houses have been the order and no wonder, for the spectacle presented is quite in accordance with the usual merit of . this famous producer. Courtneidge gpeaks for itself, and habitues of the popular Royal Court were not disappointed. On looking through the programme'one noticed old musical comedy favourites who have visited the town previously, one in particular being Mr. Aubrey Millward, who played "Slippery Jack" in "The Marriage Market" some twelve months ago. On this occasion the scope for this artiste's talent was very limited, which is to be regretted in view of his charm of manner and fine singing, and we should have liked him to occupy the boards oftener. As the Duke of Trent he performed very creditably, as did Mr. Lamont Dickson as Robert Jaffray, his land agent. The duet with Miss Frances Tritchler (who took the part of Lady Betty) entitled "Tip a Tap" was quite one of the successes of the evening's entertainment. Mr. Raymond Gasc,oyne, as Mr. Jocks, manager of the Palmyra Pearl Shop, gave to the audience a splendid character study of the uses of advertisement which, combined with an element of clean and wholesome humour, placed him in the front rank of this production. That famous comedian, Mr. James Salter, as Byles, messenger and odd man at the shop, was first- rate. He is a worthy successor of Alfred Lester, the premier comedian in the London Company. Later in the piece he takes the part of Lopez, a Spaniard. and it was in this character that he shone best.</p>
        <p>The principal lady was Miss May Tomlinson, as Miranda Peploe, secretary and typist to the Bond-street establishment, who is persuade.l by the audacious Mr. Jocks to masquerade eventually as Madame Alvarez, the wealthy South American, whom everyone is desirous of meeting. Her pleasing manner, fine voice and easy grace on the stage being delightful, it was no wonder that when the curtain fell the applause was unstinted and merited. Another artiste of exceptional ability is Migs Eva well, who capitally portrayed the vivacious Mrs. Baxter-Browne. She reminded us very forcibly of Mrs. Smith of " Arcadian " fame, and unless we are mistaken has been associated with this other famous Courtneidge production. "We raise our hats to you, Mrs. Double B." As usual there was a fine bevy of beautiful ladies in the chorus, who helped in no small way to make the evening's entertainment a thoroughly enjoyable one. The whole produc- tion, including the magnificent scenery, cos- tumes and exquisite music merited as it received whole-hearted applause and apprecia- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <head>v Drama For Next Week.</head>
        <p>v Drama For Next Week.</p>
        <p>Next week Mr. Oswald Craig's company, in "The Unmarried Mother," occupies the boards. This drama is one that ;.as created a sensa- tion in every town that it has visited and has focussed public opinion on what has been termed "the burning question of the day." The play is written by Mr. C. Vernon Proctor, who has made a . reputation in the playwriters' world. The cast includes many well-known actors, and as the scenery is of the best, the drama should be very popular amongst War- rington theatre-goers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <head>HIPPODROME AND PALACE.</head>
        <p>HIPPODROME AND PALACE.</p>
        <p>Trans- kliantie Burlesque At Friars'</p>
        <p>Green.</p>
        <p>The principal attraction at the popular variety house this week is the one entitled "Partners," a trans-Atlantic burlesque in four acts, produced by Messrs. Carr and Jackson: The applause and whole-hearted laughter during the time this famous company occupied the boards indicate that it is in for a long tour. The element of humour is very pronounced, and pleasing scenes and dances being a decided factor, it is no wonder that the entertainment is a very enjoyable one. Nat Carr, as Abraham Cohen, makes the show go With a fine swing, and is a Hebrew comedian of exceptional note, his facial expression in many diverting incidents being very clever. Touring with this company is Miss Lilian Leigh, a vocalist pleasing and refined, who renders her songs in irresistible style and manner. Other artistes of note in " Partners " are W. A. Haines, proprietor of the Diamond Palace Theatre; Lena Hicks, as the old maid and flirting wife; and the troupe of Oriental dancers, who all earn well-merited applause. The Record Quartet, described as the great South- African novelty banjo act is first-class. They have visited Warrington on vi previous occasion, and the return visit was of as fine an order as before. Keil\ and Rempton (Donald and Mabel), Scottish vocal duo, and Dorothy Leigh, in comedy songs, contribute items both pleasing and refreshing, all of which go towards the making of as fine a piocrramme we have as yet witnessed at this cosy vaudeville theatre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <head>Next Week's Good Things.</head>
        <p>Next Week's Good Things.</p>
        <p>A programme of really good "turns" 'will be at the Hippodrome next week, and the chief is that in which Terence Byron . and Company present a "humorous slice of Lancashire life," in the sketch entitled "William 'Enery's Wedding." Franco Piper should be popular with musical lovers, for he is said to have no equal as a banjo soloist. He has arranged and played pieces from the works of Wagner, Liszt, Chopin, Sullivan, and other musical celebrities. His latest novelty consists' of spinning fifteen banjos at 300 revolutions a minute and playing perfectly. • The Aforandinis are wonderful acro- bats and they will present a. sensational bend- ing bamboo pole and ladder act. The "Five Whiteleys " will be seen at their best in an original wire-walking acrobatic musical melange.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>THRILLING ,FILMS AT THE STAR</head>
        <p>THRILLING ,FILMS AT THE STAR</p>
        <p>KINEMA.</p>
        <p>"The Shooting of Dan McGrew," a magnifi- cent five part drama brimful of exciting inci- dents throughout, easily occupied premier position at the Star Kinema House durifig the earlier part of this week. A fine picture took the form of a further episode of the "Exploits of Elaine," the thrilling incidents of which made one's hair "stand on end." Several scenes from the seat of war were depicted in the " Pathe Gazette," and a 'first-rate pro- gramme was brought to a conclusion by the reeling of a " screamer " comedy. The chief picture for the week-end is entitled "The Builder of Bridges," which, for many asons, commands attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>" Star " Films Next Week.</head>
        <p>" Star " Films Next Week.</p>
        <p>The programme for next week at this popular picture house, will be up to the high standard for which it has gained a reputation. For the first three nights the film entitled "The Verdict of the Ileart" will occupy the pride of place in the programme, and for the last three nights "Jim the Penman" will occupy this position.:</p>
        <p>An Observant "Tommy." Extract of a letter from a soldier to his home: " All the, 'French boys and girls can Eay Souvenir.' It is the only English :word they know."—" Punch." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0315" n="art0315">
        <head>Overdid It A Bit.</head>
        <p>Overdid It A Bit.</p>
        <p>"Sir," began the bumptious applicant for a job, " I' have been told that you are looking for a clerle'. • • • •</p>
        <p>SO I ,am," answered the man at the desk.</p>
        <p>"I Suppose you want a person whorn neither smokes, drinks, nor swears, and never engages in games of chance? One whose character is the best? Ope who. is honest; industrious and thoroughly capable? One who will stop at nothing, consistent with his principles, to fur- ther the interests of his employer? In short, a brisk, intelligent, all-round man who knows business from A to Z, and will never he caught napping? If so, I am the individual you are looking for." "You will pardon; my diffidence, I hope," said the man at the desk. "If you are half as competent as you clàith to. he I feel myself unworthy of becoming your boss. ' Good morning."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0316" n="art0316">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0317" n="art0317">
        <p>N</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>N</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL UNION.</p>
        <p>A Successful Year Despite The War.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Sun- day School Union was held on Saturday in the Friars' Green School, Mr. A. Mounlield presid- ing in the absence of the President. The work of the past year was said to have been somewhat interrupted by the war, a large number of ,teachers and officials having joined the Army. The united scholars' service in Sep- tember was a success, being well attended. The lectures by Mr. Mounfield had been greatly appreciated, and a further course is being ar- ranged for the New Year. A useful result ,was obtained for the. Scripture examinations, there being 167 entries.. The visit of Miss Huntley to Warrington revived considerable interest in the problems of the Sunday School, and a further cotifer,nce of local Sunday School officials is to be held on December 18th.</p>
        <p>A satisfactory balance sheet was Presented by the treasurer (Mr. F. Morrall).</p>
        <p>Thanks were accorded the Rev. Wm. Reid for his services as president, and regret was ex- pressed that he was unable to continue. The following were elected officers: -President, Mr, A. Mounfield; vice-presidents, Revs. W. Reid, T. T. James, W. T. Derry, IL Ogden, C. P. Pitt, A. J. Chessum, Percy meson, ParrY" Jones and J. Howard, Alderman A. Bennett, Councillors J. Shaw and R. Henshall, Messrs. T. Dale, F. T. Light and P. Lawton; secretaxY, Mr. Fred Morrall; organising secretary, Miss Vanda M. Taylor; treasurer, Mr. A. Weave; examination secretary, Mr. H. L. Clayton; auditors, Messrs. Miingall and Thos. AThinson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-12-11_WidnesExaminer_art0318" n="art0318">
        <head>ALIN WHO BURIED HIS SOUL.</head>
        <p>THE ALIN WHO BURIED HIS SOUL.</p>
        <p>There is a fable—hut is it a fable ?—that tells of a man who in his yonth possessed a soul; a tender, true, aspiring soul which delighted in music, and in the. contemplation, of sublime thoughts.* But the man had a great ambition for wealth and power, and as much had to be accomplished before he could reach his goal, he became a very busy man indeed. As the 'years rolled by, he began to find his soul a sore in- convenience. It stopped him to gaze at the sun- set; it led him forth 'against his will to .inhale the sweet, pure freshness of the fields and woods after the summer rain. It led him into art galleries, it .delayed him on the .pavement be- cause in. a home near by a,beautiful voice was singing the air of an old memory-laden song. In hundreds of such ways the man's soul proved itself a nuisance and a hindrance. . SO it came. about that one day the man decided to free himself from his soul until he should achieve his ,fortune and satisfy his ambition. " Then,".said he, ." when I have leisure. to -in- dulge my soul and to enjoy, through it, tha beauties' of the world, I will take it to myself again." So .he selected an unfrequented spot in a wood, and there, in the soft, rich earth,- he buried his soul, covering it carefully .with eartlh and leaves and boughs. ' Then he returned to the city, where he be- came rich and powerful. Neither sunset, nor sweet-voiced birds, nor the freshness of the sum- mer rain, nor marvellous paintings, nor loftiest poems, Mir even the melody of an old tender song that might have carried recollections or the long ago, delayed him now, or turned him aside even for one fleeting moment. But pre- sently he grew satiated with wealth and power: he became discontented; the world was flat and: unprofitable; something was missing. Then he bethought himself of 'his soul, and journeyed to the little pound where he had buried it. lie saw no beauty now in the .sky or. fragrance in the wood; the singing of the birds._ was but a sound- in his ears, and the .thousand little thinks that once would have charmed and ,delighted his senses Were Passed unnoticed. The little mound was: as he had left it. Care- fully he plucked away-thp brush and' leave§ and earth and uncovered- his 'soul. :But alas! alas! there was .only. a grey.. shrivelled something that turned to ashes at his touch. "And what shall it profit a man if he gain the 'Whole world and lose his own soul ?."---7" Great Thoughts."</p>
