        <p>WHAT THE TRADESMEN ARE DOING WINTER AT THE FRONT. Rev. T. T. James On Happiness In Anxious Days. The Rev. T. T. James, writing to the Wycliffe Church Record from France, and After saying that his prayer was always for the continuance of the church activities with unimpaired energy; says:— "Out here we are always finding plenty to do. Everyone is thinking of the Christmas time which will soon be upon us, and very naturally the thought of home is more insistent than ever. We are puzzling our brains to discover ways in which something of the gladness and brightness of Christmas may be made possible for the lads here, and I trust the season may not be without its touches of that happiness and fellowship we get at home. We know you will all be think- ing of us in the homeland, and though there are so many passing through troubled and anxious days at home just now, every one here will be wishing for you a brave and cheery Christmas. "We are in the midst of the winter. Quite frequent falls of snow and heavy winds, with sometimes days of persistent rain, put things to a stiff test out here. The men stand it all with great cheerfulness. In the long winter evenings the Y.M.C.A. huts- are very widely used, and both the religious and societl activities are mani- fold. Where men stay any length or time one often becomes intimately acquainted with them, and,when they leave it is one of the happiest of experiences- to receive letters from them. "The New Year will soon be upon us, and I will let my last word be one of good wishes to you all. In home and church I trust you will be greatly encouraged. May God give you all good things."</p>
