        <p>respected • than Alderman Burton—(hear, hear) —and on the Health Committee he was equally well respected. The position of deputy-chairman very often was molt 'ornamental than useful; it was a position of -honour. Alderman Burton occupied that position for many years, and- he was universally •respected as a man of judg- ment and of tact. Alderman TINNIoN also paid a tribute to Alderman Burton, saying that his interest in the Health Committee was probably more than it was in any other committee of the Corpora- tion, and they regretted particularly his illness. A DISTINGUISHED WARRINGTONIAN. Moving the adoption of the minutes of the Museum Committee, Alderman BENNETT referred to the gift of the " Art Annual for 1915" from Messrs. Virtue and Co.. which was devoted to the art of Henry Woods, R.A. He (Alderman' Bennett) was glad to think that Mr. -Woods was born and bred in Warrington and had lived to attain such eminence in the high art that he had followed and had reflected so much glory on his native town. PRICE OF COKE. The Gas Committee recommended that the price of coke should be 18s. 4d. per ton. Councillor WILSON asked whether arrange- ments had been made to supply coke in small quantities to poor people in various parts of</p>
        <p>the town. That was done in other parts of the country, the coke being taken out in bags be- longing to the Corporation and sold in 2cwt. lots. He thought the Gas Committee might make a similar arrangement in Warrington, instead of letting the coke get into the hands of the wholesale dealers to sell at their own price. Councillor LOCKER (chairman of the commit- tee) explained the demand for coke at the present time was such that it was impossible for the committee to send it out in the way Councillor Wilson suggested. There was a shortage of labour, and people were only too willing, at the price fixed by the committee, to send to the yard for any spare coke they had. .Coke dealers were .supplied • at. a lower rate in order• that they __might go round to their customers and sell it at the price at which it was to be obtained at the Yard. At the present time the committee could not see their way to adopt the retail system suggested by Councillor Wilson, but they might recon- sider the question in normal times. The minutes were approved. THE MAYOR'S GOOD WISHES. Closing the meeting, the MAYOR expressed the hope his colleagues would have as much hap- piness at Christmas as it was possible in the circumstances. He hoped that the war would be at an end before Christmas Day next year.</p>
