        <p>ON HONOUR'S SCROLL. KILLED. . SERGEANT J. PINNOCK. "One Of The Straightest, Best-Hearted And Most Willing Of Sergeants." Official news has reached Mrs. Pinnock, 30, Philip-street, to the effect that her husband, Sergeant John Pinnock, of the 9th Cheshire Regiment, was killed .in 'action on November 16th. - Writing later, another Sergeant of the Che- shire Regiment said "Please prepare yourself for lamentable news, which I know will come as a great shock to you and your family. It is my, unpleasant duty to acquaint you with the -news of the death of your husband. It happened on Nov- ember 16th, he being killed by a German sniper. Death was instant, the bullet having penetrated 'his temple. I cannot prove by writ- ing the terrible shock which his death caused to the whole company. He wag one of the straightest, best-hearted and most willing of sergeants the company were gifted with, and it is our loss as well as yours. The whole company join with ine• in- sending our deepest sympathy." Recommended For The D.C.M. • Sergeant Pinnock, who was 36 years of age, formerly belonged to West Bromwich. He en- listed in October of last year, having been pre- viously employed at Dail= Forge. He was made Sergeant whilst at the Front and was recommended for the D.C.M., but he was killed before hearing anything further of the matter.</p>
