        <p>ceiving Knight, and Hapton pounced on the ball and opened the visitors' account. They were not long before they scored two more goals, through Holt, their centre-forward. Half-time: 'Chester, 3 goals; Emmanuel, 2 goals. Chester opened the second half in good style. :Their forwards made a rush on the Institute goal, and while Knight was kicking clear, S. Hughes could not get out of the way in time, and the ball. cannoned off him into the open val. The soldiers were now two goals to the good, which. roused the home players. Stubbs and Eaton passed the ball well, the former .finishing, with an unsuccessful shot. For the 'visitors their centre-half was opening out the game well, and considering the state of the ground,. the game was being fought at a fast pace.. The visiting forwards were persistent raiders, ' but Knight on each occasion saved. Hardy put the ball well up the field, where Stubbs held fast to it and, after beating several opponents., rushed the ball past the goalkeeper. The Institute now played with determination. the R.A.JSLC. backs having an anxious time. jilotlb teams attacked in their turn, and, con- sidering the bad weather and the slippery con- dition of the ground, both sides were playing well:. They were evenly matched, and strenuous lefforts- were made by both teams; but without [success, Emmanuel having to be content with their first hame defeat of the season by four goals to three. The soldiers were afterwards entertained to tea by, the members of the Institute Club. Songs were sung by a couple of the soldiers and the brothers S. and T. Hughes. A pleasant time was spent with various selection of songs. The visiting captain was the pianist. MATCHES FOR TO-DAY. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT JUNIOR LEAGUE. Fairfield United Reserves v. Bewsey Latchford C.C. v. Mersey Juniors. Howley United v. Padgate C.L.B. Stockton Heath Scouts v. Orford Juniors. Warrington Association v. Hollins Green. At Hood-lane. , Elmwood Congregationalists v. Cairo-street. At King and Queen ground. TEAMS FOR TO-DAY. BEWSEY v. FAIRFIELD RESERVES. At Orford-road ground. Bewsey : C. Perks; T. Blease and J. Stephens; B. Dickenson, F. Roylance and H.'Bradbury; E. Edwards, N. Pearson, F. Payton, J. Small- man and A. H. Hassan. Reserves.—J. Kirk and A. Ellis. EMMANUEL INSTITUTE v. WARRINGTON CENT HA L. At Victoria Park; 2.30. Institute: T. Knight; E. Crozier and S. Hughes (captain); F. Parr, E. Hardy and W. Ayres; F. Hughes, A. Oakes, T. llughes, H. Stubbs and Wm. 'Eaton. ON THE CORNISH RIVIERA. You are perhaps at this time wondering where you can go to escape the damp and cold which prevail in most parts of the kingdom at this season of the year. If so you can do no better than to visit the Cornish Riviera. Enjoying the beneficent warmth of the Golf Stream, Cornwall possesses a wonderfully mild and equable climate. Added to this, the mag- nificent scenery and unrivalled opportunities for healthful recreation fully justify its claim to be the English Riviera—the ideal venue for all who desire a rest or change of air. Tell-tale Footprints A man never knows when he is covering up his tracka; he never knows how long they will stay covered or who will get on his trail. Up in Canada, while digging for the". new Toronto water works, the diggers found in the blue clay 70 feet below the water line the footprints of a gentleman who had tramped around that way some years ago. The geologists disagree as to the exact time, some holding that the freshness of the footprints indicates that it was only 50,000 years ago, while others believe that the toenail prints indicate that it has been about 100,000 years since the gentleman passed that way. Not that this slight discrepancy matters to the gentleman or to us; for nobody had to sit on a fence there and wait for him 50,000 years, even if he did happen to be late.</p>
